    records: WITHDRAW `sgilinearnegidx` as a FALSE finding -- `tile_nr` is unsigned, and the declaration was three lines above the code I quoted
    
    NO PRODUCT CODE.  A withdrawal, one preserved seat answer, one new brief, one row split out.
    
    *** THE FINDING I FILED HIGH AND ESCALATED YESTERDAY IS WRONG. ***
    
        :207   unsigned int tile_nr = tile_nr_y * 16 + tile_nr_x;     <- UNSIGNED
        ...
        case 4: tile_nr = x >> 12;             <- converted to unsigned ON ASSIGNMENT
                if (tile_nr >= 32) return;     <- 4294967295 >= 32 is TRUE.  IT RETURNS.
    
    x = -1 gives 4294967295; x = -8192 gives 4294967294.  Both are >= 32, both rejected.  The guard I
    called "bounds ABOVE only" is a COMPLETE bound, because the conversion folds every negative value
    into the rejected range.  No out-of-bounds read, no guest-chosen tile pointer, no write primitive.
    The chain is broken at its FIRST link, so every later link is moot.
    
    *** COMMIT 15633bb's MESSAGE CARRIES THIS CLAIM AS ITS HEADLINE AND CANNOT BE EDITED. ***  The
    ledger row, OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md and _scratchpad/CHECKPOINT.md now all name that commit as wrong --
    the same treatment de96f11's over-broad RMW claim already has.  Both records keep the original text
    verbatim and correct it underneath, because this project corrects in place and quotes what it
    refutes.
    
    HOW IT HAPPENED, recorded because the process failure is the only thing of value left in the row:
    
      * I wrote "the chain, each step read from the source" and "every link is now verified by reading".
        I did verify the link I DOUBTED -- whether src_x is masked in the linear path -- and never
        checked the TYPE of the index variable, which is the most basic property of an index and the one
        that decides the whole question.
      * I reached for offsetof precisely to avoid trusting hand arithmetic about the struct.  THAT
        RIGOUR WAS REAL AND IT WAS AIMED AT THE WRONG LINK.  Care on one step does not transfer to a
        step you never thought to question.
      * THE DECLARATION WAS ON MY OWN SCREEN.  I printed lines 184-215 of that function earlier in the
        same session while checking the mode decode; :207 is inside that range.  I read past it because
        I was looking for the mode fields.
      * The offsetof measurement itself is TRUE and is kept at regress/artifacts/sgilinearnegidx/
        offcheck.c.  It is a correct measurement of a path nothing can reach, so it supports no finding.
    
    *** AND THE C5 REFUSAL COST ME THE REFUTATION FOR HOURS, WHICH IS THE SHARPEST ARGUMENT YET ABOUT
    THAT CLAUSE. ***  I asked the seat to attack this chain link by link; that brief was REFUSED for
    being chain-shaped.  The same seat, asked the same subject WITHOUT the chain -- "which array indexes
    are bounded on one side only?" -- put the refutation in its VERDICT LINE.  The refusal did not stop
    a bad question; it DELAYED A CORRECT ANSWER, and in the interval I escalated a false finding, wrote
    it into a commit, and reported it as the session's most severe result.
    
    A NOTE ON THE PACKET-FED SEAT THAT RAISED IT, because the doctrine comes out well.  glm noticed a
    real asymmetry between two guards in code it had been handed.  What it could not do -- exactly as
    the doctrine says -- is check a DECLARATION, because a type is a repo fact and glm is packet-fed.
    The doctrine's warning is about crediting such a seat with repo fact, and that is precisely the
    error I then made on its behalf.
    
    2. `sgirespanchk` (LOW) -- SPLIT OUT, then DOWNGRADED WITHIN THE HOUR BY ITS OWN SOURCE.
    
    The same verdict line continued past the refutation: "There are missing checks on complete write
    SPANS in TLB_A/B/C and LINEAR_A/B, whose safety depends on caller alignment."  A span check is a
    different question from an index bound -- the bound says where an access STARTS, the span says where
    it FINISHES -- so the refutation does not touch it.  Filed as its own row rather than folded in,
    because a withdrawn entry is the worst possible place to hide a live finding.
    
    I filed it from the VERDICT LINE and had not read the body.  The body enumerates the overruns (a TLB
    loop from 255 touches 256, 257, 258; a LINEAR pair from 31 touches 32) and then discharges the very
    question I had just written down as owed:
    
        "These are HANDLER ROBUSTNESS GAPS, not established guest out-of-bounds writes ... Enumerating
         aligned addresses gives TLB starting indexes 0,4,...,252 and LINEAR 0,2,...,30; their complete
         spans fit.  I VERIFIED THE ALIGNMENT HANDLING IN THE ORDINARY ACCESS CODE AND UNALIGNED-ACCESS
         HELPER, RATHER THAN ACCEPTING THE DEVICE COMMENTS."
    
    -- naming cpu_mips_instr_loadstore.c and cpu_mips_instr_unaligned.c.  That is exactly the
    checked-versus-assumed distinction the row was filed to demand.  MED -> LOW.  What remains open is
    one word, "checked": a DMA engine, a device-to-device access or the debugger's `put` reach a handler
    without a CPU load, and those were not enumerated.  SPAN_brief.md is out at the seat asking that,
    and it was written and fired BEFORE this body was read, so its answer will be independent.
    
    FIFTH UNDER-HARVEST OF THE SESSION, AND THE FIRST TO COST A WHOLE INVOCATION.  The previous four
    took a seat's finding and dropped its refutation; this one dropped the seat's own DISCHARGE of an
    obligation I then spent a codex run re-asking.  A verdict line is a summary written against the
    brief's framing and is the least likely part of an answer to carry a qualification -- this project
    already says that about corrections, and it is equally true of discharges.
    
    3. FIVE SIDE-CHECKS HARVESTED FROM THE SAME BODY, NONE OF THEM ASKED FOR, ALL NEGATIVE:
    de_reg[1024] at [regnr] and [regnr+1] reaches at most 512; both static rop_used[256] arrays are
    masked & 255, which supplies BOTH bounds; zerobuf[4096] is length-capped then page-shortened, never
    enlarged; buf[8] takes enumerated lengths 1/2/4/8, all of which fit -- with the seat volunteering
    unprompted that its INITIALISATION is a separate defect, which is sgirebufuninit, already filed.
    
    AND ONE FINDING THAT IS NOT A SPAN QUESTION: "the coordinate rectangle check does not by itself
    establish the TLB capacity bound."  At depth field 3 (bufdepth 8, tile width 64) the pre-guard
    tile_nr reaches 271, not 255 -- the x,y < 2048 rectangle admits indexes past the end of a [256]
    array, and it is the LATER tile_nr >= 256 guard that rejects them.  Both guards are present so
    nothing is broken.  What is worth recording is that THEY ARE NOT REDUNDANT: an edit deleting the
    second as "already covered by the rectangle check" would open a sixteen-element overrun.  A
    clause-(d)-shaped hazard in code nobody is editing -- noted, not filed.
    
    4. HELD OUT DELIBERATELY: regress/artifacts/sgirergbroundtrip/ (the RGB round-trip witness, which
    ran 6/6 on the committed build and reproduced its predicted value exactly -- it ships with its own
    round), regress/sgi_de_primop_probe.py and regress/artifacts/gdromdefault_461/ (standing
    exclusions).  Staged by explicit path, never a directory add.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
