commit ae68e297e781c4decbad28f0c0cca7ae13a0a27c
Author:     indigoidk <indigoidk@users.noreply.github.com>
AuthorDate: Sun Aug 30 10:36:59 2026 -0500
Commit:     indigoidk <indigoidk@users.noreply.github.com>
CommitDate: Sun Aug 30 10:36:59 2026 -0500

    wdcaddtoinbufbound: a rung-3 witness, built and green -- and it refutes two premises of the brief that asked for it
    
    *** THE WITNESS EXISTS AND WORKS: 20 of 20 predictions across both legs, every control
    green.  On the committed build the guest is handed ITS OWN INJECTED BYTES back as disk
    data. ***  Unmodified `evbmips -e malta`, wdc via the committed bus_pci/piix4_ide path,
    free-running, 1.52 s.  Controls: RAM liveness; a DEVICE SIGNATURE (two reads of one
    address giving push order, which a memory cell cannot); encodings 46/46 words AND
    mnemonics against the emulator's own disassembler; disk content at known offsets.
    The row's witness field moves from `none` to `probe:`.
    
    TWO OF MY PREMISES ARE MEASURED FALSE, AND ONE CHANGES THE ROUND.
    
    (1) The sketch AS BRIEFED could not have exhibited the defect at all.  I wrote "32
    blocks on a CD-ROM" assuming 2048-byte blocks; in this tree CD-ROM logical_block_size is
    512 -- the 2048 branch sits inside `#if 0` -- so 32 blocks is 16384 bytes, UNDER the
    32768 chunk clamp.  No second chunk, no re-arm, nothing to measure.  It needs 128.  A
    seat that had merely followed the brief would have produced a clean run and reported the
    defect absent.
    
    (2) "ANY fix that keeps foreign bytes out turns the shipped row red" is FALSE, measured,
    and it independently confirms what the adjudication derived by reading the fixture: the
    write-arm fix leaves the detector at 128 rows, 0 failures; only the re-arm fix reds it,
    at 1.  THE ROUND IS NOT UNDER THE ORACLE-CORRECTION RULE UNLESS IT PICKS THE RE-ARM
    OPTION -- and the row is a rare DISCRIMINATOR between the two, the opposite of the
    "green under every candidate fix, settles nothing" corollary.
    
    *** AND THE FLOOR FINDING, WHICH GOVERNS THE ROUND: THE SHIPPED 128-ROW DETECTOR DOES
    NOT OBSERVE THIS DEFECT AT ALL. ***  `base` scores 0 failures WITH THE DEFECT LIVE, so
    vacuity-floor clause (a) is not satisfiable from existing rows.  If the round ships the
    write-arm guard, THE WITNESS MUST SHIP AS THE DETECTOR or it ships below the floor.  Two
    one-expression clause-(d) mutants escape the detector entirely and are caught only by
    the witness.  The zero is a MEANINGFUL zero and that was proved, not asserted: a
    guard-dropping mutant moves 14 rows on the same arm.
    
    === A LIVE HAZARD ON THIS MACHINE, CLOSED ===
    
    `/tmp/gxsec-build` -- the WSL arc tree -- held a PRE-#494 dev_wdc.c while build/,
    build-asan/ and est/ all carried the fix.  A scratch clone taken from it would have
    measured #494-less code in the very file under review AND cmp-ed clean against its own
    donor, so the two-part clearance would have passed on it.  Propagated and rebuilt, both
    clearance parts run.
    
    Filed as `novpadfourth`: the carrier's no-VPATH rule names FOUR trees and precommit
    section B enforces THREE, because the fourth lives in the WSL filesystem and the
    Windows-side checker cannot walk it.  The remedy is probably not a checker -- section B
    would have to cross the WSL boundary, unlike every other check it does -- but an
    obligation in the round-boundary step beside the existing clearance.  A script exists
    and ran clean; what is missing is the duty to run it.
    
    Also recorded: nine seats on the pass-1 panel citing their dir, and a second live L4 the
    seat found in product code -- #477's objection cites the `default:` len-3/8 arm as its
    example, and #479 retired that example with `!badlen_this` in a comment fifteen lines
    below it in the same file.  The warrant was already dead of a second cause and its text
    was never updated.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

