commit 4d0981dcb6b2272d21d9f40d6f6e50582f689ccb
Author:     indigoidk <indigoidk@users.noreply.github.com>
AuthorDate: Tue Aug 18 10:31:48 2026 -0500
Commit:     indigoidk <indigoidk@users.noreply.github.com>
CommitDate: Tue Aug 18 10:31:48 2026 -0500

    #437: SYNCHRONIZE CACHE told the guest its data was safe and synced the wrong file
    
    MEASURED on the unmodified luna88k rig: OpenBSD issues SYNCHRONIZE CACHE once per boot,
    immediately after "/dev/sd0a: MARKING FILE SYSTEM CLEAN" -- the moment it has just repaired
    a filesystem. The handler was fsync(fileno(d->f)) and nothing else, wrong in two ways its
    own TODO had named for years:
    
      (a) fsync flushes the KERNEL's buffers; writes go through stdio and the userspace buffer
          was never flushed. overlay_set_block_in_use() at :1207-1211 already does it correctly
          and says why.
      (b) with an overlay, fwrite_helper writes ONLY to overlays[nr].f_data, so d->f held none
          of the guest's data and the overlay and bitmap were never synced at all. Measured
          before the fix: one fsync, on the base fd, and no fflush anywhere. (b) is the larger
          half -- on real power loss it costs everything the guest wrote.
    
    diskimage_sync() carries out the TODO; the handler now REPORTS the result (CHECK CONDITION /
    MEDIUM ERROR instead of GOOD), the same defect #416/#417 corrected for writes. The owner was
    asked before shipping the guest-visible half and chose to report the failure.
    
    Three design points from measurement, not preference: order is load-bearing (publishing a
    bitmap bit while the block's bytes are not durable makes a later read take the overlay's HOLE
    -- built by hand and driven through the shipping read path, the guest reads zeros where the
    base still held data, reported as a successful read); d->f is synced only when there are no
    overlays, and a non-writable flat disk returns early, because fflush on an "r" handle is UB
    per C99 7.21.5.2p2 and fsync on O_RDONLY is unspecified -- now that the result is CHECKED, a
    platform returning nonzero would turn every CD-ROM's sync into a spurious MEDIUM ERROR; and
    no do_fsync gate, since -f is about ordinary writes while this is the guest asking.
    
    *** THE DETECTOR TOOK THREE ATTEMPTS AND THE FIRST TWO WERE VACUOUS. ***
    
     1. The first pre-fix control reported a clean pass WHILE TESTING THE FIX TWICE: a quoted
        relative #include "../src/disk/diskimage.c" resolves against the INCLUDING FILE's
        directory, so no -I could substitute the pre-fix source. The detector must live INSIDE
        the tree under test.
     2. Moved there it FAILED TO COMPILE, because a row called diskimage_sync() directly -- a
        SETUP result, never a detection. Every row now drives diskimage_scsicommand() and the
        file names diskimage_sync() nowhere, which is what makes it a differential at all.
     3. The third version counted fflush/fsync CALLS, and a review seat killed it with FOUR
        mutants that all passed green, because a count cannot see WHICH FILE: rename f_bitmap to
        f_data (bitmap never synced), invert the order, delete the ordering continue, sync d->f
        instead of the bitmap. Each keeps the totals at 2 and 2. The row names even said "data
        and bitmap" while the bitmap went untouched, and the gate grepped those names as
        evidence. AND THE FIXTURE HAD NEVER WRITTEN A BYTE -- SCSICMD_WRITE is 0x0a (WRITE(6))
        driven with a 10-byte CDB, and data_out_offset was never set, so the handler refused the
        transfer. With nothing written there was no durability property to check, only counts.
    
    Shipped rows assert an ordered FILE TRACE plus two rows proving the guest's sector arrived.
    Verified every direction: 7 rows / 0 failed with the fix, 7 / 5 against pre-fix sources from
    git, and the two wrong-file mutants that beat the count-only version are now killed.
    SELFMUTANT_OK on a mutant that drops the overlay-data fflush while KEEPING the fsync, chosen
    because it leaves the counts intact so only the trace can see it. Gate 2: PASS (231 checks).
    
    FOUR COMMENTS CORRECTED, MEASURED FALSE, two written in this round's own first pass: the gate
    claimed dropping --wrap would "still pass against nothing" (it fails closed, 4 rows red); the
    selfmutant comment claimed the -fno-optimize-sibling-calls hazard shape, when omitting the
    flags yields SELFMUTANT_SETUP, which is never scored as a detection.
    
    Filed, not fixed (the stopping rule admits only a measured false pass or a wrong record):
    m437ord (the ordering rule has no gate row -- needs fault injection), m437multi (spurious
    CHECK CONDITION on a multi-overlay disk), m437rtmp (under R: both overlay files are unlinked
    at open, so the durability claim does not mean what it sounds like), idesync (IDE FLUSH CACHE
    has no durability path at all).
    
    Panel: pass 1 seven seats, pass 2 six. codex quota-walled four panels running; fable5 held by
    owner directive. Both recorded by name as HELD -- the round cannot close, which is the rule
    working. One seat disagreement settled by CHECKING rather than voting: glm reported a NULL
    deref in fatal(d->id); diskimage_scsicommand has a `d == NULL` guard before the switch.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
    
    Precommit caught four things before this landed, which is why it exists: grok's pass-2
    answer was unrecorded (and it had independently reached the detector's headline defect from
    reading alone, plus two comment defects nobody else found -- diskimage_scsicmd.cc for .c, and
    that my patch had inserted diskimage_sync() BETWEEN diskimage_dump_info()'s doc comment and
    its body, orphaning it; my anchor was the signature, and a signature is not the top of a
    function when a doc block precedes it); the build tree was stale on all three sources; and
    the detector had no identity guard. All fixed.

