commit abf665c73276962b8da1c532c13181a0c6965d64
Author:     indigoidk <indigoidk@users.noreply.github.com>
AuthorDate: Tue Aug 25 01:11:25 2026 -0500
Commit:     indigoidk <indigoidk@users.noreply.github.com>
CommitDate: Tue Aug 25 01:11:25 2026 -0500

    probetimebased: alive is True for a process already SIGKILLed -- measured, with a shipped instance
    
    A measuring seat built a rig from this probe's own session()/rd() and measured four branches
    with controls in both directions.  `rd()`'s loop is `while time.time() < end:`, so a death
    that lands AFTER the last budget check is never read.  A process SIGKILLed just before its
    session's `quit` scores alive == True.
    
    *** SO `alive` DOES NOT MEAN "hung but not exited".  IT DOES NOT EVEN MEAN "not exited". ***
    It means: no EOF and no OSError were observed inside the sampled windows.  A red alive==died
    also cannot distinguish a clean exit from a pty error.
    
    The controls matter: untouched -> green; SIGKILL at t+2 -> RED, so the rig can fail; guest
    `bra .` with NO signals at all -> GREEN.  That last is stronger than the SIGSTOP case it was
    asked for -- a genuine wedge from real guest code through real dispatch, scored green.
    
    R1: the probe's contract said "alive is False ONLY on EOF" and "their claim is that the host
    does not END".  Both measurably wrong, corrected in place.  On Linux a pty-master read after
    the child dies gives EIO, so the OSError arm is the USUAL death path -- "only on EOF"
    mislabels the common case -- and the defect is on neither False path but on the third,
    `return True` when the budget expires having read nothing.
    
    R2: THE SHIPPED M11 ENTRY IS WRONG AND MY CAPTION'S REASONING WITH IT.  table2 records
    `nodata stale died`; table3 records `survived`; same session, same command list.  The seat
    re-synced a tree byte-identical to SEC, rebuilt, and ran that session FIVE TIMES: 5/5
    deterministic SIGSEGV, preceded by "Transfering 408 bytes to 0x00000000 (guest asked for 0)".
    So table3 records `survived` for a session that did not reach `print r2` -- which is also why
    `stale idle` reads `absent` there.  The probe's contract listed TWO causes for that absent
    firing and omitted the one a rebuild reproduces.
    
    My caption said "kill stable, mode unstable".  Corrected: the mode did not change, the
    predicate missed the death.  M11 is a STRONGER mutant than recorded, not a flakier one, and a
    re-score is owed.  Honest limit kept: table2's tree is not recorded in the artifacts, so it
    cannot be asserted both runs used identical binaries -- non-determinism is DISPLACED as the
    leading explanation, not refuted.  The superseded caption is kept beside the correction,
    because the reasoning is the part that was wrong.
    
    Bounding it, because the gap is narrower than "the detector is hang-blind": 45 of 58 rows
    carry a positive oracle a silent session breaks.  It is hang-MISATTRIBUTING, plus two rows
    (accept8, dma accept) that are genuinely blind and killed by none of the 16 mutants.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

