commit 029a1372dba28d40d62db859d7230457a16105e8
Author: indigoidk <indigoidk@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Sun Aug 23 22:16:26 2026 -0500

    gdromdmawidth: ASSESSED, NOT CHANGED -- a unanimous panel overturned from source
    
    Documentation only.  No behaviour changes; the GD-ROM DMA shadow store stays
    width-blind, deliberately.
    
    --- THE PANEL WAS UNANIMOUS AND WRONG ---
    
    Seven of seven CLI/cloud seats converged on Option B: decline narrow writes to
    the implemented DMA registers, using #461's latched-diagnostic idiom.  Two of
    them found in-tree precedent for it, independently, and one of those precedents
    is in this very file.
    
    The flagship seat then fetched mainline Linux `drivers/cdrom/gdrom.c` and read
    it line by line:
    
        :622   __raw_writeb(1, GDROM_DMA_STATUS_REG)   the DMA TRIGGER is a byte
                                                       write to this window's +0x18
        :618   __raw_readb(GDROM_DMA_STATUS_REG)       byte-READ polling for
                                                       completion
        :585-589                                       the four VALUE registers are
                                                       always __raw_writel
    
    So a blanket decline WOULD BREAK A BOOTING LINUX.  The width-blind code
    delivers exactly the semantics the one real narrow user needs, and the defect's
    actual content -- clobbering a value register's upper bits -- sits in cells no
    known guest reaches.  It also checked NetBSD (never touches this window) and
    promemul/dreamcast.c (zero references), and was explicit that KallistiOS widths
    are BELIEVED only.
    
    --- AND OPTION A IS REFUTED ON A DIFFERENT AXIS THAN THE BRIEF EXPECTED ---
    
    The brief called endianness the real technical risk.  It is not: cpu_sh.c:96
    sets EMUL_LITTLE_ENDIAN UNCONDITIONALLY, so there is no big-endian SH in this
    tree and the merge is always the low lane.  The brief's "SH-4 can run either
    endianness" is true of silicon and false of this tree -- verified here.
    
    The real objection is an ABSENT ORACLE.  0x005f74xx is Sega's Holly ASIC,
    outside the SH7750 manual, and this project holds no documentation for it.  The
    filing's own words -- "real hardware leaves the upper 24 bits" -- were an
    UNSOURCED CLAIM, the #357/#362 shape this tree has already been burned by.  A
    read-modify-write would ship an invented behaviour and then pin the invention
    with a detector row.
    
    --- PRECEDENT TRANSFERS BY ITS WARRANT, NOT ITS SHAPE ---
    
    The BSC16 idiom (dev_sh4.c:1620-1680) is the direct shape-match for "narrow
    write to an implemented wide register", and it declines.  But its warrant is
    "a real OpenBSD/landisk boot makes ZERO non-16-bit BSC accesses (measured), so
    the latch costs a correct guest nothing" -- and that warrant fails here, where
    a real Linux boot makes byte accesses on every transfer.  Worth keeping: a
    precedent's shape is not its licence.
    
    --- WHAT SHIPS ---
    
    A comment at the tail store recording: that the store is width-blind; the
    MEASURED consequence (seed 0x11223344, byte-write 0xff, read back -> 0xff, i.e.
    0x000000ff, with the liveness control returning 0x12345678 in the same run);
    that #461's alignment rule neither causes nor excuses it, because address
    decoding and width validation are independent axes; the Linux byte-contract as
    a standing constraint; and the decided design if a fix is ever warranted --
    decline at the four VALUE registers only, keeping +0x18 byte-capable.
    
    The filing's refuted presumption is corrected in place rather than silently
    replaced, and the discriminating row is recorded WITH the warning that it must
    not ship as a green row pinning 0x000000ff, which would enshrine this model's
    own behaviour as an oracle.
    
    The item stays OPEN and gated behind sh4gdromexit by PROBE-BUILDABILITY, not
    ranking: the row that would pin the real contract is unbuildable until that
    round removes the exit(1) kills at +0x18.
    
    Build rc=0, zero warnings; four trees cmp-identical.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

commit 84f6682d532c0b13981d548bc701f5bba55e5cc6
Author: indigoidk <indigoidk@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 27 22:48:00 2026 -0500

    gdromwarnbits: the PRE-SET trigger fired -- six masks become an enum of bit
    positions, `warned` widens to uint32_t, and the row does NOT close
    
    The trigger was written into `dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c` as "at the SEVENTH bit".
    `gdromexitsix` Round 1 adds THREE latches to the six already in use, so the NINTH
    would be `1u << 8` -- truncating to zero in a uint8_t, never setting, and letting
    the diagnostic it guards FLOOD.  No compile error, no runtime complaint.
    
    *** TWO SEATS REACHED IT INDEPENDENTLY on that round's pass 1 *** -- the
    adjudicator named it as an obligation the brief had missed entirely, agy called it
    a critical implementation blocker.  Neither was told about the trigger; both
    derived the budget from the code.
    
    Conversion: six `#define`d masks -> an enum of bit POSITIONS ending in
    GDROM_W_NBITS, each mask `(1u << ..._B)`, `warned` widened to uint32_t.  NBITS is
    correct BY CONSTRUCTION rather than by maintenance, and a duplicate mask becomes
    impossible to write -- the file's other stated hazard.
    
    BEHAVIOUR-PRESERVATION MEASURED, NOT ASSERTED: a compiled check confirms all six
    masks BIT-IDENTICAL to the hand-assigned originals, NBITS == 6, 26 bits free.
    Preserved at regress/artifacts/gdromwarnbits/mask_equivalence_check.{c,out}.
    Object compiles; propagated identical to est/, build/ and build-asan/.
    
    ## The row does not close, because the gate refused it four times and was right
    
    I closed it on a TWO-SEAT assess stage with a placeholder commit hash.
    `precommit_check.sh` rejected that in three separate checks -- not a hash, no
    regress review queued, a stage moved on without the full panel -- then refused
    again after I reopened the row, because a short stage needs its HELD MARKER and
    not merely a hold field, and once more because the held-stage summary was stale.
    Holding a stage and queueing it are one action.
    
    The code ships because Round 1 cannot proceed without it; the ROW stays `in work`
    with its assess stage held.  And the two seats it did have answered
    `gdromexitsix`'s panel, not this row's, so counting them here was a stretch before
    the count was even short.
    
    Queued as a rule question: **does discharging a pre-set trigger need a full
    panel?**  The trigger named the enum, the count suffix and the mask form -- every
    choice was fixed when it was written, and this round only detected the condition
    and executed it.  My lean is "a panel only if the discharge makes a choice the
    trigger did not already fix", and this one made none; that test is self-serving
    and a seat should check it rather than accept it.  Whatever is ruled reads back
    onto `dev_sh4.c`, whose equivalent conversion fired the same way.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

commit 84765ec776309ed797d5d711351b5fdaa89870bb
Author: indigoidk <indigoidk@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Fri Aug 28 11:31:24 2026 -0500

    gdromwarnbits: the bound is now a COMPILE ERROR, and this file already had four
    
    The nine-seat assess stage this row was held for.  ALL NINE ANSWERED (7 scriptable
    -- kimi CLI 403-walled and COVERED by kimi-k3:cloud -- plus both Agent seats).
    The panel doubles as 84f6682's pass-2 receipt, because its brief inlined that
    commit's whole diff; recorded as both rather than counted twice.
    
    ## THE FINDING, and one seat of nine had it
    
    *** dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c:46 INCLUDES thirdparty/dreamcast_sysasicvar.h, AND EVERY
    SYSASIC_TRIGGER_EVENT() CALL EXPANDS SYSASIC_EVENT_RANGE_OK -- A C99 COMPILE-TIME
    BOUND ASSERT.  FOUR of them compile on every build, two hundred lines below a
    comment in this file saying the technique is unavailable to this tree. ***
    
    Idiom census, four product sites: cpu_mips.c x2 (#388), memory_rw.c (#430),
    sysasicvar.h (#468/#469).  The build passes no `-std=` at all and the compiler
    reports __STDC_VERSION__ 202311L, so "this tree is C99" is a PORTABILITY POLICY,
    not a fact about what compiles -- and #468 refuted that exact rationale by
    measurement on 2026-08-26, ONE DAY BEFORE this file shipped a comment citing it.
    
    ## What ships
    
    `gdrom_w_fits_its_carriers`, a negative-size typedef in #388's shape, with TWO
    clauses because there are TWO carriers:
    
        1.  GDROM_W_NBITS <= 8 * sizeof(...->warned)   -- the field must HOLD the bit
        2.  GDROM_W_NBITS <= 8 * sizeof(1u)            -- `1u` must PRODUCE it
    
    Clause 2 is not decoration: a uint64_t field satisfies clause 1 at 40 positions
    while `1u << 40` is undefined.  The measuring seat CONCEDED this as a real hole in
    its own one-clause proposal.
    
    *** THE OBVIOUS FORM WAS MEASURED VACUOUS AND IS NOT WHAT SHIPPED. ***  The brief
    proposed `GDROM_W_NBITS <= 32`; a 16-variant kill table shows it COMPILES CLEAN
    over a uint8_t field carrying a live `1u << 8` truncation -- floor clauses (b) and
    (d).  It is written from the DEFECT instead of the DOMAIN, which sysasicvar.h had
    already predicted in those exact words.
    
    Five-way control run, TWO-SIDED on purpose so the guard is demonstrably not a
    check that cannot fail (regress/artifacts/gdromwarnbits/bound_assert_controls*):
    
        shipped, 6 bits / uint32_t      CLEAN     <- positive control
        9 bits  / uint32_t              CLEAN
        9 bits  / uint8_t               ERROR     <- the one-token revert
        33 bits / uint32_t              ERROR
        40 bits / uint64_t              ERROR     <- clause 2 alone
    
    The positive control is what earned its keep: an earlier version of this harness
    compiled nothing at all (a missing config.h path) and its `try` only printed
    errors matching the pattern it EXPECTED, so three "passes" were an artefact.  A
    check that cannot fail, in the apparatus built to prove one could.
    
    ## It is not an added guard -- it is NBITS's first consumer
    
    GDROM_W_NBITS had ZERO references anywhere.  It sized nothing, bounded nothing,
    and DELETING it compiled clean -- so "correct BY CONSTRUCTION" rested on a
    decorative symbol.  Under the addition licence that settles it: this is L4 repair,
    not accretion.  And the dev_sh4.c precedent transfers on SHAPE but NOT on that
    property -- SH4_VAL_NCLASS genuinely sizes an array and bounds a runtime check.
    
    ## FOUR L4s corrected, three raised by one seat alone
    
      * the trigger comment SURVIVED ITS OWN DISCHARGE, verbatim and in the PRESENT
        TENSE -- still saying "SIX OF EIGHT BITS ARE USED" (it is six of 32) and
        "truncates to 0 in a uint8_t" (the field is uint32_t), sixty lines below a
        note saying the trigger had fired.  A discharged trigger must be RETIRED in
        the edit that discharges it: it is written to be read by a future round, so
        leaving it live-sounding aims the next reader at work already done.
      * the GDROM_UNIMPL_FIRST C99 rationale, refuted inside this file's own TU.
      * "THE PRE-SET TRIGGER ABOVE HAS FIRED" -- it is BELOW.
      * "a duplicate is impossible to write" -- too strong; C permits an explicit
        duplicate enumerator and a raw `|= 0x40` at a new site still collides.
    
    ## The hold question: RULED YES, and the split is reported not tallied
    
    opus5/fable5/codex/grok/agy for a full panel; deepseek/minimax/kimi for targeted
    scrutiny; glm conditional.  The three "targeted" seats are ALL PACKET-FED and all
    say they cannot settle the population premises from the packet -- they differ in
    SCOPE, not substance, and every one also concludes the row must not close on the
    evidence as given.
    
    Decisive argument, institutional rather than technical: "THE RULE IS ALREADY A
    GATE, NOT A TASTE" -- check_stage_panels.py encodes the owner's full-panel
    directive, and a seat cannot mint an exemption the owner declined to write.  Clean
    rule: THE UNIT OF PANEL COVERAGE IS THE COMMIT, NOT THE DECISION -- which resolves
    the dev_sh4.c readback WITHOUT retro-condemning it, since that conversion shipped
    inside panelled round #451.  And my own self-serving test, applied honestly,
    returns YES anyway: the trigger said "widen" and never chose uint32_t.
    
    ## The verification artifact was the real weakness
    
    mask_equivalence_check.c DUPLICATES the definitions instead of including them, so
    product drift leaves it GREEN and printing a false sentence forever.  It is an
    AUDIT of one moment, not a CHECK.  It stays as provenance only; the compile-time
    guard is the product-connected replacement, and the row's witness field moves from
    the mis-filed `harness:` to `detector:`.
    
    MY BRIEF WAS WRONG FOUR WAYS and every refutation was a file opened -- including
    one seat leading with a defect in the brief itself: the inlined "full source"
    was condensed while its output was quoted verbatim, so the source shown could not
    have produced the output beside it.
    
    DISSENT RECORDED, NOT FOLDED: minimax leans AGAINST the guard under the addition
    licence, outweighed by two independent measured kill tables and a transferred
    in-tree warrant -- not by a count of seats.  agy raised an option-set member
    nobody else did (a dynamically bounded bitset array, citing this struct's own
    unimpl_reported) -- FILED, not taken, the shipped form being measured sufficient.
    
    Propagated byte-identical to est/, build/ and build-asan/; build/ rebuilt and the
    two-part clearance re-run (source cmp-clean AND binary newer).
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

