    landiskdisk: the landisk rig now attaches a disk, and dev_wdc.c stops being untested
    
    dev_wdc.c had ZERO rig coverage in the entire battery.  The landisk rig boots a
    ramdisk with no disk image, so wdc0 attached and was never driven -- measured: not
    one ATA opcode reaches the command register across a full boot.
    
    regress/drive_guest.py
      * landisk gains an "optional_disk" key; the driver appends `-d iR:<path>` when the
        file exists and reports RIG_DISK=1/0 either way.
      * OPTIONAL BY DESIGN.  _images/ is deliberately outside the repository, so on a
        fresh clone the file is absent and the rig boots exactly as before -- but it SAYS
        SO.  A silent revert to zero wdc coverage is indistinguishable from coverage that
        ran, which is this project's silent-coverage-loss class.
    
    regress/images.md documents the image as a RECIPE, not a download:
      dd if=/dev/zero of=wdc-scratch-64m.img bs=1M count=64
    
    MEASURED, one boot on this host:
        RIG_DISK=1   BOOT_REACHED=1   VERDICT=PASS   VALUES=SH7751R,42
        wdc0 at obio0 port 0x14000000-0x1400000f
        wd0 at wdc0 channel 0 drive 0: <wdc-scratch-64m.img>
        wd0: 128-sector PIO, CHS, 64MB, 131 cyl, 16 head, 63 sec
        "Unimplemented command" warnings: 0
        image md5 IDENTICAL before and after -- R: discards guest writes, so repeated
        runs cannot inherit state
    The SH7751R,42 token gate_crossfamily.sh checks is unaffected.
    
    TWO TRAPS, carried into the code comment and images.md so they cannot be lost:
      * `-d i:R:<path>` ABORTS.  diskimage_add() takes ALL prefix chars before a SINGLE
        colon (diskimage.c:1700-1755), so the form is `-d iR:`.
      * THE DISKED BOOT RETIRES 28-34% FEWER INSTRUCTIONS in the same wall time.
        Harmless today only because landisk has "budget": 0.  A NINSTRS floor is FILED
        for this rig, and a floor derived from a diskless boot and applied to a disked
        one is a false-FAIL waiting to happen.
    
    SCOPED HONESTLY: this gives ZERO coverage of wdc_command()'s default: arm, where
    every filed wdc defect lives.  Its value is 0xF5 as wdcnoirq's witness plus general
    wdc exercise.
    
    ADJUDICATION RECORDED (fable5, on 8 of 9 seats, provisional on its face):
    
      * landiskdisk SPLITS IN TWO and only half of it ever blocked anything.  The
        probe-level -d attach belongs to wdcflood's own witness battery -- :583 is
        unreachable without a disk, so the flood measurement necessarily ran
        disk-attached and the route was already proven.  This commit is the other half.
      * wdcringambig does NOT block wdcflood.  The corrupting store is ALREADY SILENT
        today; the latch bounds repetition of a late, miscorrelated warning.
      * The pckbc-shape restructure of :145 was checked and REJECTED for a measured
        reason: it makes head==tail <=> empty sound only on the BYTE path, while the
        bulk producer can land head exactly on tail with no warning at all.  pckbc had
        one producer; wdc has three.
      * codex's repetition rule is NOT minted -- clause (d) already forces it, and two
        overlapping statements of one rule is what the carrier refuses by name.
    
    AND A WRONG RECORD OF MINE, CORRECTED.  I recorded minimax among the seats saying
    "do NOT latch :145".  It says verbatim "So: latch both."  The don't-latch camp is glm
    alone -- and glm's own fallback, downgrade to debug(), silences HARDER than the latch
    it objects to, because debug() has the early-out at core/debugmsg.c:375 and vanishes
    entirely free-running at verbose 0.  Second time in two days a seat-count in these
    records was wrong in the direction that flattered my own ruling.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

commit 4d0981dcb6b2272d21d9f40d6f6e50582f689ccb
Author: indigoidk <indigoidk@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Tue Aug 18 10:31:48 2026 -0500

    #437: SYNCHRONIZE CACHE told the guest its data was safe and synced the wrong file
    
    MEASURED on the unmodified luna88k rig: OpenBSD issues SYNCHRONIZE CACHE once per boot,
    immediately after "/dev/sd0a: MARKING FILE SYSTEM CLEAN" -- the moment it has just repaired
    a filesystem. The handler was fsync(fileno(d->f)) and nothing else, wrong in two ways its
    own TODO had named for years:
    
      (a) fsync flushes the KERNEL's buffers; writes go through stdio and the userspace buffer
          was never flushed. overlay_set_block_in_use() at :1207-1211 already does it correctly
          and says why.
      (b) with an overlay, fwrite_helper writes ONLY to overlays[nr].f_data, so d->f held none
          of the guest's data and the overlay and bitmap were never synced at all. Measured
          before the fix: one fsync, on the base fd, and no fflush anywhere. (b) is the larger
          half -- on real power loss it costs everything the guest wrote.
    
    diskimage_sync() carries out the TODO; the handler now REPORTS the result (CHECK CONDITION /
    MEDIUM ERROR instead of GOOD), the same defect #416/#417 corrected for writes. The owner was
    asked before shipping the guest-visible half and chose to report the failure.
    
    Three design points from measurement, not preference: order is load-bearing (publishing a
    bitmap bit while the block's bytes are not durable makes a later read take the overlay's HOLE
    -- built by hand and driven through the shipping read path, the guest reads zeros where the
    base still held data, reported as a successful read); d->f is synced only when there are no
    overlays, and a non-writable flat disk returns early, because fflush on an "r" handle is UB
    per C99 7.21.5.2p2 and fsync on O_RDONLY is unspecified -- now that the result is CHECKED, a
    platform returning nonzero would turn every CD-ROM's sync into a spurious MEDIUM ERROR; and
    no do_fsync gate, since -f is about ordinary writes while this is the guest asking.
    
    *** THE DETECTOR TOOK THREE ATTEMPTS AND THE FIRST TWO WERE VACUOUS. ***
    
     1. The first pre-fix control reported a clean pass WHILE TESTING THE FIX TWICE: a quoted
        relative #include "../src/disk/diskimage.c" resolves against the INCLUDING FILE's
        directory, so no -I could substitute the pre-fix source. The detector must live INSIDE
        the tree under test.
     2. Moved there it FAILED TO COMPILE, because a row called diskimage_sync() directly -- a
        SETUP result, never a detection. Every row now drives diskimage_scsicommand() and the
        file names diskimage_sync() nowhere, which is what makes it a differential at all.
     3. The third version counted fflush/fsync CALLS, and a review seat killed it with FOUR
        mutants that all passed green, because a count cannot see WHICH FILE: rename f_bitmap to
        f_data (bitmap never synced), invert the order, delete the ordering continue, sync d->f
        instead of the bitmap. Each keeps the totals at 2 and 2. The row names even said "data
        and bitmap" while the bitmap went untouched, and the gate grepped those names as
        evidence. AND THE FIXTURE HAD NEVER WRITTEN A BYTE -- SCSICMD_WRITE is 0x0a (WRITE(6))
        driven with a 10-byte CDB, and data_out_offset was never set, so the handler refused the
        transfer. With nothing written there was no durability property to check, only counts.
    
    Shipped rows assert an ordered FILE TRACE plus two rows proving the guest's sector arrived.
    Verified every direction: 7 rows / 0 failed with the fix, 7 / 5 against pre-fix sources from
    git, and the two wrong-file mutants that beat the count-only version are now killed.
    SELFMUTANT_OK on a mutant that drops the overlay-data fflush while KEEPING the fsync, chosen
    because it leaves the counts intact so only the trace can see it. Gate 2: PASS (231 checks).
    
    FOUR COMMENTS CORRECTED, MEASURED FALSE, two written in this round's own first pass: the gate
    claimed dropping --wrap would "still pass against nothing" (it fails closed, 4 rows red); the
    selfmutant comment claimed the -fno-optimize-sibling-calls hazard shape, when omitting the
    flags yields SELFMUTANT_SETUP, which is never scored as a detection.
    
    Filed, not fixed (the stopping rule admits only a measured false pass or a wrong record):
    m437ord (the ordering rule has no gate row -- needs fault injection), m437multi (spurious
    CHECK CONDITION on a multi-overlay disk), m437rtmp (under R: both overlay files are unlinked
    at open, so the durability claim does not mean what it sounds like), idesync (IDE FLUSH CACHE
    has no durability path at all).
    
    Panel: pass 1 seven seats, pass 2 six. codex quota-walled four panels running; fable5 held by
    owner directive. Both recorded by name as HELD -- the round cannot close, which is the rule
    working. One seat disagreement settled by CHECKING rather than voting: glm reported a NULL
    deref in fatal(d->id); diskimage_scsicommand has a `d == NULL` guard before the switch.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
    
    Precommit caught four things before this landed, which is why it exists: grok's pass-2
    answer was unrecorded (and it had independently reached the detector's headline defect from
    reading alone, plus two comment defects nobody else found -- diskimage_scsicmd.cc for .c, and
    that my patch had inserted diskimage_sync() BETWEEN diskimage_dump_info()'s doc comment and
    its body, orphaning it; my anchor was the signature, and a signature is not the top of a
    function when a doc block precedes it); the build tree was stale on all three sources; and
    the detector had no identity guard. All fixed.

commit 8404520529804f747c6da0daf0c16aeba3bb5dd4
Author: indigoidk <indigoidk@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Sun Aug 30 15:12:00 2026 -0500

    wiringcliff: eight probe exemptions expire on one day, and one was about to be wired blind
    
    MEASURED, not reasoned:
    
        check_probe_wiring.py --today 2026-09-19  ->  PROBEWIRING_PASS  (46/54 gated, 8 exempt)
        check_probe_wiring.py --today 2026-09-21  ->  PROBEWIRING_FAIL  8 problem(s)
    
    So every commit after 2026-09-20 is blocked until all eight are wired or the dates move.
    The checker is right -- a dated exemption that never comes due is not an exemption -- but
    eight coming due together is a scheduling hazard rather than a deadline.
    
    THE REGRESS REVIEW NAMED FOUR; THE EXPIRY IS SHARED BY EIGHT.  It was right about the
    single-leg-mode cluster specifically (four wdc probes, one design round).  Four more expire
    the same day for entirely different reasons: two are plain gate wirings, one is a
    construction gate, one is a single variable.
    
    THE SHAPE IS BETTER THAN THE COUNT.  Three of the four cheap ones can ship before the wdc
    design round even starts, shrinking the cliff from eight to four before it arrives.  Each is
    independently shippable, and the row now carries a per-probe plan investigated read-only so
    a round can start without re-deriving any of it -- including the one mismatch to resolve
    (hpcmips_ctor defaults to build/gxemul while the construction gate drives an ASAN binary;
    --binary makes it parameterisable) and the one trap (fbpending_drain is REPORT-ONLY until
    --expect-cap is chosen, so wiring it without choosing N ships a row that cannot fail, and
    choosing N needs a measurement on a quiet machine because the probe has a 31.51 s wall-clock
    floor by construction).
    
    *** AND ONE OF THE EIGHT WAS ABOUT TO BE GATE-WIRED WITH NO WAY TO DETECT A TRUNCATION. ***
    
    pit8253_latch_probe.py's last output was a bare `PASS`/`FAIL` -- a word that occurs in dozens
    of contexts and is not uniquely greppable.  Its only SCREAMING_TOKEN was `print("MEASURED")`,
    a MID-RUN SECTION HEADER that a truncated log still contains.  A gate row written against it
    could not have distinguished a completed run from a killed one.
    
    That is the `killdrivercrash` class arriving in NEW work rather than old, and it is fixed
    before the wiring rather than after: it now emits PIT8253_LATCH_DONE, on both the pass and
    the fail path, because a completed FAILURE is not a truncation.
    
    A METHODOLOGICAL CORRECTION WORTH MORE THAN THE FIX, recorded because it nearly became a
    filed defect count.  A sweep for probes lacking a _DONE/_PASS token returned 34 of 46.  That
    number is meaningless: gate 10 verifies its probe by grepping SH_ROUND_RESULT=, a perfectly
    good completion marker in a different shape.  The convention is per-probe and per-gate, so
    there is no 34-probe defect and the question is not sweepable -- it is "does this probe's
    CONSUMER verify completion, and by what?", one probe at a time.
    
    It was caught because the pattern flagged sh4_bsc_width_probe.py, which I had confirmed
    compliant ten minutes earlier.  A pattern that selects the wrong set produces a clean,
    confident, wrong population -- the third instance today, after the outcomegap recount and
    the empty-note audit.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

