        "state":  "in work",
        "commits":  "4d0981d",
        "hold":  "FILE - the honest residual of b120\u0027s stale third",
        "closure":  {
                        "by":  "fable5",
                        "badge":  "",
                        "note":  "The base file IS flushed (#134). The code\u0027s own note admits that overlay data and the bitmap are not flushed by this command, and the inline flushes happen only when the sync option is on. This is what remains of the old row\u0027s first third once the stale claim is deleted."
                    },
        "entries":  [
                        {
                            "phase":  "assess",
                            "seat":  "opus5",
                            "date":  "2026-08-17",
                            "verdict":  "REAL",
                            "evidence":  "CONFIRMED",
                            "note":  "TRUE, AND THE FILED WORDING UNDERSTATES IT -- the only item on this brief the seat MEASURED rather than read. diskimage_scsicmd.c:915-931 fsyncs only d-\u003ef and its own TODO at :922-924 says overlays and bitmaps should be too; with an overlay, fwrite_helper writes ONLY to the overlay (diskimage.c:1291-1302), so SYNCHRONIZE CACHE syncs the one file with no new data in it. THE BIGGER HALF: it does not even sync d-\u003ef, because :926 calls fsync(fileno(d-\u003ef)) with NO fflush -- while diskimage.c:1208-1210 carries the comment explaining exactly why fflush must come first. do_fsync defaults to 0, so nothing is flushed at write time either. HOST PROBE, _exit() to simulate host death: 512B written -\u003e 0 persisted (512 LOST); 4096 -\u003e 0 (4096 LOST); 8192 -\u003e 4096 (4096 LOST); fflush+fsync in the diskimage.c shape -\u003e PERSISTED.  [panel_20260817_174701]"
