                            "note":  "*** A WRONG RECORD I PROPAGATED ALL EVENING, AND CORRECTING IT MAKES THIS WORSE. ***  I asserted repeatedly -- in briefs, in commit messages and in three ledger entries -- that `fatal()` is \u0027byte-for-byte debug()\u0027. MEASURED FALSE, found by the seat driving the pitlatch round:\n\n    void debug(...) { ...; if ((quiet_mode \u0026\u0026 !ss) || v \u003c 0) return; va_debug(...); }\n    void fatal(...) {                                              va_debug(...); }\n\n`debug()` carries an EARLY-OUT on quiet_mode and verbosity; `fatal()` has NONE. They are not the same function and never were. What I got right is that fatal() does not EXIT -- that part stands, and it was the point I was making each time.\n\nTHE CONSEQUENCE IS THE OPPOSITE OF REASSURING: a guest-drivable fatal() flood CANNOT BE SILENCED, not even with -q. The operator has no switch. So every row in this class is MORE severe than recorded, not less -- and the seat that found it noted that this STRENGTHENS the defect rather than excusing it.\n\nRecorded here rather than only in a commit message, because a claim repeated in five places is not corrected by fixing one of them -- this project\u0027s own rule is to grep for the siblings before calling a correction done.",
