        "id":  "pitclobber",
        "title":  "pitclobber  An 8253 LATCH command clobbers the counter\u0027s programmed RW format and counting mode, which a real 8254 never does",
        "state":  "in work",
        "commits":  "020a672",
        "hold":  "FIX SHIPPED (#439, 020a672); CLOSURE HELD FOR TWO REASONS, and the second is the substantive one. (1) The flagship review of the diff is held by owner allocation. (2) *** PASS 2 FOUND A REAL DEFECT THAT IS NOT YET FIXED: the latch is never CONSUMED, so after one latch the counter returns 0 indefinitely. *** Handed to the in-flight pitflip round because the consume semantics interact with the LSB/MSB flip-flop that round is fixing. Closing now would record as finished a round whose own pass 2 found it incomplete.",
        "closure":  {
                        "by":  "fable5",
                        "badge":  "",
                        "note":  "CLOSE (regress and review)"
                    },
        "entries":  [
                        {
                            "seat":  "opus5",
                            "phase":  "assess",
                            "d":  "�o"",
                            "date":  "2026-08-20",
                            "note":  "READ, NOT MEASURED -- graded honestly, and it is a sibling of pitlatch rather than part of it. dev_8253.c\u0027s mode-write arm is UNCONDITIONAL:\n\n    d-\u003emode_byte = idata;\n    d-\u003ecounter_select = (idata \u003e\u003e 6) \u0026 3;\n    d-\u003emode[d-\u003ecounter_select] = idata \u0026 0x0e;\n\nA real 8254 COUNTER-LATCH command does NOT disturb the programmed RW format or the counting mode -- it only latches the count. Here a latch write CLOBBERS mode_byte (destroying the RW format the guest set earlier) and ZEROES d-\u003emode[counter], because the latch command\u0027s mode bits are 0.\n\nSO EVEN A GUEST THAT NEVER READS THE LATCHED VALUE HAS HAD ITS COUNTER SILENTLY REPROGRAMMED. That is a distinct consequence from pitlatch\u0027s (a latch-then-read returns 0 and floods the host log): this one damages state on the WRITE alone.\n\nReachability inherits pitlatch\u0027s: OpenBSD\u0027s microtime() issues the latch command (_guest-src-openbsd22/sys/arch/i386/isa/clock.c:207-209), so the clobber happens on every call. NOT MEASURED -- no probe has yet shown the downstream effect of the corrupted RW format, and the honest grade is READ until one does. The pitlatch panel was asked prong by prong whether this travels in the same round or its own.",
                            "grade":  "MANUAL",
                            "ev":  "src/devices/dev_8253.c (read while writing the pitlatch design brief)"
                        },
                        {
                            "seat":  "opus5",
                            "phase":  "review",
                            "d":  "�o"",
                            "date":  "2026-08-20",
                            "note":  "*** THE DRIVING SEAT REFUSED THE PANEL\u0027S DESIGN AND MEASURED WHY. ***  Four of six seats converged on \u0027make the latch non-clobbering and non-fatal, RETURN THE RELOAD VALUE, and document that it is not a live count\u0027. The seat BUILT that as candidate C1 and measured it causing a GUEST DIVIDE-BY-ZERO: OpenBSD\u0027s findcpuspeed() (_guest-src-openbsd22/.../clock.c:333-344) programs the counter to 0xffff and divides by (0xffff - gettick()); under C1 gettick() returns 0xffff, so the divisor is 0.\n\nAND C1 DID NOT EVEN DELIVER WHAT IT PROMISED: it returned the HIGH BYTE TWICE (0x2e2e, not the reload 0x2e9c), because pitflip leaves the LSB/MSB selector stuck at MSB. The \u0027return the reload value\u0027 option does not return the reload value.\n\n*** THE SEAT-CLASS DOCTRINE DECIDED IT, NOT THE COUNT. ***  The three seats voting for the reload value were the PACKET-FED Ollama seats, which PIPELINE.md says are never equivalent votes on repo fact. BOTH FILE-READING seats that answered (agy, grok) warned against it, and kimi flagged the same hazard class. Codex was a SEAT FAILURE at that panel, not agreement. A 4-2 count concealed a 0-2 split among the seats that could open the file.\n\nWHAT SHIPPED (#439): the latch is ACCEPTED, latches NOTHING, and disturbs NO state -- a latched[3] flag, a write-arm branch returning before it touches mode_byte/mode[], and a read-arm branch returning 0 with the limitation documented in the code and in the file header. Guest-visible values are unchanged from the committed build, so it cannot regress anything. pitclobber travels with it: one fix design (\u0027the latch disturbs no state\u0027) covers both arms.\n\nMEASURED, rung 3, unmodified -E cats, PIT at 0x7c000040, five controls green in every run (RAM liveness, CP15 MMU-off readback, a device signature RAM cannot produce, an MMU-ON negative control, encodings checked against the emulator\u0027s own disassembly):\n    committed HEAD  mode_byte after latch 0x00 (DESTROYED), latched reads 0x00 0x00,\n                    two \u0027huh? reading from counter 0\u0027 complaints\n    shipped         mode_byte after latch 0x24 (PRESERVED), 0 complaints,\n                    findcpuspeed divisor 65535 (safe)\n\nTHE DETECTOR KILLS PER VARIANT, INCLUDING THE OVER-FIX:\n                    R1 clobber   R2 false error   R3 divisor\n    committed HEAD     FAIL          FAIL            ok\n    C1 panel shape      ok            ok            FAIL = 0\n    C2 read-arm only   FAIL           ok             ok\n    shipped             ok            ok             ok\nR3 exists specifically to catch the plausible over-fix the panel recommended.\n\n*** THE SEAT CAUGHT A VACUITY BUG IN ITS OWN FIRST DRAFT: *** the rows read absent registers as ok (-1 == -1), so three \u0027kills\u0027 were runs that never started -- an execvp PATH bug. Rows now fail on absent data (R0), and R0 is why the table above can be believed.\n\nFOUR CLAIMS IN MY BRIEF MEASURED FALSE, and one of them I had propagated all evening: fatal() is NOT \u0027byte-for-byte debug()\u0027 -- debug() returns early under quiet_mode/verbosity and fatal() never does, so a fatal() flood CANNOT BE SILENCED even with -q, which makes every flood row MORE severe. The guest function is gettick(), not microtime(). d-\u003emode[] is behaviourally inert (written in three places, read only inside a debug() printf), so pitclobber\u0027s only guest-visible effect is via mode_byte. And \u0027return the reload value\u0027 does not return the reload value.\n\nGates: gate_hygiene PASS 48, gate 2 PASS 259. dev_8253.c byte-identical in est/, build/ and /tmp/gxsec-build -- the arc tree was found STALE and synced. gate_hygiene\u0027s EXPECT_CONVERTED 17-\u003e18, measured read-only with the gate\u0027s own definitions before and after staging; that coupling has gone red three times.\n\nOPEN: the probe ships UNWIRED (gate_hygiene\u0027s own note says wiring is the structural fix but it needs an operator who can run gate_arm.sh), and pitflip is untouched -- it is why C1 returned the high byte twice.",
                            "grade":  "CONFIRMED",
                            "ev":  "regress/pit8253_latch_probe.py"
                        },
