                            "d":  "�o"",
                            "date":  "2026-08-20",
                            "note":  "*** DISSENT, RECORDED AND NOT ADOPTED -- and it is the ninth seat arriving after the fix shipped. ***  Codex was ECHO-ONLY when this panel fired (7042b = brief 6354b + 688b); it answered a health test later the same day and was re-fired against the byte-identical brief.\n\nITS VERDICT: \u0027SPLIT -- suppress the false read-side error here, document the zero fallback, and reopen write-side state and real-count semantics separately.\u0027\n\nSo it would have shipped pitlatch\u0027s read-side half alone and made pitclobber its own round. THE SHIPPED FIX PAIRED THEM. The pairing is justified rather than assumed, which is what the adjudicator required: one fix design -- \u0027the latch disturbs no state\u0027 -- covers both arms of one command, which is the reopening rule\u0027s third prong. Six seats on this design panel treated them together because the brief carried both, and the adjudicator ruled the pairing \u0027plausibly passes; it must say so\u0027.\n\nWHY IT IS NOT ADOPTED, stated so a reader can disagree with me: under the stopping rule a review finding is fixed in-round only if it is a MEASURED FALSE PASS or a WRONG RECORD, and this is a SCOPE PREFERENCE -- codex names no defect in what shipped. Unpicking a gated, probe-witnessed change to satisfy a scope opinion would trade measured safety for tidiness. *** BUT IT AGREES WITH THE PIT CLUSTER PANEL\u0027S OWN \u0027no merges\u0027 INSTINCT, and that is worth a reader\u0027s attention: two independent panels leaned toward splitting this device\u0027s defects and one brief -- mine -- put two of them together. If a future round finds the pairing caused trouble, this entry is where the warning was. ***\n\nIts other half, \u0027real-count semantics separately\u0027, is already the standing position: the file\u0027s own TODO at line 35 and #439\u0027s header both say the count is unmodelled.",
