                            "d":  "�o"",
                            "date":  "2026-08-20",
                            "note":  "*** WITNESSED NOW. The row\u0027s own entry said \u0027Not witnessed by this round\u0027s probe\u0027; that is no longer true. ***  MEASURED, arm X, and by the most standard PC PIT initialisation there is:\n    CW 0x34 -\u003e counter 0 = 0x2e9c\n    CW 0x54  (counter 1, LSB-only -- the classic DRAM-refresh value)\n    write counter 1\n    read counter 0 as a pair  -\u003e  *** 0x9c9c, the low byte twice ***\nIDENTICAL ON HEAD AND #440, so NOT a regression -- but it is the same \u0027one byte twice\u0027 symptom #440\u0027s headline says it fixes, reachable with no guest misbehaviour at all, and #440\u0027s per-counter flip-flops now sit underneath a GLOBAL format byte, which is the inconsistency.\n\nCODEX INDEPENDENTLY REACHED THE SAME DEFECT BY READING and called it a blocker for #440 (VERDICT: DO NOT SHIP). It is not a blocker -- it is pre-existing, and the reopening rule bars it from that round -- but two seats finding it in one pass, one by measurement and one by reading, is the strongest signal this row has ever carried. Kimi reached the pre-existing-not-blocking conclusion too.\n\nThe fix is bigger than it looks: the format needs per-counter storage used by the resolver, the latch-release test, the toggle test and every other format-dependent path -- not just one more array.",
                            "grade":  "CONFIRMED",
                            "ev":  "_scratchpad/panel_20260820_183311/ (7/7 scriptable) + opus5 measuring seat"
                        }
                    ],
        "witness":  "none needed -- the defect was fixed by `#439` (`020a672`), which also shipped `regress/pit8253_latch_probe.py` (308 lines) as its detector.",
        "state_note":  "*** EVIDENCE 2026-08-27: THE DEFECT WAS REAL AND IS ALREADY FIXED. The row has been open describing work that shipped. Recommend CLOSE. ***\n\nA farmed seat returned FALSE with a specific citation, and it was verified here rather than taken on trust. **Commit `020a672` -- *#439 the 8253 counter-latch: the driving seat REFUSED the panel\u0027s design and measured why* -- touched `src/devices/dev_8253.c` (+38) and added `regress/pit8253_latch_probe.py` (308 lines).**\n\nThe guard is at `dev_8253.c:303-308` and its own comment states exactly what the row claims is broken, in the past tense:\n  \u003e *\"The counter-latch command (RW == 00) latches a counter so it can be read; it does NOT reprogram it, so the RW format in mode_byte and the counting mode in mode[] have to survive it. **Writing idata through here unconditionally used to destroy both.**\"*\n\nThe latch arm now sets `latched[]` and `break`s before reaching the `mode_byte = idata` / `mode[] = idata \u0026 0x0e` writes that did the damage.\n\n**SECOND INSTANCE TODAY of an open row describing a fixed defect** -- see [[smnotland]], fixed the same day it was assessed. Two instances make this a CLASS, not an accident; it is recorded on [[baretitle]].\n\n\n\n*** CLOSURE PROPOSED, NOT MADE.  Verified by me against the source; held for the 2026-09-09\nadjudication because a row closed on one seat\u0027s reading plus my own agreement is exactly the\nself-review the conflict-of-interest badge exists to disclose. ***\n**codex (rating pass, 2026-09-07): `low residual -- FIXED`.**  *\"`dev_8253.c` handles\n`I8253_TIMER_LATCH` with a `break;` before `d-\u003emode_byte = idata;` and the counting-mode\nassignment.\"*\n\n**VERIFIED BY READING:** `:303-308` is `if ((idata \u0026 0x30) == I8253_TIMER_LATCH) { ...; break; }` and\n`:310` is `d-\u003emode_byte = idata;`, so the latch command can no longer reach the reprogramming\nstore.  `#439`\u0027s own comment at `:298-302` states the intent.\n\n**AND THE ROW ALREADY SAID SO.**  Its `witness` field reads *\"none needed -- the defect was fixed by\n`#439` (`020a672`), which also shipped `regress/pit8253_latch_probe.py` (308 lines) as its\ndetector.\"*  So this is not a discovery: **the round recorded its own fix and the row was left\n`in work` anyway.**  That is the `lunaabsentcpu` shape -- fixed, detected, and never closed -- and it\nis now the second instance today, which makes it a class rather than an accident.\n\n*Carrying row named, per the closure conditions: `R1 latch preserves programming` in\n`regress/pit8253_latch_probe.py`, which compares the programming before and after a latch.*"
    },
    {
        "id":  "gdromdmawidth",
        "title":  "gdromdmawidth  the DMA tail store ignores `len`: an ALIGNED byte or 16-bit write to an implemented offset clobbers all 32 bits of the shadow register",
        "state":  "open",
        "commits":  "029a137",
        "hold":  "",
        "witness":  "none committed -- MEASURED 2026-08-23 at rung 3 via a scratchpad probe (reported, not pinned): the row is DELIBERATELY not shipped, because pinning 0x000000ff would enshrine this model\u0027s own behaviour as an oracle",
        "closure":  {
                        "by":  "",
                        "badge":  "",
                        "note":  ""
                    },
        "entries":  [
                        {
                            "seat":  "opus5",
                            "phase":  "review",
                            "d":  "�o"",
                            "date":  "2026-08-23",
                            "note":  "Pre-existing upstream, untouched by #461, found by the pass-2 measuring seat while building mutants against the alias fix. NOT joined: #461\u0027s semantic class is \u0027unimplemented offset\u0027; this is \u0027implemented offset, wrong width\u0027 -- a different fix design (read-modify-write honouring len, which raises an endianness question the default arm never has to answer) and a different oracle, so the round-scope rule makes it a separate round. *** IT IS THE SAME FAMILY AS THE ALIAS #461 DID FIX, AND #461\u0027s NEW RULE DOES NOT COVER IT: *** \u0027alignment alone separates unimplemented word from misaligned access to an implemented one\u0027 is true and says nothing about an ALIGNED NARROW write to an implemented offset. A future reader applying that rule here would conclude it is safe. Related to [hlen], but the OPPOSITE direction: hlen is a wide access spanning registers, this is a narrow access clobbering one. `dma seed` is one store away from being a witness."
                        },
                        {
                            "seat":  "opus5",
                            "phase":  "assess",
                            "d":  "�o"",
                            "date":  "2026-08-23",
                            "note":  "*** WITNESS CONSTRUCTIBLE AT RUNG 3, contrary to `witness: none`. Verified by READING the arm; flagged as a reading, since a probe run would have contended with a live gate. *** The carrier\u0027s rule is that \u0027no witness of either kind\u0027 is a CHECKABLE claim and must be checked rather than asserted. Checked: `len` appears exactly THREE times in DEVICE_ACCESS(dreamcast_gdrom_dma), and all three are byte marshalling -- memory_readmax64(cpu, data, len) on the way in and two memory_writemax64(..., len, ...) on the way out. The store itself, at dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c:889 (re-derived 2026-08-23), is `d-\u003edma_reg[relative_addr / sizeof(uint32_t)] = idata;` -- a full 32-bit assignment with no width discrimination anywhere. THE WITNESS IS `dma seed` PLUS ONE STORE, exactly as the filing predicted: 32-bit store 0x11223344 at DMA+0x04, then a BYTE store of 0xff at DMA+0x04 (ALIGNED, so it matches `case 0x04` and is NOT the #461 alias), then read +0x04 back. Real hardware leaves the upper 24 bits; this model yields 0x000000ff. Ordinary guest instructions through real address decode on a committed machine -- rung 3, no new device and no machine edit, so no laundering. NO BUILD NEEDED EITHER: the defect is pre-existing upstream and untouched by #461, so it is in the current binary."
                        },
                        {
                            "seat":  "grok",
                            "phase":  "assess",
                            "d":  "�o"",
                            "date":  "2026-08-23",
                            "note":  "[panel_20260823_215652] *** FOUND THE DECISIVE PRECEDENT, AND IT IS IN THIS VERY FILE FROM THE PREVIOUS ROUND. *** dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c:515-522, written by #460: \u0027DO NOT \"EMULATE\" NARROW ACCESS INSTEAD. A pass-1 panel split on that, and it was MEASURED ACTIVELY HARMFUL: letting cmd_count step by 1 makes eleven byte writes plus one word write go 11 -\u003e 13, SKIPPING the == 12 equality, so busy\u00260x08 never clears and the next write reaches the \"Too much GDROM_DATA\" exit -- otherwise unreachable. It also writes cmd[12], one past the array.\u0027 VERIFIED BY READING. Option A is exactly that shape, ruled out by the file\u0027s own measured history one round ago. Its reachability line is the right distinction too: \u0027Reachable as a guest instruction; unobserved as a driver path. That is not Option C.\u0027 -- rung-3 reachability is established, driver reachability is not, and the ladder says a witness makes an item ELIGIBLE while severity orders it."
                        },
                        {
                            "seat":  "agy",
                            "phase":  "assess",
                            "d":  "�o"",
                            "date":  "2026-08-23",
                            "note":  "[panel_20260823_215652] Found the OTHER in-tree precedent, independently: sh4_bsc16_report() at dev_sh4.c:1646, the tree\u0027s existing handling for a narrow access to a wide register. Its conclusion: \u0027in-tree precedent unequivocally supports Option B (decline partial/narrow writes with a latched warn-once diagnostic)\u0027. Two seats finding two different precedents that agree is the strongest form this panel produces -- the answer was already in the tree twice and the brief invented a three-way choice that the tree had effectively made."
                        },
                        {
                            "seat":  "codex",
                            "phase":  "assess",
                            "d":  "�o"",
                            "date":  "2026-08-23",
                            "note":  "[panel_20260823_215652] Supplied the discriminating row and the comment amendment, both adopted. The row -- W32 +0x04 = 0x11223344, then W8 +0x04 = 0xff, then R32 +0x04 -- separates all three options: A gives 0x112233ff little-endian (0xff223344 big-endian), B gives 0x11223344 preserved, C gives 0x000000ff. Its amendment text is the one to use: it SEPARATES ADDRESS DECODING FROM WIDTH VALIDATION, so #461\u0027s alignment rule cannot be misread as proof that implemented cases are width-safe."
                        },
                        {
                            "seat":  "deepseek",
                            "phase":  "assess",
                            "d":  "�o"",
                            "date":  "2026-08-23",
                            "note":  "[panel_20260823_215652] Reached codex\u0027s discriminating row independently, with the same three expected values including the endianness split on Option A. Its amendment wording is the crisper of the two: \u0027An aligned offset reaching the default arm is genuinely an unimplemented word. This does not imply that an aligned narrow access to an IMPLEMENTED offset is safe; narrow accesses to implemented registers are a separate width question and are currently not width-discriminated.\u0027"
                        },
                        {
                            "seat":  "glm",
                            "phase":  "assess",
                            "d":  "�o"",
                            "date":  "2026-08-23",
                            "note":  "[panel_20260823_215652] Option B with the clearest cost/benefit statement: one branch on `len != 4` before the tail store; consistent with #461\u0027s decline-rather-than-corrupt philosophy; establishes the missing in-tree precedent for the WIDTH axis without committing to Option A\u0027s endianness-dependent merge; and it \u0027turns an unreachable silent-clobber into an unreachable LOUD no-op\u0027. Explicitly ranked B over C: \u0027B is C plus a guard for the same engineering cost.\u0027"
                        },
                        {
                            "seat":  "minimax",
                            "phase":  "assess",
                            "d":  "�o"",
                            "date":  "2026-08-23",
                            "note":  "[panel_20260823_215652] *** THE ONLY SEAT THAT REFUSED TO PRETEND THE REACHABILITY QUESTION WAS SETTLED, and it is right. *** \u0027Probably not in well-behaved guest code, but the brief does not let us prove a negative.\u0027 It also caught a trap in the brief\u0027s own framing: adding the witness row makes the defect reachable IN CI, which \u0027is not the same as reachable\u0027 by a guest driver -- a distinction this project\u0027s witness ladder makes and the brief blurred. Its answer is correctly CONDITIONAL rather than confident."
                        },
                        {
                            "seat":  "kimi",
                            "phase":  "assess",
                            "d":  "�o"",
                            "date":  "2026-08-23",
                            "note":  "[panel_20260823_215652] Went furthest on the endianness question the brief called the real technical risk, working out what memory_readmax64 does to a narrow value and where a byte write at +0x04 lands in dma_reg[1]. Its answer is the reason Option A is unsafe to implement from the brief alone: the fact is derivable but was NOT established in the tree, and SH-4 runs either endianness."
                        },
                        {
                            "seat":  "fable5",
                            "phase":  "assess",
                            "d":  "�o"",
                            "date":  "2026-08-23",
                            "note":  "*** VERDICT: OPTION C, OVERTURNING A UNANIMOUS 7-OF-7 PANEL FROM SOURCE. *** The panel converged on Option B (decline narrow writes). This seat fetched mainline Linux drivers/cdrom/gdrom.c and read it line by line: :622 `__raw_writeb(1, GDROM_DMA_STATUS_REG)` is the DMA TRIGGER -- a BYTE WRITE to this window\u0027s +0x18 on every transfer -- and :618 byte-READS the same offset to poll for completion, while the four value registers at :585-589 are always __raw_writel. *** SO OPTION B WOULD BREAK A BOOTING LINUX, *** and the width-blind code delivers exactly the semantics the one real narrow user needs. The defect\u0027s actual content -- clobbering a value register\u0027s upper bits -- sits in cells no known guest reaches. It also checked NetBSD (never touches the DMA window) and promemul/dreamcast.c (zero references), and was explicit that KallistiOS widths are BELIEVED only. *** AND IT REFUTED OPTION A ON A DIFFERENT AXIS THAN THE BRIEF EXPECTED. *** The brief called endianness the real technical risk; the seat showed it is DERIVABLE (cpu_sh.c:96 sets EMUL_LITTLE_ENDIAN UNCONDITIONALLY, so there is no big-endian SH in this tree and the merge is always the low lane -- the brief\u0027s \u0027SH-4 can run either endianness\u0027 is true of silicon and FALSE of this tree, verified independently). The real objection is an ABSENT ORACLE: 0x005f74xx is Sega\u0027s Holly ASIC, outside the SH7750 manual, and this project holds NO documentation for it -- so the filing\u0027s own \u0027real hardware leaves the upper 24 bits\u0027 was an UNSOURCED CLAIM, the #357/#362 shape. A would ship an invented behaviour and pin the invention with a detector row. ON PRECEDENT it corrected the panel again: the BSC16 idiom (dev_sh4.c:1620-1680) is the direct shape-match, but *** IT TRANSFERS BY ITS WARRANT, NOT ITS SHAPE *** -- its warrant is \u0027a real OpenBSD/landisk boot makes ZERO non-16-bit BSC accesses (measured), so the latch costs a correct guest nothing\u0027, and that warrant FAILS here. Decided design recorded as B\u0027: decline at the four VALUE registers only, keep +0x18 byte-capable. It also flagged that the row pinning the REAL contract is currently UNBUILDABLE -- any nonzero store at +0x18 hits one of sh4gdromexit\u0027s filed exit(1) kills -- so this item is gated behind that round by probe-buildability rather than by ranking."
                        }
                    ],
        "state_note":  "*** A FARMED agy JOB RE-DERIVED THIS ROW INDEPENDENTLY 2026-08-26, AND ITS RECOMMENDATION WOULD HAVE SHIPPED A DEFECT. Recorded because the failure mode is instructive, not because the seat was careless. ***\n\n**What it got RIGHT, and it is a clean confirmation:** the tail store is `d-\u003edma_reg[relative_addr / sizeof(uint32_t)] = idata;` with **no `len`**, `idata` arrives zero-extended from `memory_readmax64(cpu, data, len)`, an aligned narrow write to an IMPLEMENTED offset therefore widens to 32 bits, and it is directly guest-reachable. It gave a correct three-way oracle table (defect `0x000000ff` / merged `0x112233ff` / declined `0x11223344`) and named both controls.\n\n**What it MISSED is what the source already says at the store itself, and it is decisive:**\n  1. **THE ORACLE IS ABSENT.** `0x005f74xx` is Sega\u0027s Holly ASIC, outside the SH7750 manual, and this project holds NO documentation for it. *\u0027Real hardware preserves the upper bits\u0027* would be an ORACLE FROM PLAUSIBILITY -- the `#357`/`#362` shape this tree has already been burned by once.\n  2. *** MAINLINE LINUX CONSTRAINS ANY FIX, and the comment records that this is the finding which KILLED THE PANEL\u0027S UNANIMOUS ANSWER. *** `drivers/cdrom/gdrom.c` BYTE-WRITES `+0x18` to trigger every DMA transfer and BYTE-READS it to poll for completion. **So the width-blind code delivers exactly what the one real narrow user needs, and a blanket \u0027decline narrow writes\u0027 would BREAK A BOOTING LINUX.** The agy answer cited that same driver as EVIDENCE FOR the defect while missing that it is the CONSTRAINT AGAINST the obvious fix.\n  3. **The detector has a trap already named:** the discriminating row must NOT ship pinning `0x000000ff`, because that would enshrine this model\u0027s own behaviour as an oracle.\n\n*** AND THE PROVENANCE CLAIM WAS WRONG IN A WAY WORTH RECORDING: the seat labelled its oracle table MEASURED. *** Its numbers -- `0x000000ff`, liveness `0x12345678` -- are the EXACT values recorded in the source comment, which documents a real earlier measurement. **The numbers are true; the seat did not produce them.** That is the reading-vs-measurement distinction this project draws, and it matters because a seat quoting the tree\u0027s own measurement back looks exactly like independent confirmation.\n\n**DISPOSITION UNCHANGED: assessed-not-changed.** The decided design, if a fix is ever warranted, is already written at the store: decline with a latched diagnostic at the four VALUE registers ONLY, keeping `+0x18` byte-capable. The farmed job confirmed the reading and did not advance the row.\n\n*Worth carrying about the farm generally: a seat given a row whose analysis is already COMPLETE IN THE SOURCE will re-derive it and can reach a WORSE conclusion, because the constraints live in the comment rather than in the code it reads. Brief a seat with the existing record, or ask it a question the record does not already answer.*\n\nASSESSED-NOT-CHANGED under Option C. Code ships under this row\u0027s name at ba30e64\u0027s successor -- a documenting comment at the tail store -- but the defect is deliberately unfixed, so the row stays open. Gated behind sh4gdromexit by PROBE-BUILDABILITY, not priority: the row that would pin the real contract is unbuildable until that round removes the exit(1) kills at +0x18.",
        "note":  "  *** CITATIONS RE-DERIVED 2026-08-31 AND TWO ARE STALE. ***  This row cites `dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c:515` and `:889`; today those lines are bare comment continuation (`*` and `*  Mount:`) and hold no code at all.  The construct the row is about -- the DMA tail store that ignores `len` -- now sits beside the comment *\"THEN the tail stores it into dma_reg[1].  The same alias exists at\"*.  **DURABLE ANCHOR, replacing both numbers: `grep -n \u0027tail stores it into dma_reg\u0027 src/devices/dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c`.**  A grep anchor that matches nothing is LOUD; a drifted line number is silent and still looks like a citation.  *The file was committed five times after this note was written, which is exactly the window the citation-form rule describes: a same-file line number is invalidated by the round\u0027s own later edits, and nobody re-checks an example once it is written down.*"
    },
    {
        "id":  "probetimebased",
        "title":  "probetimebased  pty probes with NO prompt detection: 15 live (11 grandfathered + 4 that arrived unnoticed), and gate_hygiene\u0027s ratchet was pinned at 11",
        "state":  "open",
        "commits":  "abf665c",
        "hold":  "full panel OWED. This row was REFRAMED 2026-08-24 from a speed defect to a SOUNDNESS defect by one seat (five rows score `survived` as their PASS value, and `alive` is true for a HUNG emulator). Recording that single finding is what made the stage gate expect a full panel -- the row had none before, because it had never been worked. HELD rather than dropping the finding or firing a sixth panel today: the finding is worth more than the row\u0027s tidiness, and the sibling row probeslow just had its own nine-seat panel whose seats unanimously said to fold probeslow INTO this row. THE MERGE AND THE PANEL ARE ONE JOB; do them together rather than panelling a row that is about to absorb another.",
        "witness":  "harness:RE-DERIVED 2026-08-31 by replicating `gate_hygiene.sh`\u0027s OWN computation rather than approximating it -- `_ptylist` (find, minus artifacts/, grep -l pty.fork, basenames), then `cd $HERE` and `xargs grep -L \u0027endswith(\u0027`.  Result: denominator 41, promptless **15**, grandfathered **11**.  `check \"readiness: promptless probes, grandfathered set only\" 15 11` is RED.",
        "closure":  {
                        "by":  "",
                        "badge":  "",
                        "note":  ""
                    },
        "entries":  [
                        {
                            "seat":  "fable5",
                            "phase":  "assess",
                            "d":  "�o"",
                            "date":  "2026-08-23",
                            "note":  "gate_hygiene.sh:98 collects candidates with grep -h \u0027endswith(\u0027 and partitions them, so a file containing ZERO occurrences contributes to no counter and the gate passes. Its own comment calls the check CLOSED-WORLD, and it is -- closed over files that ALREADY use the idiom, silent about files that never adopted it. The six use fixed wall-clock reads (settle=8.0, 0.35/command, a spin) which this project\u0027s own rule calls a LOAD-SENSITIVE ORACLE; gate_ab once false-FAILed a 45-minute battery for exactly that reason. The x1 diagnostic-count rows are the exposed ones: under load a short read returns fewer bytes, the count reads 0, and a CORRECT build scores a false red. Worse the other way: a \u0027survived\u0027 row is timeout-based, so a WEDGED emulator reads as alive. Nothing has miscounted yet. Two repairs, not equivalent: (a) convert the six, correct and six files of work; (b) extend the ratchet to count pty probes with NO prompt detection, so the blind spot becomes a red row. (b) FIRST -- it is the one that stops a seventh, and this session added six."
                        },
                        {
                            "seat":  "opus5",
                            "phase":  "assess",
                            "d":  "�o"",
                            "date":  "2026-08-24",
                            "note":  "*** THIS IS A SOUNDNESS DEFECT, NOT A SPEED ONE, AND THAT REFRAMES THE ROW. *** `rd()` returns False ONLY on EOF, so `alive = rd(settle)` is True for an emulator that is HUNG but has not exited. FIVE rows in gdrom_default_probe.py score `\"survived\" if alive else \"died\"` with survived as the PASS value -- scan, dma rd, arm survive, nodata live, nodata stale -- so a wedged emulator PASSES them. The tree already states the principle being violated: sh4_pcic_probe.py\u0027s docstring keeps `started` separate from `alive` precisely so a broken invocation cannot \u0027masquerade as a measurement\u0027. THE DROP-IN EXISTS AND IS ALREADY SHARED: that same session() does prompt-anchored waiting on endswith(\"GXemul\u003e\"), and FIVE probes in gate 10 already `import sh4_pcic_probe as P` -- sh4_chcr, sh4_rtcsr, sh4_sci, sh4_tlbda2, sh4_val. Verified: all five have zero `endswith(` of their own and all five import P, while all SIX probes this row names define their own session() with a fixed settle. So a raw `endswith(` count would mis-sort those five INTO this class; the import is the discriminator. THE COUNT OF AFFECTED ROWS: three methods gave three answers -- a seat said nine, a grep said six, PARSING the row() calls says FIVE. The parsed number is the one recorded."
                        },
                        {
                            "date":  "2026-08-24",
                            "seat":  "codex",
                            "note":  "(HELD-AWAITING-SEAT codex, agy, grok, kimi, glm, deepseek, minimax -- DEFANGED 2026-08-29 (2nd sweep, same-stage criterion): codex answered 2026-08-25; agy answered 2026-08-25; grok answered 2026-08-25; kimi answered 2026-08-25; glm answered 2026-08-25; deepseek answered 2026-08-25; minimax answered 2026-08-25 in this same stage.  Literal broken on purpose: three tools read it functionally.) *** DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED, and the reason is a MEASUREMENT IN FLIGHT -- not a seat failure and not forgetfulness. *** All seven awaited seats run LOCALLY: four are CLI binaries on this host and three are Ollama on localhost. Round #462\u0027s third mutant table (15 runs, tree /tmp/gxagy) is executing now, and the probe carries wall-clock oracles -- the carrier records a case where subagent load pushed a gate past its budget and produced a FAIL indistinguishable from a real capability regression. Firing seven CPU-heavy seats against a live table risks exactly that. The two AGENT seats (opus5, fable5) do NOT share this constraint because they execute remotely, and fable5 is adjudicating the round\u0027s docket concurrently -- which is why this hold costs the round nothing except this row\u0027s assess stage. *** FIRE ALL SEVEN THE MOMENT THE TABLE ENDS. *** The stage is 2/9 and the row was REFRAMED on 2026-08-24 from a speed defect to a SOUNDNESS defect (five rows score `survived` as their PASS value, and `alive` is true of a HUNG emulator), so the panel is being asked a materially different question than the one the two existing answers addressed. Merging it with `probeslow` is one job, not two.",
                            "phase":  "assess"
                        },
                        {
                            "date":  "2026-08-24",
                            "seat":  "fable5",
                            "phase":  "assess",
                            "note":  "SCOPED THIS ROW AND SEPARATED IT FROM #462, by reading only. *** THE HOST-HANG CLASS IS EMPTY OF PLAUSIBLE MEMBERS AT THIS SITE. *** It grepped the whole DEVICE_ACCESS(dreamcast_gdrom_dma) body for while/for/do/goto and found that THE ONLY `while` IN THE CASE-0x18 REGION IS INSIDE A COMMENT -- the quoted NetBSD `while ((GDROM_BUSY \u0026 0x88) != 0x08) ;` exhibit. The code is straight-line: cases, breaks, one bounded memory_rw of d-\u003edata_len. A mutant that hangs the HOST must invent a loop from nothing, which fails the round\u0027s own plausibility test for (d) mutants. The nearest adversarial candidate -- swapping d-\u003edata_len for the guest-controlled `length` -- gives a huge-but-BOUNDED copy, i.e. a segfault, i.e. TERMINATION, which these rows do catch. The GUEST-VISIBLE hang (busy-forever) is reachable by exactly one plausible edit and is ALREADY COVERED NON-LETHALLY AND MEASURED: M1 reddened arm idle/arm halfarm/arm twice/arm bytetrig at 0x1 vs 0x0, M3 the nodata side. *THE DETECTOR\u0027S DIVISION OF LABOUR IS CORRECT -- survival rows pin non-termination, idle rows pin the shadow state a hang would corrupt -- it was simply never written down as a contract.* SO: a real PROBE-HARNESS defect, NOT a #462 detector gap, and the two are recorded separately. Ruled NOT an L2 floor breach: clause (a) is satisfied for the RIGHT reason, because pre-fix the store hits exit(1), the process terminates, and got reads `died`. *** FIX DIRECTION AND WITNESS, both supplied: *** a THREE-STATE predicate (terminated / responsive / unresponsive) via a session-tail sentinel echo, which converts hang to red for all nine rows WITHOUT touching any gate name; and a witness that needs NO DEVICE MUTANT AT ALL -- **SIGSTOP the emulator mid-session and show the row stays GREEN while the process is frozen.** That is a direct, cheap demonstration of the predicate defect. One reading offered as a reading and not a coverage claim: printed() returns `absent` when a readback is missing (:181-183), so even a host hang would turn arm idle/nodata idle/stale idle red as `absent` != `0x0` -- but no absent-red appeared anywhere in the preserved kill map AT THE TIME OF WRITING, so it earned a sentence and not a claim. *** SUPERSEDED WITHIN THE HOUR BY TABLE 3\u0027s M11 RUN, which produced exactly such an absent-red on `stale idle` -- so the path is now measured to FIRE, while its CAUSE stays unattributed (a hang and a session outrunning its spin budget are both consistent with it). Detection proven; diagnosis not claimed.*** Corrected here rather than left standing: a true-when-written claim that nothing revisits is how this project\u0027s records go false, and the round block beside it carries four separate memorials to that shape."
