                            "outcome":  "STAYS-OPEN-commit-message-was-wrong",
                            "note":  "*** #484\u0027s COMMIT MESSAGE AND ROUND BLOCK BOTH CLAIM THIS ROW CLOSED.  THE DETECTOR #484 SHIPPED SAYS THE OPPOSITE, IN ITS OWN CONTRACT SECTION. ***  Verbatim from `regress/diff_wdc_identify.c`: *`wdc_addtoinbuf()` itself is still UNBOUNDED.  The fix bounds its CALLER, and no row here constrains the primitive.  A future caller that pushes without a clamp reproduces the same overrun and these rows will not see it.*  Two bullets down the same block names this row as tracked open work -- **in the very commit whose message reports it closed.**  A third comment gives the structural reason: *a detector aimed at a primitive cannot verify a fix made in its caller: right defect, wrong instrument.*  *** THE HARM IS THE QUEUE-ID-COVERING-TWO-DEFECTS SHAPE REACHED THROUGH PROSE RATHER THAN THROUGH THE ROW. ***  The ids were never merged -- two correct rows existed.  One sentence in a commit message merged them, and prose is what a reader trusts when deciding whether to re-open something.  **The ledger was RIGHT and the prose was WRONG, which is why this survived: every check that compares the two reads the LEDGER as the thing needing correction.**  Found only because closing the sibling row required reading the detector instead of trusting the commit message that named it.  L4 correction inserted in the #484 round block, quoting the false sentence and leaving it standing.  **The quotation is DEFANGED** -- the id is spelled out away from the verb -- because the deriver that found this greps for exactly that adjacency and would otherwise re-match its own quotation, which is the quoted-marker-re-arms-the-tool class this project has already paid for once."
