                            "note":  "*** THE REOPENING WAS RIGHT AND ITS WARRANT WAS ALREADY OBSOLETE WHEN IT WAS WRITTEN.  This entry corrects a claim I made yesterday and repeated four times in one day. ***  The sentence quoted as proof -- *wdc_addtoinbuf() itself is still UNBOUNDED ... no row here constrains the primitive* -- is **`#473`\u0027s**, written at 04:59:34 and scoped in its own heading to *the six #473 rows*.  **`#484` bounded the primitive at 17:59:02 the same day** (`git log -L` on those lines returns exactly two commits: the upstream import and `cf300c0`), adding a refuse-before-the-store guard with a sacrificed slot and a latched diagnostic, plus four detector rows that call the primitive DIRECTLY and assert the refusal.  So at HEAD: the primitive is BOUNDED, rows DO constrain it, and a future caller that over-pushes gets a silent byte-drop plus one latched `fatal()` rather than a silent overwrite of unread data.  **All three clauses of the quoted warrant are false.**  The `#473` contract block was simply never revisited when `#484` landed -- a frozen record inside the artifact that exists to prevent frozen records.  *** WHAT SURVIVES IS ENOUGH, AND IT IS A DIFFERENT DEFECT. ***  The contract\u0027s SECOND bullet is intact and is the one that names this row: the data-port write arm pushes with **no phase guard**, confirmed by reading the path -- between handler entry and the `wdc_addtoinbuf()` calls there is a debug print, a latch and a width switch, and erent oracles and different severity language.  The title has been corrected accordingly; it previously read *\"wdcaddtoinbufbound  wdc_addtoinbuf() is still UNBOUNDED, and the live route is the data-port WRITE arm, not the DATAIN arms #473 fixed\"*.  *** ified `evbmips -e malta` and reads back through the same port, 20/20, in 1.53 s.  The sketch: arm READ(10) for 32 blocks on a `ciR:` CD-ROM, drain all but one byte of chunk 1, issue ONE 2-byte store, drain the last byte to fire the re-arm, then read.  **The committed build hands the guest its own injected byte back as disk data; a fix returns the first byte of chunk 2** -- an ABSOLUTE oracle tied to disk content through a channel outside the ring, so it satisfies the anchor rule.  Risks named honestly: the 2-byte store width is unproven at rung 3 (fallback: two byte stores), and the CD image must satisfy `res == 1` with `data_in != NULL`.  **ROUND-DESIGN CONSTRAINT, and it is the one most likely to be missed:** any fix that keeps foreign bytes out of the DATore (pckbc additionally exposes a `room_left()` helper so callers can ask first -- a fourth option nobody had named), `dev_wdc.c` now matches pckbc, and **`dev_luna88k.c` and `dev_dreamcast_maple.c` still check AFTER the store with UNLATCHED `fatal()`s** -t row\u0027s bulk-arm mutants -- a seat fired against it would have answered the wrong row\u0027s question, which is the id-merge this correction is unpicking, one level up.  Corrected.  And `tools/pipeline/fable_queue.md` says *119-row detector* where the CHANGELOG says 127."
