        "title":  "dev_pcc2.c: TEN live width kills (of twelve guards -- #119 already fixed two in this same file, and its shapefinding is a symptom of it: *** all four HIGH rows tonight were in `dev_sgi_re.c`, which invited the question of whether that file is uniquely BAD or merely uniquely LOOKED AT.  Comparing live terminating-call density against ledger attention answers it:  `dev_sgi_re.c` 15 exits / 11 rows / 4 HIGH -- heavily examined.  **`dev_pcc2.c` 13 exits / 2 rows / 0 HIGH.  `dev_lca.c` 11 exits / 2 rows / 0 HIGH.**  The kill density is comparable and the attention is not.  **`dev_lca.c` IS THE OTHER ONE AND IT IS NOT EXAMINED HERE** -- filed as a companion question rather than swept in, because a second file\u0027s worth of claims made in the same breath is how a census gets over-claimed.  MACHINE: `machine_mvme88k.c` is the only consumer.  **There is no mvme88k rig in this tree**, which is why the file has escaped attention -- and is also why a witness will be harder here than for the sgi rows: gate 9 constructs machines, but a rung-3 access witness needs a guest that reaches these registers.  *That is a reason to rank the WITNESS work, not a reason to rank the DEFECT lower: reachability by construction is established, and no rig means no coverage rather than no bug.*  *** THE STATED BLOCKER -- \"there is no rig\" -- IS NOT A REASON A RUNG-3 WITNESS IS IMPOSSIBLE, AND THIS PROJECT\u0027S OWN MOST RECENT WITNESS REFUTES IT.  Measured 2026-08-31. ***  `#498`\u0027s rung-3 witness for `sgidewidth` execs `gxemul -V -x -E sgi -e ip32 -M 64 0x80001000:\u003cDUMMY\u003e` and **`DUMMY` falls back to `/dev/null`** when `regress/dummy_kernel.bin` is absent (anchor: `grep -n \u0027DUMMY = \u0027 regress/sgi_de_width_probe.py`).  **No disk image, no kernel, no boot -- a cold-debugger probe needs a machine that CONSTRUCTS and an empty load file, and that is all.**  Verified by running all four candidates under WSL with `\u003caddr\u003e:/dev/null`: `-E sgi -e ip32`, `-E alpha -e alphabook1`, `-E mvme88k -e mvme187` and `-e mvme188` **all reach `machine:` and print a cpu line**.  *A rig is what a rung-4 BOOT needs.  Conflating the two isthe observable differs pre- and post-fix.  Constructing is necessary, not sufficient.  *My first run of this test reported all three machines failing -- because I ran the WSL-built ELF from git-bash and got `cannot execute binary file`, while my own grep for `GXemul|machine:` matched the word `gxemul` INSIDE that error and scored it a healthy banner.  A broken apparatus plus a check that matched the error text nearly filed a confident \"these machines do not construct\".*  For this row the target is **`-E m AND THE DISPOSITION WAS ALREADY SETTLED BY THIS PROJECT\u0027S OWN `#119` IN THIS SAME FILE. ***  **THE COUNT.  Twelve `if (len != 1)` guards; TEN still `exit(1)`; TWO already carry a warn-once decline.**  Deriver rather than a literal:  `grep -c \u0027if (len != 1)\u0027 src/devices/dev_pcc2.c` = 12, and an `awk` window over each guard separates the arms ending in `exit(1);` from those carrying `static int w`.  The two already-fixed arms are `PCCTWO_IPL` and `PCCTWO_MASK`.  *** AND `#119`\u0027s SHAPE IS THE ANSWER TO THE DISPOSITION QUESTION, WRITTEN IN THE FILE WITH ITS OWN ROUND NUMBER ON IT: ***  `static int w = 0;  /* #119: was exit(1) -\u003e warn-once + continue */` followed by `if (!w) { fatal(\"[ pcc2: non-byte access to PCCTWO_IPL; ignored ]\"); w = 1; }`.  So the member is **decline + latched announce**, the message says *ignored*, and a prior round already chose it here.  **This row\u0027s remaining work is to finish a job `#119` started and left two-twelfths done** -- which is a materially different framing from *twelve host kills nobody has looked at*.  *** BUT THE EXISTING PRECEDENT USES THE ANTI-PATTERN LATCH, AND THE NEW WORK MUST NOT COPY IT. ***  `static int w = 0` inside the arm is FUNCTION-STATIC -- process-global, shared by every device instance, so one guest\u0027s access silences the diagnostic for every other `pcc2` in the machine.  That is the `n_trunc_msgs` shape the `sgideprimop` seat censused hours earlier as the tree\u0027s one bad model.  The house form is a per-instance `d-\u003ewarned` bit with `(once)` in the text.  **Copy `#119`\u0027s DISPOSITION and TONE; do not copy its LATCH SCOPE** -- and the round should say whether it converts the two existing sites too, or leaves a file with two latch idioms in it.  **glm\u0027s premise audit was right about method and the conclusions split.**  *(a)* It refused my unshown claim that `fatal()` returns.  **Checked: it returns** -- `va_start / va_debug / va_end`, no exit -- so the in-file precedent really is announce-and-continue.  My premise was right and I should have inlined the evidence.  *(b)* It challenged `grep -c \u0027^\\s*exit(1);\u0027` as blind to terminating calls spelled otherwise.  The count is unchanged at 13 -- **but the four extra `exit(` hits are `#119`\u0027s own comments, which is how the fourfold precedent was found at all.**  A methodological objection that found a fact even though the number held.  *(c)* **It refuted my Q3 premise outright.**  I wrote that the read path for these arms assigns `odata`.  Measured: `odata` appears twice in the whole file and both are in a DIFFERENT access function; the twelve guarded arms handle `MEM_WRITE` and `break`.  So a declined *read* of these registers returns whatever the caller pre-set -- and `#95`\u0027s pre-zero in `memory_rw.c` supplies 0.  **The ACCEPT option\u0027s premise -- that the model computes a value and discards it -- is false here.**  *** Q1 IS ANSWERED AND THE ROW IS A DEFECT, NOT DOCUMENTATION -- confirmed from the tree\u0027s own header, which documents the legality of exactly what these guards kill. ***  `mvme_pcctworeg.h:9-10` states the PCCchip2 CSRs *\"can be accessed as bytes (8 bits), two-bytes (16 bits), or four-bytes (32 bits)\"*, and the m88k load/store path is compiled for `LS_SIZE` 1/2/4/8 and passes that width to `memory_rw`.  **So the ten live `len != 1` kills fire on accesses this fork\u0027s own vendor header calls legal**, and the *byte-wide* framing describes the MODEL rather than the chip.  My premise that a wide access might be undeliverable is refuted, and the row\u0027s severity goes UP rather than down.  A rung-3 probe has a concrete target: debugger `put h`/`put w` at `0xfff42000 + off` under `-E mvme88k -e mvme187`.  *** THIS ROW NOW HAS A MEASURED RUNG-3 WITNESS, NOT AN ENUMERATED ONE.  The; 0xfff4201c` then `st r1,r2,0x0 ; [0xfff4201c] = 0x00000042`, then `TODO: pcc2: non-byte reads and writes of PCCTWO_SCCERR`.  **A real store, through real address decode, on an unmodified in-tree machine description** -- which is the ladder\u0027s rung 3, not a debugger poke.  *** B. A PROBE-AUTHORSHIP TRAP THAT WOULD PRODUCE A CONFIDENTLY WRONG RESULT, and it is worth more than this row: `put b` NEVER REACHES THE DEVICE. ***  Measured: a byte `put` to a guarded register produces **0** device messages, while `put w` to the same register produces **1**.  The debugger\u0027s byte path passes `NO_EXCEPTIONS`, and `memory_rw` skips the device call entirely for `DM_DEFAULT` devices.  **So a byte-wide `put` probe prints success, exits 0, and would let its author pendently.**  Only **5 of the 10 live kills** are reachable from a guest instruction stream, because `dev_pcc2_access` switches on the transaction\u0027s STARTING offset and the odd-based registers cannot be an aligned start.  The rest are debugger-only.  an UNGUARDED base (`0xfff42000`) returns `0x2000000f` -- spanning CHIPID, CHIPREV, GENCTL and VECBASE -- **straight through VECBASE\u0027s guard**.  It is not a different proposition; it is already accepted today.  *** L4, AND IT WAS MINE: THE WITNESS I COchannel.  **The REACHABILITY half was never in doubt and is unaffected**: the transcript still shows `st r1,r2,0x0 ; [0xfff4201c] = 0x00000042` followed by the SCCERR message, and both controls (`put b` -\u003e 0 device messages, `put w` -\u003e 1) are indecedent was found at all**.  (c) REFUTED my Q3 premise outright: I claimed the read path assigns `odata`; it appears twice in the file and both are in a DIFFERENT access function, so a declined read returns the caller\u0027s pre-set value and the ACCEPT option\u0027s premise is false here."
