                            "grader":  "mainloop -- every link read in the tree, not inferred",
                            "ev":  "cpu_m88k_instr_loadstore.c (LS_SIZE 1/2/4/8; MISALIGNED_ACCESS guard; memory_rw with sizeof(data) == LS_SIZE); mvme_pcctworeg.h GENCTL 0x0002 / VECBASE 0x0003",
                            "outcome_note":  "*** THE ROW\u0027S OWN HELD QUESTION Q1 -- `IS A WIDE ACCESS EVEN REACHABLE?  If it cannot be, ten guards are unreachable and this row is DOCUMENTATION\u0027 -- IS ANSWERED: YES, AND BY THE MOST ORDINARY ACCESS THERE IS.  Settled by reading, in about two minutes, per the rule that anything checkable in two minutes gets CHECKED rather than judged. ***\n\nTHE CHAIN, each link read rather than assumed:\n\n1. `cpu_m88k_instr_loadstore.c` is generated for `LS_SIZE` of 1, 2, 4 AND 8 -- the m88k ISA has byte, halfword, word and doubleword load/store, so wide accesses exist at the CPU.\n2. Alignment is enforced, and enforced as an EXCEPTION rather than a silent mask: `if (addr \u0026 (LS_SIZE - 1)) m88k_exception(cpu, M88K_EXCEPTION_MISALIGNED_ACCESS, 0);`.  So a MISALIGNED wide access never reaches memory_rw -- but an ALIGNED one is untouched by that guard.\n3. The access is delivered AT FULL WIDTH IN ONE CALL, not split into bytes: the buffer is `uint8_t data[LS_SIZE]` and the call is `cpu-\u003ememory_rw(cpu, cpu-\u003emem, addr, data, sizeof(data), MEM_READ, memory_rw_flags)`.  `sizeof(data)` IS `LS_SIZE`.  So a halfword load arrives at the device handler with `len == 2`.\n4. The registers are CONSECUTIVE BYTES and the boundary is EVEN: `mvme_pcctworeg.h` gives `PCCTWO_GENCTL 0x0002` and `PCCTWO_VECBASE 0x0003` (also `PCCTWO_IPL 0x003e` / `PCCTWO_MASK 0x003f`).  **0x0002 is 2-ALIGNED.**\n\n*** THEREFORE: a guest halfword load at offset 0x0002 spans GENCTL and VECBASE, takes no misalignment exception, reaches DEVICE_ACCESS(pcc2) with len == 2, hits `case PCCTWO_GENCTL:` and dies on `if (len != 1) { fatal(); exit(1); }`.  A SINGLE NATURALLY-ALIGNED HALFWORD LOAD ENDS THE HOST. ***\n\nThat is the same shape as `#499`\u0027s finding -- an ordinary access, no unusual width choice needed in the sense of `unusual\u0027, because a halfword read of two adjacent byte registers is exactly what a driver does when it reads a register PAIR.  It is not an exotic probe.\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR THE ROW: it is a LIVE DEFECT, not documentation, and its HIGH severity stands.  The disposition question (which of the six members) is unchanged and still open; what is settled is that the question is worth asking.\n\nWHAT THIS DOES NOT SETTLE, stated so the next round does not over-read it: it establishes reachability for a LOAD spanning two byte registers.  Whether a real MVME driver actually issues one, and whether the WRITE direction is equally reachable, are separate questions -- the row\u0027s own Q1 flagged the read/write asymmetry and this note does not close that half.  A rung-3 witness already exists for this row; the natural next step is to extend it with an aligned halfword access at 0x0002 and record which direction it was taken in.",
                            "note":  "*** THE ROW\u0027S OWN HELD QUESTION Q1 -- `IS A WIDE ACCESS EVEN REACHABLE?  If it cannot be, ten guards are unreachable and this row is DOCUMENTATION\u0027 -- IS ANSWERED: YES, AND BY THE MOST ORDINARY ACCESS THERE IS.  Settled by reading, in about two minutes, per the rule that anything checkable in two minutes gets CHECKED rather than judged. ***\n\nTHE CHAIN, each link read rather than assumed:\n\n1. `cpu_m88k_instr_loadstore.c` is generated for `LS_SIZE` of 1, 2, 4 AND 8 -- the m88k ISA has byte, halfword, word and doubleword load/store, so wide accesses exist at the CPU.\n2. Alignment is enforced, and enforced as an EXCEPTION rather than a silent mask: `if (addr \u0026 (LS_SIZE - 1)) m88k_exception(cpu, M88K_EXCEPTION_MISALIGNED_ACCESS, 0);`.  So a MISALIGNED wide access never reaches memory_rw -- but an ALIGNED one is untouched by that guard.\n3. The access is delivered AT FULL WIDTH IN ONE CALL, not split into bytes: the buffer is `uint8_t data[LS_SIZE]` and the call is `cpu-\u003ememory_rw(cpu, cpu-\u003emem, addr, data, sizeof(data), MEM_READ, memory_rw_flags)`.  `sizeof(data)` IS `LS_SIZE`.  So a halfword load arrives at the device handler with `len == 2`.\n4. The registers are CONSECUTIVE BYTES and the boundary is EVEN: `mvme_pcctworeg.h` gives `PCCTWO_GENCTL 0x0002` and `PCCTWO_VECBASE 0x0003` (also `PCCTWO_IPL 0x003e` / `PCCTWO_MASK 0x003f`).  **0x0002 is 2-ALIGNED.**\n\n*** THEREFORE: a guest halfwo PAIR.  It is not an exotic probe.\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR THE ROW: it is a LIVE DEFECT, not documentation, and its HIGH severity stands.  The disposition question (which of the six members) is unchanged and still open; what is settled is that the question is worth asking.\n\nWHAT THIS DOES NOT SETTLE, stated so the next round does not over-read it: it establishes reachability for a LOAD spanning two byte registers.  Whether a real MVME driver actually issues one, and whether the WRITE direction is equally reachable, are separate questions -- the row\u0027s own Q1 flagged the read/write asymmetry and this note does not close that half.  A rung-3 witness already exists for this row; the natural next step is to extend it with an aligned halfword access at 0x0002 and record which direction it was taken in."
                        },
                        {
                            "seat":  "grok",
                            "phase":  "assess",
                            "d":  "N/A",
                            "date":  "2026-09-01",
                            "grade":  "MANUAL",
                            "outcome":  "ANSWERED",
                            "grader":  "mainloop -- the memcpy and dispatch legs re-traced, not transcribed",
                            "ev":  "_scratchpad/panel_20260901_183841_pcc2p1",
                            "outcome_note":  "FOUND THE THING THAT RESCOPES THE ROW.  *** IT ASKED WHETHER THE TOP-OF-FUNCTION `memcpy\u0027 ALREADY MAKES WIDE READS SUCCEED, AND THAT QUEhe ACCEPT premise appears false here\u0027* and asked them to check rather than take it from me; they checked and I was wrong.  ACCEPT means *the model already computes the right value and then discards it* -- which is EXACTLY what the read path does.  Tha.\n\n**So the ACCEPT member is LIVE for the read direction, and my brief told the panel it was not.**  I wrote *\u0027the ACCEPT premise appears false here\u0027* and asked them to check rather than take it from me; they checked and I was wrong.  ACCEPT means *the model already computes the right value and then discards it* -- which is EXACTLY what the read path does.  That changes the fix design: the read direction may want the kill simply DELETED (the value is already correct and no event needs announcing), while the write direction still needs a disposition."
                        },
                        {
                            "seat":  "glm",
                            "phase":  "assess",
                            "d":  "N/A",
                            "date":  "2026-09-01",
                            "grade":  "MANUAL",
                            "outcome":  "ANSWERED",
                            "grader":  "mainloop -- the memcpy and dispatch legs re-traced, not transcribed",
                            "ev":  "_scratchpad/panel_20260901_183841_pcc2p1",
                            "outcome_note":  "the reachability claim is ONE LEG SHORT -- and it was right.  *The CPU -\u003e memory_rw leg is fully inlined, but the memory_rw -\u003e device-accessor leg (that a wide access is not SPLIT into per-byte device calls) is ASSERTED, not evidenced, and that leg is the one that decides whether anything dies.*\n\n**CORRECT, AND NOW EVIDENCED.**  memory_rw.c calls `mem-\u003edevices[i].f(cpu, mem, paddr, data, len, writeflag, extra)\u0027 ONCE, with `len\u0027 passed through.  There is no loop around it.  `len\u0027 is written in exactly two places: a PAGE-STRADDLING split path, and a clamp to the device\u0027s own end which prints a diagnostic.  An aligned halfword at 0x0002 neither straddles a page nor reaches the device end, so it arrives as ONE call with len == 2.  The leg holds -- but glm was right that I had asserted it, and this is the second time today a seat caught me presenting an untraced leg as settled.",
                            "note":  "the reachability claim is ONE LEG SHORT -- and it was right.  *The CPU -\u003e memory_rw leg is fully inlined, but the memory_rw -\u003e device-accessor leg (that a wide access is not SPLIT into per-byte device calls) is ASSERTED, not evidenced, and that leg is the one that decides whether anything dies.*\n\n**CORRECT, AND NOW EVIDENCED.**  memory_rw.c calls `mem-\u003edevices[i].f(cpu, mem, paddr, data, len, writeflag, extra)\u0027 ONCE, with `len\u0027 passed through.  There is no loop around it.  `len\u0027 is written in exactly two places: a PAGE-STRADDLING split path, and a clamp to the device\u0027s own end which prints a diagnostic.  An aligned halfword at 0x0002 neither straddles a page nor reaches the device end, so it arrives as ONE call with len == 2.  The leg holds -- but glm was right that I had asserted it, and this is the second time today a seat caught me presenting an untraced leg as settled."
                        },
