{"id": "ovsync", "title": "ovsync  SYNCHRONIZE CACHE does not flush overlay and bitmap files", "state": "in work", "commits": "4d0981d", "hold": "FILE - the honest residual of b120's stale third", "closure": {"by": "fable5", "badge": "", "note": "The base file IS flushed (#134). The code's own note admits that overlay data and the bitmap are not flushed by this command, and the inline flushes happen only when the sync option is on. This is what remains of the old row's first third once the stale claim is deleted."}, "witness": "none yet -- guest-reachable, but a witness needs a build. Note the LIVE half is reachable through the same path as [[b120]], so one probe can witness both.", "hold_question": "SYNCHRONIZE CACHE does not flush overlay and bitmap files. Work SHIPPED as 4d0981d. QUESTION: is the residual real, and may the row close? The hold calls it 'the honest residual of b120's stale third'. Two things to settle: (a) does the shipped fix leave any path where a guest-issued SYNCHRONIZE CACHE returns success while overlay or bitmap data is still unflushed; (b) this row's assess, research AND review stages are ALL held for you -- so answer whether the shipped work is complete, not merely whether the code is correct.", "state_note": "*** ADJUDICATED 2026-08-27 (fable): the collapse onto [[b120]] is CONFIRMED **MEASURED**, but it is a collapse of the SAFETY HALF ONLY -- do NOT read it as 'drop ovsync'. ***\n\nb120's fix makes opcode 0x91 an honest CHECK CONDITION. **It does not implement SYNCHRONIZE CACHE(16), so it does not make the flush happen.** That residual -- implement 0x91, or formally decline it -- is NOT covered by b120 and is what this row should carry from now on.\n\nSo: keep this row OPEN and NARROWED to the implement-or-decline question. The false-success danger is b120's; the missing flush is this row's.\n\n*** EVIDENCE 2026-08-27: TRUE, BUT NARROWER THAN THE TITLE -- and the live half turns out to be AN INSTANCE OF [[b120]], which neither row could show on its own. ***\n\n**HALF THAT IS ALREADY FIXED:** SYNCHRONIZE CACHE(10) is `SCSICMD_SYNCHRONIZE_CACHE = 0x35` (`diskimage.h:254`), handled at `diskimage_scsicmd.c:915`, and it **does** flush -- `:932` calls `diskimage_sync(d)`, with `:925` recording that this was the TODO carried out. *So the title, read as covering all synchronize-cache commands, is FALSE for the one the tree implements.*\n\n*** THE LIVE HALF, AND ITS DIAGNOSIS: SYNCHRONIZE CACHE(16) IS OPCODE `0x91`, AND `0x91` APPEARS **ZERO** TIMES IN THE DISPATCHER. *** It therefore falls into the `default:` arm at `:1380` -- which logs through a verbosity-gated call, never sets a status, and returns the same value a successful command returns. **The guest asks for a cache flush, is told GOOD, and nothing is flushed.**\n\n> **SO THIS IS NOT AN INDEPENDENT DEFECT: IT IS [[b120]] WEARING A SPECIFIC OPCODE.** `b120`'s fix -- refuse an unknown opcode with CHECK CONDITION / ILLEGAL REQUEST via the existing `diskimage__return_check_condition()` helper -- would convert this from a SILENT FALSE SUCCESS into an honest refusal, letting a guest fall back to CACHE(10). **A full `ovsync` fix additionally implements `0x91`**; the two are separable and should be ranked separately.\n\n**Rank `b120` first.** It is the general case, it is cheaper, and it makes this row's damage visible instead of silent. Implementing `0x91` without fixing `b120` would close one opcode and leave every other unimplemented one lying about success.\n\n*Method note: the seat reported the CACHE(10)/CACHE(16) split and the existing `diskimage_sync()` idiom; the `0x91`-falls-to-default connection was found here by grepping the dispatcher for the opcode and getting zero hits. **A count of zero was the whole finding** -- and it is only evidence because the denominator was checked: the file really does dispatch by opcode, and `0x35` is present in the same census.*\n\n"}
LATEST ENTRY [{"seat": "codex", "phase": "review", "d": "\u2713", "date": "2026-08-26", "ev": "codex 5.6-SOL xhigh, fired against the row's own hold_question (_scratchpad/holdq_briefs/holdq_ovsync.md; answer at scratchpad/holdq_out/ovsync.txt) -- REVIEW phase", "note": "*** NO-GO FOR CLOSURE AS PRESENTLY WORDED (REVIEW phase, same seat, recorded separately because the phases have different answers). *** The row may close only after one of two explicit acts: add a dirty-overlay 0x91 regression, or NARROW `ovsync` to SYNCHRONIZE CACHE(10) and TRANSFER the 0x91 instance to `b120`. TWO RECORD DEFECTS IT FOUND ON THE WAY, both checkable: (1) **the original filing's premise was false** -- it assumed the BASE file was already flushed (#134), when before `4d0981d` the handler called `fsync()` with no `fflush()`, so even the flat base's stdio buffer was unflushed. The shipped commit is therefore BETTER than its own filing claimed. (2) **the pass-2 statement that the 0x91 sibling was filed is NOT explicit in `b120`'s record**, and no dedicated SC(16) row exists -- so that is a wrong record to fix rather than a filing to assume. COVERAGE GAP NAMED: the committed differential exercises only 0x35 (`diff_diskimage_sync.c:371`), and the later `e051fc5` coverage commit gates ordering without adding a 0x91 case. It also CLOSED the 0x35 question properly instead of asserting it: no ordinary 0x35 path returns GOOD with dirty data pending, because a missing data handle fails, a missing bitmap handle is unreachable (`diskimage_add_overlay()` appends only after opening BOTH files), and a read-only flat image cannot hold guest-written dirty data."}]
{"id": "wdcflood", "state": "in work", "commits": "92e917e", "title": "wdcflood  wdc_command()'s default: fatal() is unlatched -- 100 commands, 100 host log lines", "closure": {"by": "fable5", "badge": "", "note": "CLOSE (assess)"}, "state_note": "*** STATUS FIRST, BECAUSE THIS NOTE OPENED WITH PRE-FIX GUIDANCE THAT THE SHIPPED FIX HAS ALREADY CONSUMED.  Added 2026-08-29. ***  **FIXED at HEAD** -- #464 (six unlatched guest-drivable `fatal()` sites, re-scoped from one arm to six), #485 (the completion interrupt, without which the abort was polling-only), #486 (repaired the round's own non-discriminating clause-(b) gate row).  A rung-3 witness with both admissibility controls is on the row.  **It stays `in work` for exactly two reasons: a minimax pass-2 seat failure owed a re-fire with a repaired brief, and closure adjudication.**  The title is the FOUNDING claim, which is this ledger's convention and not a wrong record -- but a reader meeting `in work` plus a '100 commands, 100 host log lines' title plus work-it-this-way guidance concludes the flood is live, which is the es438 / [[ledgerstatelies]] shape.\n\n  *** THE THREE-MEMBERED OPTION SET APPLIES DIRECTLY TO THIS ROW (carrier rule, minted 2026-08-25 from #463). *** #463 de-fatalised a device by framing the choice as KEEP the unlatched `fatal()` versus DELETE it, chose delete, and its own research stage then found the third member had been shipping in-tree the whole time: **`memory_rw.c:495-523` bounds a guest-drivable diagnostic to 8 emissions via `n_trunc_msgs`, because 'probing at the end of a device's mapped range is common and benign'.** The carrier already said 'a guest-drivable fatal() needs a latch and not a verbosity flag' -- standing rule, never consulted by that round's two-membered framing. **So before this row is worked: the option set is DELETE / DECLINE / DECLINE+BOUNDED-ANNOUNCE, and the first step is to grep the fork for the existing idiom rather than reason from first principles.** #463 also established that a bounded announce is not merely acceptable but RECOVERS the self-announcing property that de-fatalising destroys -- which for an unlatched flood is the whole point, since the flood and the announcement are the same channel.  *** MEASURED 2026-08-25, AND THE ROW IS AIMED AT THE WRONG SITE. *** The opus5 seat built a `cp -a` copy at /tmp/gxwdc, verified freshness independently of MAKE_RC, and left GXEMUL-SEC untouched (verified by `git status --porcelain`: no src file modified). *** THE FLOOD IS CONFIRMED AND IS WORSE THAN THE TITLE SAYS: *** 20 stores produce 20 `fatal()` lines WITH and WITHOUT `-q` (0 `debug()` lines either way), and the OVER-READ site at 500 iterations produced 2000 lines / 158,449 bytes of host log (`regress/artifacts/wdcflood/witness_20260825/n3.out`, preserved off the volatile session scratchpad this row used to cite). *** CORRECTED IN PLACE 2026-08-26: this sentence said \"the read path\", which points a reader at the non-8-bit READ guard. `n3.out` is 2000x \"reading too much\" -- the RING OVER-READ. Same numbers, wrong site named. The correction had already landed in the round block, the source comment and the gate comment while this field kept the false wording -- which is the corrections-land-in-CHANGELOG-only failure this ledger records having made twice before, now three times. It was found by a mechanical sweep for stale claims across all five records, not by reading. *** *** BUT `:583`, THE SITE THIS ROW NAMES, IS THE MORE GATED OF TWO FLOODS. *** It requires an attached IDE image (`:461-468` early-returns with a silenceable `debug()` otherwise) and yields ONE line per store. The unnamed sibling `wdc_get_inbuf` over-read at `:160` requires NOTHING -- landisk adds wdc unconditionally at machine_landisk.c:90 -- is on the READ path, and yields FOUR lines per single guest instruction (measured: mov.b/mov.w/mov.l x20 give 20/40/80). SIBLING CENSUS: ten `fatal()` in dev_wdc.c, FOUR latched by #337, SIX UNLATCHED -- :145, :160, :583, :607, :704, :1025. **Fixing :583 alone leaves the strictly worse :160 floodable at 4x with no precondition**, which is the project's recorded 'grep for its siblings' failure. SCOPE RULING SOUGHT: the round is the FILE, not the line -- six sites, one design, one detector. *** WHY #337 MISSED IT, AND IT IS EXPLICABLE RATHER THAN AN OVERSIGHT: *** that round's scope was \"each replaced `exit(1)`\", and `:583` was always a `fatal()`, never an `exit(1)`, so it fell outside the enumeration. *** THE OPTION-SET PREMISE WAS REFUTED, AND IT WAS MINE. *** The three-membered option set rule describes member 3 as \"announces the first N occurrences and then goes quiet\". That describes `memory_rw.c:495-523` -- which is the tree's ONLY counter-style bound, is DOUBLE-GATED so the counter never increments at default verbosity (`&&` short-circuits), is GLOBAL not per-instance, and prints no give-up notice. The house idiom is different and mature: **N=1 PER-INSTANCE LATCH plus an explicit \"(further occurrences suppressed)\" in the message text**, at roughly 34 sites across 11 device files. `dev_wdc.c` itself holds FOUR (`warned_badlen`, `warned_atapi_err`, `warned_atapi_dataout`, struct at :75-77) and is the tree's REFERENCE implementation -- dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c:147 says its own latch is \"Modelled on dev_wdc.c's warned_badlen (#337)\". *** AND `fatal()` SHOULD BE KEPT, ON A RECORDED HOUSE WARRANT: *** dev_asc.c:250-257 states that under `-q` verbosity settles at VERBOSITY_ERROR, so a WARNING-level debugmsg would be INVISIBLE to the boot-log hygiene grep and an ERROR-level one would trip #261's break-on-ERROR. The chronology runs one way -- #245 chose verbosity-gating; #265/#269, #337, #460, #461 all chose latch + fatal(). TWO HAZARDS FOR THE DIFF, both from the seat: (1) SKETCH RULE -- DEVICE_ACCESS(wdc) has a common tail at :1019-1030 (`ret:` -> memory_writemax64 -> return 1), so any decline at the ACCESS level must `goto ret`; `wdc_command()` itself is void and called at :1003, so returning inside IT is safe. (2) CLAUSE (d) -- the guard would read the new `warned_*` field while the body reads `d->cur_command`, so the detector owes a state where a SECOND, DIFFERENT unimplemented command arrives after the latch has set; the 0x5a-vs-0xaa control is the seed for that row. OPEN QUESTION THE SEAT DECLINED TO SETTLE, recorded as open rather than guessed: #337 notes its two warned_badlen sites SHARE one flag, while #460's gdrom comment records a MEASURED warrant that per-site bits beat a shared bit when two sites run in one session. For pure flood control a shared latch is strictly better; the tradeoff is information loss, and which matters here is unmeasured. PRIVILEGE, stated exactly: no device-layer check exists and memory_rw dispatches on physical address, so \"unprivileged\" overstates it -- the exact claim is \"any code that can reach the I/O window, with no emulator-side gate\".  *** OWNER SCOPE DECISION 2026-08-25: THE ROUND IS THE WHOLE FILE -- ALL SIX UNLATCHED SITES, ONE DESIGN, ONE DETECTOR. *** Asked to choose between the filed single site, the two measured floods, and the file, the owner took the file. So the round covers :145, :160, :583, :607, :704 and :1025 using dev_wdc.c's own #337 idiom (per-instance latch + \"(further occurrences suppressed)\"), keeping `fatal()` on the dev_asc.c:250-257 warrant and keeping the existing `d->error |= WDCE_ABRT`. The reopening rule permits it: same device, same semantic class, one fix design, one detector, no new oracle. **Only :160 and :583 carry a rung-3 witness today; the other four are joined on the SAME-CLASS clause and each should get a one-store witness in pass 1, per #337's measured partial-file miss.**  *** PASS 1 (panel_20260825_195526): agy SETTLED Q1 WITH AN ARGUMENT NOBODY HAD MADE, AND IT DISSOLVES THE EVIDENCE CONFLICT RATHER THAN PICKING A SIDE. *** The brief posed shared-vs-per-site latching as #337 (shares `warned_badlen` across two sites) versus #460 (measured warrant for per-site bits). **Neither is wrong: #337's two sites are the two arms of ONE ENDIANNESS BRANCH.** VERIFIED BY READING: dev_wdc.c:709 is `if (cpu->byte_order == EMUL_LITTLE_ENDIAN) {` containing :731, and :739 is its `} else {` containing :761. A CPU does not flip endianness mid-session, so those two are MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE AT RUNTIME and sharing a flag loses exactly zero information. #460's warrant governs sites that can BOTH fire, which is all six here. **DESIGN SETTLED: SIX separate per-instance latches** (or one bitfield with six masks), never shared across read/write or overrun/over-read pairs. *** Q2 SETTLED: NO SUPPRESSION NOTICE. *** Census verified independently by agy against all of `src/devices/`: only dev_asc.c (3 sites) and dev_vga.c (1) carry it, and dev_wdc.c's own four #337 sites do not. Adding it to six new sites would make the file internally inconsistent; editing the four existing ones is unjustified churn into working code. *** Q3 ANSWERED, AND IT CARRIES AN IMPLEMENTATION TRAP THE NAIVE FIX WALKS INTO. *** `:160` is a GUEST error and is safe to latch -- real hardware returns open bus on a read past the transfer, and the emulator returns `(uint64_t) -1`. **BUT the `return (uint64_t) -1;` MUST STAY OUTSIDE the latch guard.** Nesting it inside `if (!d->warned_...)` makes the SECOND over-read fall through to `d->inbuf_tail = (d->inbuf_tail + 1) % WDC_INBUF_SIZE;` and CORRUPT THE RING POINTER -- the fix would introduce a defect worse than the flood it removes. Same shape at `:1025`, inside the common tail: guard the `fatal()` only; `data[0] = odata;` and `return 1;` run regardless. *** Q5: agy CAUGHT AN INTERNAL CONTRADICTION IN MY OWN BRIEF. *** The proposed design specifies scalar `warned_*` fields while Q5 demanded a detector catch a per-device-versus-per-opcode mutant -- which would require a 256-bit bitmap (`uint32_t warned_unimpl_cmd[8]`). Resolution: the scalar per-site latch is correct (it is what stops a guest cycling 0x00..0xff for 256 lines), and **the TRUE clause-(d) mutant is a different one: scope `d->error |= WDCE_ABRT` INSIDE the latch guard at :583.** Then the second unimplemented command silently SUCCEEDS. A detector that counts only host log lines passes it clean. That is a better (d) mutant than the one the brief proposed and it is the round's headline detector obligation. TWO FURTHER EVASIONS from the same seat: nesting the `return -1` at :160 (above), and using `static int warned` instead of a per-instance field, which silences a secondary IDE controller at 0x170 because the primary at 0x1f0 already warned. *** Q7 WITNESS COSTS, one of which is a genuine obstacle: *** `:607` = a 16-bit write to wd_seccnt; `:1025` = a 16-bit read from wd_status; `:704` = a store to wd_data with no active command -- all cheap. **`:145` is NOT cheap: WDC_MAX_SECTORS is 512 and WDC_INBUF_SIZE is `512*(512+1)` = 262,656 bytes (verified by reading dev_wdc.c:47-48), so filling the ring by guest stores costs ~65,664 32-bit stores.** The disk-read fill path at :343 moves 512 bytes per sector and would be far cheaper, but it needs an attached image -- i.e. it needs `landiskdisk` first. ALL THREE of agy's checkable claims were VERIFIED against source rather than credited: the endianness branch, the buffer arithmetic, and that wdc_get_inbuf() is called once per BYTE inside 16/32-bit transfers (:616, :620, :622, :623 little-endian; :627, :629, :630 big-endian), which is exactly the measured 4 lines per 32-bit guest load.  *** FULL PASS-1 PANEL COMPLETE (panel_20260825_195526): 8 SEATS ANSWERED. *** codex 501 KB, grok 19.5 KB, minimax 13.6 KB, agy 13.2 KB, kimi-k3:cloud 10.8 KB (the SUBSTITUTE transport, fired by hand -- the CLI is 403-walled), glm 4.4 KB, deepseek 4.1 KB. The kimi CLI is the only seat failure and it is a transport wall, not a seat outage. *** Q2 IS UNANIMOUS ACROSS EVERY SEAT THAT ANSWERED IT: NO SUPPRESSION NOTICE. *** *** Q1 SPLIT 3-1-2, AND THE SPLIT IS DECIDED BY EVIDENCE RATHER THAN COUNT. *** SIX separate latches: agy, grok, kimi. FIVE plus a per-opcode set for :583: deepseek. FOUR (pairing): glm, minimax. **The two pairing seats both cite #337's shared `warned_badlen` as the licence -- and that precedent is measurably not a licence.** agy and grok independently derived that #337's two sites are the two arms of ONE ENDIANNESS BRANCH (:709 `if (cpu->byte_order == EMUL_LITTLE_ENDIAN)`, :739 its `} else {`), so they are mutually exclusive at runtime and sharing loses nothing. grok put it exactly: *\"two endian copies of one check, not a licence to pair different events.\"* kimi added an independent refutation from THIS ROUND'S OWN WITNESS: the :160 over-read fired with NO DISK ATTACHED, so no overrun could have occurred -- **the two ring halves are causally independent, which is precisely what the pairing assumes they are not.** DESIGN SETTLED: six per-instance flags, wrap ONLY the `fatal()`, leave every guest-visible side effect outside the guard. *** Q3: FOUR SEATS INDEPENDENTLY LOCATE THE REAL DEFECT AT :145, NOT :160 -- see `wdcringambig`, filed. *** glm: *\":145 is an emulator error, do NOT latch without fixing the ring overrun.\"* minimax: *\":145 IS a real defect that the latch does not fix -- it corrupts the ring and continues, so a TODO is owed there.\"* kimi located it first and most precisely. grok: *\":145 is the unfixed pckbc/asc wrap, not a twin of the measured :160 flood.\"* Only agy said :160 was the site to worry about, and it was answering the question as the brief framed it. *** Q5 WAS CONFUSED IN THE BRIEF AND THREE SEATS SAID SO. *** grok: *\"Q5's per-opcode (d) mutant contradicts the per-instance design the brief actually proposes -- that mutant is a spec change, not a floor test.\"* minimax and agy said the same. deepseek dissents usefully in the other direction: :583's diagnostic VALUE is the opcode, so a single flag hides distinct guest actions and a 256-bit set would preserve it. **Disposition: per-instance scalar for the fix (it is what stops a guest cycling 0x00..0xff for 256 lines); deepseek's per-opcode proposal is a SPEC IMPROVEMENT and is filed, not folded in.** The real (d) mutants, from three seats: scope `d->error |= WDCE_ABRT` INSIDE the latch at :583 (agy -- the second unknown command then silently SUCCEEDS); nest the `return (uint64_t) -1;` inside the guard at :160 (agy -- corrupts inbuf_tail); and **reuse a GUEST-RESETTABLE field such as `d->seccnt` as the latch (glm)** -- the sharpest of the three, because the guard then reads a field the guest controls. *** THE :145 WITNESS IS NOT A ONE-STORE WITNESS, AND codex SUPPLIED THE TWO HONEST ROUTES: *** prime `head = WDC_INBUF_SIZE - 1, tail = 0` in the detector and call the producer once; or, for a real guest-path measurement, attach an image and issue **513 undrained IDENTIFY commands** -- each adds 512 bytes through `wdc_addtoinbuf`, and the final byte of the 513th reaches the 262,656-byte wrap. The second route needs `landiskdisk`. *** AND A DETECTOR RULE FROM codex THAT APPLIES TO EVERY ROW: each site row must FIRE THE SITE TWICE. \"A one-off first warning proves announcement, not suppression.\" *** A row that triggers a site once cannot distinguish a working latch from no latch at all -- which is this project's vacuity floor, stated for the latch class specifically.  *** A WRONG RECORD IN THIS ROW, WRITTEN BY ME AND CAUGHT BY THE ADJUDICATOR. CORRECTED 2026-08-25. *** Earlier in this note minimax is attributed the position that :145 is a real defect the latch does not fix, and is COUNTED AMONG SEATS SAYING DO-NOT-LATCH. **That count is FALSE.** minimax's Q3 says verbatim: **\"So: latch both.\"** Its position is latch-and-mark, which is the disposition this round adopted -- it was recorded as dissenting from a ruling it agreed with. *** THE DON'T-LATCH CAMP IS glm ALONE *** (\"`:145` is an emulator error, do NOT latch without fixing the ring overrun\"), which turns a 2-of-8 reading into 1-of-8. **AND glm's OWN FALLBACK SILENCES HARDER THAN THE LATCH IT OBJECTS TO:** it proposes \"downgrade it to `debug()`\", and `debug()` has the early-out at `core/debugmsg.c:375` -- free-running at `verbose == 0` gives `v < 0`, so it vanishes ENTIRELY in production, while a latched `fatal()` still announces the first occurrence and cannot be silenced by `-q`. The dissent's remedy is strictly worse for the property the dissent defends. **Weigh deepseek's Q5 dissent knowing its ring analysis was the panel's weakest:** its \":145 is a guest error -- the guest wrote more data than the ring can hold\" is factually off. The EMULATOR writes that ring on the guest's behalf; the defect is state representation. *Second time in two days that a seat-count in this project's records was wrong in the direction flattering the writer's own ruling. The tally was never the argument -- but a misquoted seat is a wrong record whichever way it points.*  *** ADJUDICATED 2026-08-25 (fable5, main-loop hat, on 8 of 9 mandated seats -- PROVISIONAL on its face w.r.t. the absent ninth, per that seat's own rule). *** **(1) Q5 UPHELD, two sharpenings.** Scalar per-device flag at :583 this round; deepseek's per-opcode set FILED. Per-opcode-versus-per-device is a SPEC choice, and deepseek's framing (\"the scalar IS the (d) mutant\") is circular -- it assumes per-opcode is the baseline and then calls the alternative a weakening. Clause (d) tests the CHOSEN spec's repair; it does not adjudicate between two acceptable specs. Put per-device in the CONTRACT (clause e) -- \"does not claim per-opcode uniqueness at :583\" -- so a later alternating-opcode observation is OUTSIDE the contract rather than a floor breach. **SHARPENING 1, the best single point of the panel: the two-different-opcodes row SHIPS ANYWAY, with the expectation INVERTED -- ONE LINE TOTAL.** Same stimulus deepseek wanted, opposite oracle. It kills a last-warned-opcode implementation (under which an alternating 0x5a/0xaa guest floods forever) AND pins the per-device spec in one row. **SHARPENING 2: the filed item inherits a TWO-membered framing and must not.** Honest weakening of my own precedent: #337's latched messages at :816 and :855 carry NO VALUE PAYLOAD -- :583 is the only site whose message names an opcode -- so \"three latches in this file\" is weaker precedent for discarding value-resolution than it looks. The filed option set is THREE-membered: scalar (shipped), per-opcode set (deepseek), and the in-tree COUNT-BOUND idiom (`n_trunc_msgs`, `src/cpus/memory_rw.c:495-523`) -- which this row already banners and which nobody in the dispute named. **(2) `wdcringambig` DOES NOT BLOCK.** The latch bounds the announcement to first occurrence; it does not silence it. **The corrupting store is ALREADY SILENT TODAY** -- :141-146 stores, advances, then tests, so the wrap line fires once per 262,656-byte lap and the N+1th store that destroys data prints nothing. What the latch removes is repetition of a late, miscorrelated warning. THREE OBLIGATIONS make it honest, all in-round and cheap: (a) /* #NNN */ comments at :145 and :160 naming `wdcringambig` -- \"latch bounds the announcement; the ring invariant is untouched\"; (b) the detector CONTRACT disclaims ring health, and the :145 primed-state row is marked for re-pin under the oracle-correction rule when `wdcringambig` lands; (c) file glm's dissent VERBATIM into `wdcringambig` as provenance. **THE TEMPTING THIRD PATH WAS CHECKED AND REJECTED FOR A MEASURED REASON.** grok and kimi both prefer restructuring :145 to the pckbc #288 shape (check-before-store, drop, latch -- verified in-tree at `dev_pckbc.c:158-167`). It must NOT fold in, and not merely by the reopening rule: **the restructure makes head==tail <=> empty sound ONLY on the byte path.** The bulk producer at :343-349 compares only against WDC_INBUF_SIZE and can land head exactly on tail with NO WARNING AT ALL (:876-879 mirrors it on tail). *pckbc had ONE producer; wdc has THREE.* A byte-path-only repair ships a half-invariant under a record that reads as soundness. **(3) SEQUENCING: `landiskdisk` SPLITS IN TWO.** The PROBE-level -d attach is part of `wdcflood`'s own witness battery and blocks nothing -- verified: :583 is unreachable without a disk (:461-468 early-returns), so the opus flood measurement necessarily ran disk-attached and the route is already proven. The BATTERY-RIG integration -- the half carrying the 28-34% NINSTRS poison -- is an independent deliverable and NOT a blocker. **The primed-head/tail row is a RUNG-1 DETECTOR row and cannot discharge :145's witness**; the 513-IDENTIFY route can, and was verified sound: no ring reset exists at command start (the only head/tail writes are :143, :165, :347-349, :876-879), IDENTIFY feeds wdc_addtoinbuf 512 bytes per command (:525-533), and 513 x 512 = 262,656 = WDC_INBUF_SIZE exactly. **(4) THE REPETITION RULE: DO NOT MINT, and my dichotomy was wrong on BOTH horns.** Clause (a) does NOT cover it -- (a) is DETECTOR-level and the defect is ROW-level: a one-off row at a latched site prints one line pre-fix and one line post-fix, colourless in both worlds, so it contributes nothing to (a) and hides behind rows that do. **But no new clause is needed, because clause (d) already forces it mechanically:** the fix adds six latch predicates, (d)-DEPENDS-ON mandates a null-repair mutant per predicate (flag never set), and the only row shape that reddens a nulled latch is one firing the same site twice with an EXACT count. Codex's sentence is the row-construction corollary of (d) for the latch class -- the same relation the accept-row failability rule bears to the de-escalation clause. Two overlapping statements of one rule is what the carrier refuses by name. **Disposition: one derivation sentence in the round block, not a carrier clause.** If a future latch round ships a SURVIVING one-off row, that measured escape is the (f)-style warrant to mint. **REFINEMENT ON THE ROWS (minimax): repetition with a \"<= 1 line\" oracle passes a mutant that prints ZERO. The oracle must be EXACTLY ONE, THEN ZERO** -- announcement AND suppression, which is the pair a latch actually promises.  *** PASS 2 (panel_20260825_204943) FOUND THREE ESCAPES AGAINST A DETECTOR MEASURED 14/14, AND ONE OF THEM IS A STRUCTURAL BLINDNESS OF THE ROWS RATHER THAN A GAP IN THEM. *** All three are L1 -- pre-ship construction, fixed in-round, not filed. **(1) glm -- THE RESET-ON-NORMAL-OPERATION ESCAPE, and it is the sharpest.** ONE line, reusing the existing flag, guard and branch, added inside an existing case for a VALID command: `d->warned_unimpl_cmd = 0;`. **Every site row fires its site TWICE IN SUCCESSION with no normal device operation between**, so the flag is never reset between fires and the row stays green -- while a real guest that probes an unimplemented opcode, issues IDENTIFY, probes again, issues IDENTIFY, floods forever. The same one-line trick applies at every one of the six sites. *This is the `#463` distinctness-invariance finding wearing new clothes: rows that test a property back-to-back are invariant under any edit that only changes what happens BETWEEN the two fires.* **(2) agy -- THE COVERAGE CENSUS, CONFIRMED AGAINST MY OWN PROBE.** \"The detector's six site rows do not test six distinct C sites\": `:145` has **ZERO** rows while its sibling `:160` has two, and `:583` has three. Also **the non-byte rows probe 16-bit ONLY** -- `mov.w` at both :607 and :1025, never `mov.l`, so a `len != 1` guard narrowed to `len == 2` would escape. **(3) deepseek -- `d->warned_X = !d->warned_X;`**, claimed to toggle so that fires 1 and 3 print while the detector only fires twice. **READING SAYS THIS DOES NOT WORK** -- the assignment sits INSIDE the guard (`if (!d->warned_unimpl_cmd) { d->warned_unimpl_cmd = 1; ...}`), so fire 2 never enters and the toggle never runs. **RECORDED AS A PREDICTION TO BE MEASURED, NOT AS REFUTED** -- this project settles disagreements by measurement, and the seat may be modelling a placement I have not considered. It goes in the second table either way, because the row that would kill it kills a whole CLASS. *** THE ROWS OWED, AND THE THIRD IS THE GENERAL ONE: *** `interleave` -- fire the site, issue a VALID command (0xEC IDENTIFY), fire again, demand ONE line; this is what kills glm's escape. `w32`/`r32` -- 32-bit non-byte access at :607 and :1025, closing agy's width gap. **`fire3` -- fire ONE site THREE times and demand exactly one line**, which kills the toggle class and any period-N latch at once, and is the cheapest row of the three. `:145` remains DISCLOSED-NOT-COVERED in the contract; agy's point stands but the contract already says so. *** AND AN HONEST RECORD ABOUT PROCESS: THE CARRIER'S PRE-TABLE CHECK WOULD HAVE CAUGHT agy's FINDING AND I DID NOT RUN IT. *** Its FIRST item is SIBLING PARITY -- *for each arm the diff touches, compare its observable channels against its nearest sibling arm; an asymmetry is a row you owe*. `:145` with zero rows beside `:160` with two IS that asymmetry, visible by counting, before any mutant was built. The check exists precisely because a table re-run is expensive, and this round is now paying that cost: the first table was measured against a 14-row detector and adding rows changes other mutants' scores, so a SECOND table is owed. **The check costs three greps and about thirty minutes; skipping it cost a table.**  *** THE FULL PASS-2 PANEL (8 of 8 answering; the kimi CLI walled and COVERED BY ITS SUBSTITUTE -- panel.sh reported that correctly for the first time) FOUND AN L2 FLOOR BREACH, TWO WRONG RECORDS IN SHIPPED TEXT, AND A DESIGN CLAIM WITH NO ROW BEHIND IT. *** **(L2 -- FLOOR BREACH, clause (b), grok) `:145` HAS NO DETECTOR ROW, SO THE FIX HAS SIX FIXED SITES AND THE DETECTOR SEES FIVE.** Clause (b) requires failure on a verbatim reinstatement at EVERY fixed site; reinstating `:145`'s unlatched form escapes clean. grok's verdict was DO-NOT-SHIP-AS-A-SIX-SITE-FLOOR and it is right. **Confirmed by counting my own probe: the six site-x2 rows cover FIVE sites -- `P_OVERRUN` appears only in `disjoint`, which fires an unimplemented command and never touches the ring.** The 8/14 -> 14/14 colour change is five tested sites plus `unimpl 2op`. Fix in-round, however late: either add a `:145` row or narrow the fix to five sites. **(L4 -- WRONG RECORD IN SHIPPED PROBE TEXT, codex and grok independently) THE HEADER CONTRADICTS ITS OWN CONTRACT.** It claims to catch *removal of any of the six per-instance latch guards* while the contract eight lines below excludes `:145`. It catches FIVE unique sites. **(L4 -- `disjoint` PROVES LESS THAN ITS NAME, codex.** It exercises only the unimplemented-command transcript, so it proves THAT stimulus matches one regex -- not that all six patterns map uniquely. The row's note overstates it. *** (DESIGN CLAIM WITH NO ROW, codex -- and this is the one I would have missed for good.) 14/14 DOES NOT ENFORCE SIX INDEPENDENT FLAGS. *** Every site test starts a NEW emulator (15 `session()` calls), so **pairing the read/write width flags, or the two ring flags, would pass every isolated row -- as would a PROCESS-GLOBAL latch**, unless one session exercises two controller instances. The round's headline design decision -- six flags, not four -- is therefore UNMEASURED by its own detector. codex adds the mitigating half, checked: the implementation does use six separately initialised per-instance fields, `DEVINIT(wdc)` zeroes the whole allocation, and none is reset or aliased later. **So the CODE is right and the DETECTOR cannot prove it** -- which under clause (e) must be disclosed, not claimed. **(ESCAPE, codex) An inserted `return 1;` immediately after the non-byte-read `fatal()`** leaves the FIRST 16-bit read's buffer untouched while the second runs normally -- and `r16 x2` checks only the line count. Needs a VALUE oracle, not a count. **(ESCAPE, ollama_kimi) `idata = data[0]` at the write-width site is a FOURTH guest-visible side effect** and has no pin row; my Q2 list named three. *** THE EPOCH-LATCH QUESTION (ollama_kimi and minimax, independently) IS ANSWERED AND THE ANSWER IS NO. *** They asked whether a guest-reachable device reset re-zeroes the `warned_*` flags, which would make the shipped code an epoch latch no row can see. Checked by reading: the only `memset(d, 0, sizeof(struct wdc_data))` is at :1126, immediately after the malloc at :1125 in DEVINIT. `COMMAND_RESET` (:484) and `ATAPI_SOFT_RESET` (:601) exist and neither re-zeroes the struct. **A real question, cheaply settled, and worth recording as a negative result so nobody re-asks it.** **(RECORDS, minimax) MY OWN BRIEF'S ROW ARITHMETIC DID NOT ADD UP:** it enumerated 2 controls + 6 site + 3 accept + 1 disjoint = 12 against a stated 14. The two unstated rows are the side-effect rows `abrt 2nd` and `overread -1` -- which are the most interesting rows in the file and the brief left them out of its own census. **(CHALLENGE, minimax) `fatal()` RETURNING IS ASSUMED IN THE :1025 COMMENT, AND \"no early-out\" DOES NOT ESTABLISH IT** -- no-early-out is about verbosity, not program lifetime. The claim is true and this project settled it on 2026-08-21 by reading `core/debugmsg.c:384-390`, but the comment should CITE that rather than lean on the adjacent no-early-out fact. A fair catch. **(USEFUL FOR `wdcringambig`, ollama_kimi) A WASTE-ONE-SLOT DISAMBIGUATOR IS NOT FORBIDDEN BY CAPACITY:** 512 x 512 = 262,144 usable is still <= 262,655, so a max-length sector transfer still fits. Only the 513-undrained-IDENTIFY fill exceeds it. **Any future claim that \"waste-one breaks the exact-fit sizing\" is wrong**, and that is worth carrying to the ring round before it invents the objection.  *** KILL TABLE 1 -- 8 MUTANTS AGAINST THE 14-ROW DETECTOR, AND ONE ESCAPED. *** Preserved at `regress/artifacts/wdcflood/table1_14rows/` (12 files + a README stating what the numbers mean and why the directory is stamped). Tree restored and cmp-verified against SEC, sentinel cleared, zero live processes. SCORES, every reddened row re-extracted with an ANCHORED trailing-`FAIL` match: `base` 14/14 (identity -- the harness scores the unmutated copy exactly as SEC does); `Mb1` 12/14 delete the :583 guard -> `unimpl x2`, `unimpl 2op`; `M1` 12/14 null the repair, flag never set -> same two; `M2` 11/14 SET BEFORE ANNOUNCE, zero lines -> those two plus `disjoint`; `M3` 13/14 last-warned-opcode -> **`unimpl 2op` ALONE**; `M4` 13/14 ABRT moved inside the guard -> **`abrt 2nd` ALONE**; `M5` 13/14 `return -1` moved inside the guard -> **`overread -1` ALONE**; `M6` **14/14 -- ESCAPED, ZERO ROWS REDDENED**. *** THE THREE SINGLE-ROW KILLS ARE THE USEFUL RESULT, NOT THE SCORES. *** `M3`, `M4` and `M5` each redden exactly ONE row, and those three rows exist for exactly those escapes -- nothing else in the file covers them. `abrt 2nd` and `overread -1` are the two rows that read a guest-visible SIDE EFFECT rather than a line count, and they are the only rows that can see a repair swallowed by a guard. *** M2 IS THE MEASURED VINDICATION OF AN ORACLE CHOICE. *** It sets the flag before the test so the site emits ZERO lines. **A `<= 1 line` oracle would have passed it**; `exactly 1` catches it. That choice was argued from a seat's reasoning and is now measured. *** AND M6 IS THE ESCAPE, PREDICTED BY A SEAT ON PASS 2 AND CONFIRMED HERE. *** It swaps the latch operand for the GUEST-RESETTABLE `d->seccnt`: `if (len != 1 && !d->seccnt) { d->seccnt = 1; fatal(...); }`. Every row in table 1 fires its site TWICE IN SUCCESSION, so nothing between the fires ever puts the operand back and the mutant looks perfectly latched. A guest that writes `seccnt = 0` between wide writes floods without bound. **This is a STRUCTURAL blindness of the row design, not a missing mutant** -- the same shape as `#463`'s distinctness invariance, where rows that assert a property back-to-back cannot see an edit that changes only what happens BETWEEN the assertions. *** DETECTOR NOW 22 ROWS, MEASURED 22/22 ON THE FIXED BUILD (`table2_22rows/base_22rows.raw`). *** Seven rows added, each closing something table 1 or pass 2 exposed: `overrun x2` (the :145 row -- **this is what closes the L2 floor breach**, and it needed a hardware loop, `dt r3` / `bf` with a computed displacement, because 513 undrained IDENTIFYs unrolled would be 513 debugger commands); `interleave` (fire, VALID command, fire); `w16 reset` (fire, guest CLEARS the operand, fire -- the M6 killer); `fire3` (kills period-N latches, not just period-2); `w32 x2` and `r32 x2` (the width rows probed 16-bit only, so a guard narrowed to `len == 2` escaped); `r16 value` and `w16 value` (value oracles at the two width sites, for a `return` inserted after a latched `fatal()` -- which a count-only row cannot see). Row hygiene checked: no name over 13 chars, no duplicates, no name that is a prefix of another followed by a space. TABLE 2 is running against the 22-row detector with three mutants table 1 could not have had: `Mb2` removes the :145 guard verbatim (the clause-(b) reinstatement at the site that had no row), `M7` is the reset-on-normal-operation escape, `M8` is the period-2 latch. All three anchors were verified to occur EXACTLY ONCE in the source first -- **a mutant that fails to apply scores a false clean**, which is the worst possible failure for a kill table. *** A CAPTION DEFECT IN THE APPARATUS, FOUND AND FIXED AT SOURCE. *** `killwdc.sh` wrote its header at start with the row count and the pre-fix score as LITERALS (`14 rows`, `8/14`). The moment pass 2 forced seven more rows, table 2's own header became wrong about its own size. The script now DERIVES the count (`grep -c '^row(' ...`) and stamps a UTC start time instead of quoting scores it cannot know. Table 2's already-written header is stale and is corrected in place; the script fix stops it recurring. *A table that misreports its own size cannot be cited safely at all.*  *** KILL TABLE 2 IS CLEAN: 22-ROW DETECTOR, TEN MUTANTS, **ZERO ESCAPES**, EVERY LOG INTERNALLY CONSISTENT. *** Preserved at `regress/artifacts/wdcflood/table2_22rows/` with a header that DERIVES its row count rather than quoting one. Tree restored and cmp-verified against SEC, sentinel cleared, zero live processes. base 22/22; Mb1 18/22; M1 18/22; M2 17/22; M3 21/22; M4 21/22; M5 21/22; M6 21/22; M7 21/22; M8 21/22; Mb2 21/22. **Consistency check on all eleven logs: score + anchored-red count == 22 in every one**, which is the check that would catch a contaminated extractor. *** SIX ESCAPES, SIX SINGLE-ROW KILLS, EACH BY THE ROW BUILT FOR IT -- and that one-to-one correspondence is the result, not the scores: *** M3 last-warned-opcode -> `unimpl 2op` ALONE; M4 ABRT inside the guard -> `abrt 2nd` ALONE; M5 `return -1` inside the guard -> `overread -1` ALONE; M6 guest-resettable operand -> `w16 reset` ALONE; M7 reset-on-normal-operation -> `interleave` ALONE; Mb2 the :145 verbatim reinstatement -> `overrun x2` ALONE. **Nothing else in the file sees any of them.** *** THE L2 FLOOR BREACH IS CLOSED BY MEASUREMENT. *** `Mb2` is the exact edit that swept CLEAN against the 14-row detector -- removing :145's guard verbatim -- and it now reddens `overrun x2`, the row that did not exist when the breach was found. Clause (b) holds at all six fixed sites. *** M8 VINDICATES A CAUTION, AND THE CAUTION IS THE TRANSFERABLE PART. *** deepseek proposed `d->warned_X = !d->warned_X;` as a period-2 escape. I read its SPECIFIC edit as unworkable -- the assignment sits INSIDE the guard, so fire 2 never reaches it -- and recorded it as **\"a prediction to be MEASURED, not refuted\"**. M8 constructs the same class differently (toggle BEFORE the guard) and escapes a two-fire oracle exactly as deepseek said the class would. **Wrong about the placement, RIGHT ABOUT THE CLASS.** Declaring it refuted on the strength of a correct reading of one construction would have lost a real escape. *A seat can be wrong about the mechanism and right about the family; the family is the part worth building a row for.* *** CLAUSE (c), THE NULL MEDIUM, IS NOW MEASURED AND IN ITS STRONGEST FORM: 0/22 ON BOTH. *** `nullmedium_absent.raw` (a binary that does not exist) and `nullmedium_notanemu.raw` (a real executable that is not an emulator) each score **0/22 -- every row fails, not merely some**. It had NOT been verified before; the question was raised against the round's own readiness and answered in seconds. **AND THE MEASUREMENT EXPOSED A DESIGN PROPERTY THAT WAS ACCIDENTAL RATHER THAN INTENDED:** the three accept rows expect ZERO diagnostics, so a naive implementation would PASS against silence -- zero is exactly what silence produces. They fail correctly only because `n()` returns `\"died\"` rather than `\"0\"` when the run produced nothing (probe :227-228). **That single line is what stops every accept row in this file from being vacuous under clause (c)**, and it was not written for that reason. Worth carrying to any future detector with absence-shaped oracles: an \"expect 0\" row MUST distinguish 'ran and saw nothing' from 'did not run'.  *** THE READINESS ADJUDICATION RETURNED **HOLD-FOR** AND FOUND TWO SHIPPED ROWS VACUOUS. ASKING BEFORE THE TABLE FINISHED IS WHAT MADE THAT POSSIBLE -- a clean sweep invites ratification. *** **`r16 value` WAS VACUOUS AS BUILT.** Its oracle was `printed(t,\"r2\") != \"absent\"` against a wanted `\"read\"`, so it passed whenever the debugger echoed ANY hex value -- structurally incapable of failing while the emulator lived. **Confirmed: green under every one of table 2's ten mutants -- it did not exist for table 1, which measured a 14-row detector, and an earlier note said 'tables 1 and 2', which is false.** The `live` control already covered session death, so the row measured nothing at all. **AND BOTH WIDTH VALUE ROWS CHECKED THE WRONG FIRE.** The escape they cite -- a `return` inserted after a latched `fatal()` -- corrupts the FIRST access (flag unset, guard entered, return skips the repair); the SECOND finds the flag set, skips the guard and repairs normally. Both rows read the value ONCE, after both fires, and so saw a perfectly good value. The contract line claiming they carried value oracles \"for exactly that edit\" was FALSE AS BUILT. Repaired to check BOTH fires, with a low-byte mask because the repair sets only `data[0]` and asserting the upper byte would make the row about the wrong thing. *** TABLE 3 EARNS THE REPAIRED ROWS THEIR MEASURED KILL, WHICH IS WHAT A REPAIRED ROW OWES: *** `table3_repaired/`, base 22/22. `Mv1` (delete `idata = data[0]`, the write-side repair) 18/22 -> r16 value, w16 value, overrun x2, accept ec. `Mv2` (delete `data[0] = odata`, the read-side repair inside the `ret:` tail) 18/22 -> devsig, abrt 2nd, r16 value, w16 value. **`Mv3` (codex's EXACT edit -- insert `return 1;` after the latched READ `fatal()`) 21/22 -> `r16 value` ALONE.** *** Mv3 IS THE ONE THAT PROVES THE REPAIR RATHER THAN THE ROW. *** It corrupts only the first access, so **the ORIGINAL one-value form would have scored 22/22 against it** -- the escape would have shipped invisibly behind a row whose whole stated purpose was to catch it. One row in twenty-two sees it, and it is the repaired one. COLLATERAL WORTH READING RATHER THAN DISMISSING: `Mv2` reddens `devsig`, because deleting `data[0] = odata` breaks the status read the device-signature control depends on. That is the control doing its job -- a device that cannot answer through its own decode is exactly what `devsig` exists to notice -- not a spurious red. Tree restored and cmp-verified against SEC, sentinel cleared, zero live processes. All three logs internally consistent (score + anchored reds == 22). *** THE STANDING LESSON, and it is the second time in one round: A REPAIRED ROW IS NOT A ROW UNTIL A MUTANT KILLS IT. *** Both rows looked correct after repair and one of them still could not have caught the edit it was written for. Reading a row and believing it is exactly the state the vacuity floor exists to refuse.\n\n*** codex (rating pass, 2026-09-07) reports this as `low residual -- FIXED`, and I VERIFIED the\nlatch -- but this row is `in work` with its own commit, so I am NOT proposing closure. ***\n\n**Verified:** `dev_wdc.c:1065` is `if (!d->warned_unimpl_cmd) {` around the `fatal()`, with\n`d->error |= WDCE_ABRT;` and `d->int_assert = 1;` deliberately OUTSIDE it at `:1063-1064` -- the\nplacement `#464` (`baacf9b`) and `#485` (`d819188`) established, and the comment at `:1060-1062`\nrecords why.\n\n**Why not closed:** an `in work` row with a commit may carry an outstanding obligation that a\nsource reading cannot see, and this file's sibling row [[wdcperopcode]] is the residual of exactly\nthis fix.  **The closure question goes to the adjudication with the evidence assembled, not to me.**", "witness": "probe:regress/artifacts/wdcflood/witness_20260825/ (rung 3, MEASURED 2026-08-25 by opus5) -- real SH4 guest instructions through real decode and real memory_rw on the UNMODIFIED committed `-E landisk` description (machine_landisk.c:87-90, addr_mult=2, wd_command at physical 0x1400000e). Both admissibility controls present: LIVENESS (implemented command 0xec yields ZERO lines, so the rig can produce zero) and DEVICE-SIGNATURE (storing 0x5a echoes `command 0x5a` x20 and `command 0xaa` x0 -- the guest's own byte, which RAM cannot produce). No device was added to reach it."}
LATEST ENTRY [{"seat": "opus5", "phase": "review", "d": "OK", "date": "2026-08-27", "ev": "regress/artifacts/wdcfloodstale/reaim_20260827/", "grade": "CONFIRMED", "note": "*** #486 REPAIRED THIS ROUND'S OWN CLAUSE-(b) CARRIER, WHICH HAD BEEN NON-DISCRIMINATING. ***\n\n`overrun x2` is #464's sole clause-(b) carrier at the ring producer.  MEASURED on the\ncommitted binary before #486: it reads OVERRUN = 0 on the shipped latch AND 0 on a build with\nthe latch DELETED.  Not merely red -- it could not tell the fix from its absence, so #464's\nfloor was open at that site with nothing saying so.\n\n#479 killed it: an unconditional `wdc_new_command_reset()` capped per-command occupancy at\n512, so 1026 IDENTIFY commands could no longer lap a 262,656-byte ring.  Proven by a\nsingle-variable control -- revert only that reset, keep #484's ring, and OVERRUN goes 0 -> 1.\n\n#486 re-aimed the stimulus at the guest data port, which takes no command and therefore no\nreset.  Now: shipped 1,1,0 ok; latch deleted 1,145,0 FAIL.  Probe 25/26 -> 26/26, with the\ngate's pinned row count unchanged at 26.\n\nThe commit is named here because work shipped for this row and a row that does not name its\ncommit cannot be traced back from the log -- the gate refused the next commit until it did."}]
{"id": "landiskdisk", "state": "in work", "commits": "b744299", "title": "landiskdisk  the landisk rig attaches NO DISK, so dev_wdc.c has zero rig coverage in the whole battery", "closure": {"by": "fable5", "badge": "", "note": "CLOSE (assess), and RANK IT UP"}, "state_note": "  *** COMMITS FIELD FILLED 2026-08-26: `b744299` shipped this row's work and the row did not name it, so section L hard-failed the NEXT commit -- correctly. The gate is doing exactly its job: it caught a records gap left by the previous round, not by this one. b744299 changed regress/drive_guest.py (+57, the landisk rig now attaches `wdc-scratch-64m.img` via `-d iR:` and prints RIG_DISK=1/0), regress/images.md (+41, the dd recipe, since the image is generated and not downloaded), and the ledger/matrix pair. The row stays `in work` rather than closing: the disk half shipped, and this row was split in two by adjudication -- see below. ***\n  *** SPLIT IN TWO BY ADJUDICATION 2026-08-25, AND HALF OF IT IS DONE. *** This row was ranked as a BLOCKER on four other rows' witnesses. It is really two deliverables and only one of them ever blocked anything. **HALF A -- the PROBE-level attach. NOT A BLOCKER, and it was already proven:** :583 is unreachable without a disk (dev_wdc.c:461-468 early-returns), so the opus5 flood measurement necessarily ran disk-attached. A witness battery can attach its own disk; it does not need the rig. **HALF B -- the BATTERY-RIG integration. SHIPPED 2026-08-25 and verified by a boot.** `_images/wdc-scratch-64m.img` is 67,108,864 bytes of zeros, generated not downloaded (recipe in `regress/images.md`). `drive_guest.py` gained an `optional_disk` key and appends `-d iR:<path>` when the file exists. **MEASURED on this host, one boot:** RIG_DISK=1, BOOT_REACHED=1, VERDICT=PASS, VALUES=SH7751R,42 (the token gate_crossfamily checks, unaffected), zero \"Unimplemented command\" warnings, **and the image md5 IDENTICAL before and after -- so `R:` is discarding guest writes as intended and repeated runs cannot inherit state.** **OPTIONAL BY DESIGN AND IT SAYS SO.** `_images/` is deliberately outside the repository, so on a fresh clone the file is absent; the rig then boots exactly as before and prints `RIG_DISK=0 absent:<path>`. That report is the point -- a silent revert to zero wdc coverage is indistinguishable from coverage that ran, which is this project's silent-coverage-loss class. **THE NINSTRS POISON IS THE PART THAT STILL MATTERS AND IT IS UNCHANGED:** the disked boot retires 28-34% fewer instructions in the same wall time. Harmless today only because drive_guest.py sets \"budget\": 0 for landisk. A NINSTRS floor is FILED for this rig, and a floor derived from a diskless boot and applied to a disked one is a false-FAIL waiting to happen -- whoever calibrates it must calibrate the DISKED config. Carried into the code comment and into images.md so it cannot be lost. **SCOPE IT HONESTLY: this gives ZERO coverage of wdc_command()'s `default:` arm**, which is where every filed wdc defect lives. Its real value is 0xF5 SEC_FREEZE_LOCK as `wdcnoirq`'s witness plus general wdc exercise."}
LATEST ENTRY [{"seat": "opus5", "phase": "review", "d": "\u2713", "date": "2026-08-20", "note": "*** THE '10 REAL OPCODES' FIGURE IS MEASURED FALSE, AND THE CORRECTION REMOVES THIS ROW'S STATED VALUE. ***  Booted with the exact rig args plus a disk: 14 command-register writes across SEVEN opcodes to the installer prompt --\n    3x 0xEC IDENTIFY  3x 0x20 READ  2x 0xC6 SETMULTI  2x 0x91 IDP\n    2x 0x10 RECAL     1x 0xF5 SEC_FREEZE_LOCK        1x 0xC4 READMULTI\n    \"Unimplemented command\" warnings: 0\nNo 0xE0, no 0x30 WRITE, no 0xC5 WRITEMULTI -- those come from a full filesystem lifecycle through halt -p, which this rig never performs. Grok predicted this from reading; it is now measured. *** CONSEQUENCE: adding -d i: gives ZERO coverage of the default: arm -- the arm all four defect rows live in. ***  Its real value is as [[wdcnoirq]]'s rung-4 witness via 0xF5, which is a different and smaller claim.\n\nSAFE TO ADD -- measured, fixed 25 s windows, same host and binary:\n    no disk         prompt_reached=1  instrs=2,852,727,965\n    iR: 64MB blank  prompt_reached=1  instrs=1,879,394,907\n    no disk    (2)  prompt_reached=1  instrs=2,382,496,189\n    iR: 64MB   (2)  prompt_reached=1  instrs=1,711,379,982\nBoth reach the installer menu; wd0 attaches and is probed; gate_crossfamily.sh's SH7751R,42 token is unaffected; the installer waits rather than auto-installing.\n\n*** TWO TRAPS. (1) SYNTAX: `-d i:R:<path>` ABORTS *** (diskimage_add(): invalid prefix char -- the parser at diskimage.c:1700-1755 takes all prefix chars before a SINGLE colon). The correct form is `-d iR:<path>`. Getting this wrong is exactly the 'latently red for two commits' shape. *** (2) THE DISK CONFIG RETIRES 28-34% FEWER INSTRUCTIONS IN THE SAME WALL TIME. ***  Harmless today only because drive_guest.py:84 sets \"budget\": 0 -- landisk deliberately has no instruction ceiling. But its own comment says a NINSTRS floor is FILED, and *** a floor derived from today's diskless boot and applied to a disked one is a false-FAIL waiting to happen. ***  Also: the base image must be R:-prefixed or the guest writes the shared rig image, and the installer prompt has NO TRAILING NEWLINE, which is why the rig needs drive_guest.py's pty predicate rather than line-oriented matching.", "grade": "CONFIRMED", "ev": "landisk boots with and without -d iR:, 4 fixed-window runs"}]
{"id": "pitclobber", "title": "pitclobber  An 8253 LATCH command clobbers the counter's programmed RW format and counting mode, which a real 8254 never does", "state": "in work", "commits": "020a672", "hold": "FIX SHIPPED (#439, 020a672); CLOSURE HELD FOR TWO REASONS, and the second is the substantive one. (1) The flagship review of the diff is held by owner allocation. (2) *** PASS 2 FOUND A REAL DEFECT THAT IS NOT YET FIXED: the latch is never CONSUMED, so after one latch the counter returns 0 indefinitely. *** Handed to the in-flight pitflip round because the consume semantics interact with the LSB/MSB flip-flop that round is fixing. Closing now would record as finished a round whose own pass 2 found it incomplete.", "closure": {"by": "fable5", "badge": "", "note": "CLOSE (regress and review)"}, "witness": "none needed -- the defect was fixed by `#439` (`020a672`), which also shipped `regress/pit8253_latch_probe.py` (308 lines) as its detector.", "state_note": "*** EVIDENCE 2026-08-27: THE DEFECT WAS REAL AND IS ALREADY FIXED. The row has been open describing work that shipped. Recommend CLOSE. ***\n\nA farmed seat returned FALSE with a specific citation, and it was verified here rather than taken on trust. **Commit `020a672` -- *#439 the 8253 counter-latch: the driving seat REFUSED the panel's design and measured why* -- touched `src/devices/dev_8253.c` (+38) and added `regress/pit8253_latch_probe.py` (308 lines).**\n\nThe guard is at `dev_8253.c:303-308` and its own comment states exactly what the row claims is broken, in the past tense:\n  > *\"The counter-latch command (RW == 00) latches a counter so it can be read; it does NOT reprogram it, so the RW format in mode_byte and the counting mode in mode[] have to survive it. **Writing idata through here unconditionally used to destroy both.**\"*\n\nThe latch arm now sets `latched[]` and `break`s before reaching the `mode_byte = idata` / `mode[] = idata & 0x0e` writes that did the damage.\n\n**SECOND INSTANCE TODAY of an open row describing a fixed defect** -- see [[smnotland]], fixed the same day it was assessed. Two instances make this a CLASS, not an accident; it is recorded on [[baretitle]].\n\n\n\n*** CLOSURE PROPOSED, NOT MADE.  Verified by me against the source; held for the 2026-09-09\nadjudication because a row closed on one seat's reading plus my own agreement is exactly the\nself-review the conflict-of-interest badge exists to disclose. ***\n**codex (rating pass, 2026-09-07): `low residual -- FIXED`.**  *\"`dev_8253.c` handles\n`I8253_TIMER_LATCH` with a `break;` before `d->mode_byte = idata;` and the counting-mode\nassignment.\"*\n\n**VERIFIED BY READING:** `:303-308` is `if ((idata & 0x30) == I8253_TIMER_LATCH) { ...; break; }` and\n`:310` is `d->mode_byte = idata;`, so the latch command can no longer reach the reprogramming\nstore.  `#439`'s own comment at `:298-302` states the intent.\n\n**AND THE ROW ALREADY SAID SO.**  Its `witness` field reads *\"none needed -- the defect was fixed by\n`#439` (`020a672`), which also shipped `regress/pit8253_latch_probe.py` (308 lines) as its\ndetector.\"*  So this is not a discovery: **the round recorded its own fix and the row was left\n`in work` anyway.**  That is the `lunaabsentcpu` shape -- fixed, detected, and never closed -- and it\nis now the second instance today, which makes it a class rather than an accident.\n\n*Carrying row named, per the closure conditions: `R1 latch preserves programming` in\n`regress/pit8253_latch_probe.py`, which compares the programming before and after a latch.*"}
LATEST ENTRY [{"seat": "opus5", "phase": "review", "d": "\u2713", "date": "2026-08-20", "note": "*** WITNESSED NOW. The row's own entry said 'Not witnessed by this round's probe'; that is no longer true. ***  MEASURED, arm X, and by the most standard PC PIT initialisation there is:\n    CW 0x34 -> counter 0 = 0x2e9c\n    CW 0x54  (counter 1, LSB-only -- the classic DRAM-refresh value)\n    write counter 1\n    read counter 0 as a pair  ->  *** 0x9c9c, the low byte twice ***\nIDENTICAL ON HEAD AND #440, so NOT a regression -- but it is the same 'one byte twice' symptom #440's headline says it fixes, reachable with no guest misbehaviour at all, and #440's per-counter flip-flops now sit underneath a GLOBAL format byte, which is the inconsistency.\n\nCODEX INDEPENDENTLY REACHED THE SAME DEFECT BY READING and called it a blocker for #440 (VERDICT: DO NOT SHIP). It is not a blocker -- it is pre-existing, and the reopening rule bars it from that round -- but two seats finding it in one pass, one by measurement and one by reading, is the strongest signal this row has ever carried. Kimi reached the pre-existing-not-blocking conclusion too.\n\nThe fix is bigger than it looks: the format needs per-counter storage used by the resolver, the latch-release test, the toggle test and every other format-dependent path -- not just one more array.", "grade": "CONFIRMED", "ev": "_scratchpad/panel_20260820_183311/ (7/7 scriptable) + opus5 measuring seat"}]
{"id": "gdromdmawidth", "title": "gdromdmawidth  the DMA tail store ignores `len`: an ALIGNED byte or 16-bit write to an implemented offset clobbers all 32 bits of the shadow register", "state": "open", "commits": "029a137", "hold": "", "witness": "none committed -- MEASURED 2026-08-23 at rung 3 via a scratchpad probe (reported, not pinned): the row is DELIBERATELY not shipped, because pinning 0x000000ff would enshrine this model's own behaviour as an oracle", "closure": {"by": "", "badge": "", "note": ""}, "state_note": "*** A FARMED agy JOB RE-DERIVED THIS ROW INDEPENDENTLY 2026-08-26, AND ITS RECOMMENDATION WOULD HAVE SHIPPED A DEFECT. Recorded because the failure mode is instructive, not because the seat was careless. ***\n\n**What it got RIGHT, and it is a clean confirmation:** the tail store is `d->dma_reg[relative_addr / sizeof(uint32_t)] = idata;` with **no `len`**, `idata` arrives zero-extended from `memory_readmax64(cpu, data, len)`, an aligned narrow write to an IMPLEMENTED offset therefore widens to 32 bits, and it is directly guest-reachable. It gave a correct three-way oracle table (defect `0x000000ff` / merged `0x112233ff` / declined `0x11223344`) and named both controls.\n\n**What it MISSED is what the source already says at the store itself, and it is decisive:**\n  1. **THE ORACLE IS ABSENT.** `0x005f74xx` is Sega's Holly ASIC, outside the SH7750 manual, and this project holds NO documentation for it. *'Real hardware preserves the upper bits'* would be an ORACLE FROM PLAUSIBILITY -- the `#357`/`#362` shape this tree has already been burned by once.\n  2. *** MAINLINE LINUX CONSTRAINS ANY FIX, and the comment records that this is the finding which KILLED THE PANEL'S UNANIMOUS ANSWER. *** `drivers/cdrom/gdrom.c` BYTE-WRITES `+0x18` to trigger every DMA transfer and BYTE-READS it to poll for completion. **So the width-blind code delivers exactly what the one real narrow user needs, and a blanket 'decline narrow writes' would BREAK A BOOTING LINUX.** The agy answer cited that same driver as EVIDENCE FOR the defect while missing that it is the CONSTRAINT AGAINST the obvious fix.\n  3. **The detector has a trap already named:** the discriminating row must NOT ship pinning `0x000000ff`, because that would enshrine this model's own behaviour as an oracle.\n\n*** AND THE PROVENANCE CLAIM WAS WRONG IN A WAY WORTH RECORDING: the seat labelled its oracle table MEASURED. *** Its numbers -- `0x000000ff`, liveness `0x12345678` -- are the EXACT values recorded in the source comment, which documents a real earlier measurement. **The numbers are true; the seat did not produce them.** That is the reading-vs-measurement distinction this project draws, and it matters because a seat quoting the tree's own measurement back looks exactly like independent confirmation.\n\n**DISPOSITION UNCHANGED: assessed-not-changed.** The decided design, if a fix is ever warranted, is already written at the store: decline with a latched diagnostic at the four VALUE registers ONLY, keeping `+0x18` byte-capable. The farmed job confirmed the reading and did not advance the row.\n\n*Worth carrying about the farm generally: a seat given a row whose analysis is already COMPLETE IN THE SOURCE will re-derive it and can reach a WORSE conclusion, because the constraints live in the comment rather than in the code it reads. Brief a seat with the existing record, or ask it a question the record does not already answer.*\n\nASSESSED-NOT-CHANGED under Option C. Code ships under this row's name at ba30e64's successor -- a documenting comment at the tail store -- but the defect is deliberately unfixed, so the row stays open. Gated behind sh4gdromexit by PROBE-BUILDABILITY, not priority: the row that would pin the real contract is unbuildable until that round removes the exit(1) kills at +0x18.", "note": "  *** CITATIONS RE-DERIVED 2026-08-31 AND TWO ARE STALE. ***  This row cites `dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c:515` and `:889`; today those lines are bare comment continuation (`*` and `*  Mount:`) and hold no code at all.  The construct the row is about -- the DMA tail store that ignores `len` -- now sits beside the comment *\"THEN the tail stores it into dma_reg[1].  The same alias exists at\"*.  **DURABLE ANCHOR, replacing both numbers: `grep -n 'tail stores it into dma_reg' src/devices/dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c`.**  A grep anchor that matches nothing is LOUD; a drifted line number is silent and still looks like a citation.  *The file was committed five times after this note was written, which is exactly the window the citation-form rule describes: a same-file line number is invalidated by the round's own later edits, and nobody re-checks an example once it is written down.*"}
LATEST ENTRY [{"seat": "fable5", "phase": "assess", "d": "\u2713", "date": "2026-08-23", "note": "*** VERDICT: OPTION C, OVERTURNING A UNANIMOUS 7-OF-7 PANEL FROM SOURCE. *** The panel converged on Option B (decline narrow writes). This seat fetched mainline Linux drivers/cdrom/gdrom.c and read it line by line: :622 `__raw_writeb(1, GDROM_DMA_STATUS_REG)` is the DMA TRIGGER -- a BYTE WRITE to this window's +0x18 on every transfer -- and :618 byte-READS the same offset to poll for completion, while the four value registers at :585-589 are always __raw_writel. *** SO OPTION B WOULD BREAK A BOOTING LINUX, *** and the width-blind code delivers exactly the semantics the one real narrow user needs. The defect's actual content -- clobbering a value register's upper bits -- sits in cells no known guest reaches. It also checked NetBSD (never touches the DMA window) and promemul/dreamcast.c (zero references), and was explicit that KallistiOS widths are BELIEVED only. *** AND IT REFUTED OPTION A ON A DIFFERENT AXIS THAN THE BRIEF EXPECTED. *** The brief called endianness the real technical risk; the seat showed it is DERIVABLE (cpu_sh.c:96 sets EMUL_LITTLE_ENDIAN UNCONDITIONALLY, so there is no big-endian SH in this tree and the merge is always the low lane -- the brief's 'SH-4 can run either endianness' is true of silicon and FALSE of this tree, verified independently). The real objection is an ABSENT ORACLE: 0x005f74xx is Sega's Holly ASIC, outside the SH7750 manual, and this project holds NO documentation for it -- so the filing's own 'real hardware leaves the upper 24 bits' was an UNSOURCED CLAIM, the #357/#362 shape. A would ship an invented behaviour and pin the invention with a detector row. ON PRECEDENT it corrected the panel again: the BSC16 idiom (dev_sh4.c:1620-1680) is the direct shape-match, but *** IT TRANSFERS BY ITS WARRANT, NOT ITS SHAPE *** -- its warrant is 'a real OpenBSD/landisk boot makes ZERO non-16-bit BSC accesses (measured), so the latch costs a correct guest nothing', and that warrant FAILS here. Decided design recorded as B': decline at the four VALUE registers only, keep +0x18 byte-capable. It also flagged that the row pinning the REAL contract is currently UNBUILDABLE -- any nonzero store at +0x18 hits one of sh4gdromexit's filed exit(1) kills -- so this item is gated behind that round by probe-buildability rather than by ranking."}]
{"id": "probetimebased", "title": "probetimebased  pty probes with NO prompt detection: 15 live (11 grandfathered + 4 that arrived unnoticed), and gate_hygiene's ratchet was pinned at 11", "state": "open", "commits": "abf665c", "hold": "full panel OWED. This row was REFRAMED 2026-08-24 from a speed defect to a SOUNDNESS defect by one seat (five rows score `survived` as their PASS value, and `alive` is true for a HUNG emulator). Recording that single finding is what made the stage gate expect a full panel -- the row had none before, because it had never been worked. HELD rather than dropping the finding or firing a sixth panel today: the finding is worth more than the row's tidiness, and the sibling row probeslow just had its own nine-seat panel whose seats unanimously said to fold probeslow INTO this row. THE MERGE AND THE PANEL ARE ONE JOB; do them together rather than panelling a row that is about to absorb another.", "witness": "harness:RE-DERIVED 2026-08-31 by replicating `gate_hygiene.sh`'s OWN computation rather than approximating it -- `_ptylist` (find, minus artifacts/, grep -l pty.fork, basenames), then `cd $HERE` and `xargs grep -L 'endswith('`.  Result: denominator 41, promptless **15**, grandfathered **11**.  `check \"readiness: promptless probes, grandfathered set only\" 15 11` is RED.", "closure": {"by": "", "badge": "", "note": ""}, "state_note": "  *** THE FILED COUNT OF `FIVE ROWS` IS WRONG: IT IS NINE OF 57, AND SIX OF THE NINE PREDATE #462. Measured 2026-08-24 from the archived log `_scratchpad/kill462/probe_M2.raw`. *** The nine whose got-column is the pure-liveness form (`'survived' if alive else 'died'`, want `survived`) are: rd lo, wr lo, rd hi, rd w16, scan, dma rd, arm survive, nodata live, nodata stale. **So the reframing is NOT confined to the three #462 rows the vacuity-floor carve-out blessed** -- six ordinary read rows in the pre-existing detector assert nothing except that the process did not exit, and under this row's finding that predicate is TRUE OF A HUNG EMULATOR. *** THE DERIVATION IS RECORDED BECAUSE I GOT IT WRONG TWICE FIRST, in both of the directions the carrier warns about. *** (1) Counting `row()` CALL SITES through `ast` gave `6 of 52` -- but CALL SITES ARE NOT ROWS: the probe emits 58 from 52 literal-label sites because three sites sit inside loops, and the site at :263 has a COMPUTED label, which is exactly why four of the nine (rd lo, wr lo, rd hi, rd w16) cannot be found by grepping for `row(\"name\"`. (2) Anchoring the got-column at offset 14 instead of 15 -- the `%-14s` field is followed by a separator space -- returned `0 of 57`, a clean, confident PHANTOM ABSENCE that would have been read as `no row uses this form`. Both were caught only by carrying the DENOMINATOR: `0 of 57` and `0 of 0` are different claims and only one is evidence.  *** AND THE SECOND HALF OF THIS ROW'S CLAIM MEASURES AS SEVEN, NOT SIX, of 33. *** The filing says `six pty probes have NO prompt detection at all`. Counted 2026-08-24 with one pass -- `grep -l pty.fork *.py` then `xargs grep -L 'endswith('` -- **33 pty-forking probes, 7 with zero occurrences**: drive_guest.py, gdrom_data_probe.py, gdrom_default_probe.py, sh_intprio_probe.py, sh_iprbcd_probe.py, sh_pclock_probe.py, sh_tlbmulti_probe.py. The CLOSED-WORLD mechanism is confirmed by reading `gate_hygiene.sh:97-99`: `py_code()` pipes every file through `xargs -0 grep -h \"endswith(\"`, so a file containing ZERO occurrences contributes to no counter and the ratchet passes. **A probe with no prompt detection is invisible to the ratchet built to catch probes with BAD prompt detection.**  *** AND THE `absent` PATH WENT FROM READING TO MEASUREMENT WITHIN THE HOUR, IN THIS ROUND'S OWN THIRD TABLE. *** The adjudicating seat wrote that printed() returning `absent` would redden the idle rows under a hang, but offered it as `a reading, not a measurement -- no absent-red appears anywhere in the preserved kill map`. Verified: 0 of the 14 table-2 logs contain one. **Table 3's M11 run contains one.** The comparison, same mutant, two runs: TABLE 2 -- `nodata stale` reads `died`, host TERMINATED, M11 scores 54/57 with three reds. TABLE 3 -- **all NINE liveness rows read `survived`**, so the host never terminated, and M11 scores 56/58 with two reds: `stale says` at `0` against a wanted `1`, and `stale idle` at **`absent`** against `0x0`. *** SO M11'S OBSERVABLE FAILURE IS NOT STABLE ACROSS RUNS -- a NULL-pointer read of 408 bytes faults on one run and does not on another -- AND ON THE RUN WHERE IT DID NOT FAULT, NOT ONE LIVENESS ROW REDDENED. *** Had `stale says` and `stale idle` not existed, M11 would have scored 58/58 and ESCAPED. That is a direct, measured demonstration of why the not-EOF predicate's blind spot matters, and it arrived inside the very round that was arguing about it. **HONEST LIMIT: `absent` with the process alive is CONSISTENT WITH a hang, and that reading is not yet established** -- it is equally consistent with the session simply outrunning its spin budget. Distinguishing them is exactly what the proposed three-state predicate (terminated / responsive / unresponsive) would do, and it is now the strongest argument for building it. *This also strengthens the case that the DETECTOR is sound while the PREDICATE is not: the value rows caught what the liveness rows could not see.*  *** THE `absent` CENSUS, WITH DENOMINATORS, AND A CONTROL THAT RULES OUT A FLAKY RIG. *** Across every preserved log: table 1 **0 of 10**, table 2 **0 of 14**, table 3 **1 of 4 completed runs** -- so one firing in 28 mutant runs, and it is M11. *** THE CONTROL IS M10, MEASURED IN THE SAME TWO TABLES: its reds are BYTE-IDENTICAL across them -- `arm count2` and `arm enable2` both times, 55/57 then 56/58. *** So the instability is specific to M11's NULL-source `memory_rw` fault path and is NOT a general property of the rig, the probe, or the host under load. **That distinction is the whole value of the measurement**: without it, `M11's mode changed` is indistinguishable from `this rig is flaky`, and the second reading would discredit every score in all three tables rather than one cell in one of them.  *** THE STALL DIAGNOSIS WAS CONFIRMED BY INTERVENTION, NOT INFERRED FROM A CONTRAST -- 125 BYTES -> 24,211 BYTES, SAME SEAT, SAME PANEL, SAME EVENING, ONE VARIABLE. *** minimax stalled at 125 bytes on a brief with ZERO inlined code that said `Read gdrom_default_probe.py's session()/rd()`. The brief was corrected -- `rd()` and the command loop inlined verbatim -- and the same seat was re-fired into the same panel: **24,211 bytes, a 194x difference.** The carrier's inline-every-fact rule has been stated for months from anecdote; this is the controlled version, and the counterfactual is measured rather than argued. *** AND THE SEAT THAT `STALLED` IMMEDIATELY CORRECTED THIS ROW'S CENTRAL CLAIM. *** This row rests on `rd() returns False ONLY on EOF`. minimax, as soon as it could SEE the code: `this is NOT just EOF -- it could be EIO, EBADF, etc.` The `except OSError` arm catches considerably more than end-of-file, so `alive == False` means `the read raised OSError or returned empty`, not `the process exited`. **That sharpens the row rather than refuting it** -- the hang-blindness stands, since none of those conditions fires for a wedged-but-open pty -- but the premise as written is imprecise and a detector built on the literal wording would mis-describe what it observes. *The seat was never unable to contribute; it was unable to SEE.*  *** THE PANEL CONVERGED ON THE PREMISE, NOT THE CONCLUSION: THREE SEATS INDEPENDENTLY ISOLATED `rd() RETURNS FALSE ONLY ON EOF` AS THE SINGLE LOAD-BEARING AND UNVERIFIED CLAIM -- and one of them showed it is WRONG AS WRITTEN. *** minimax: `rd() claim is wrong` (`except OSError` catches EIO, EBADF). kimi: real `only if` that claim holds. glm: `rests on a single unverified load-bearing premise`. **That is a panel doing the job a panel is for -- not voting on the conclusion, but finding the one sentence everything else hangs from.** deepseek then gave the corrected form its sharpest statement: **`alive` is a NON-EXIT predicate, not a liveness predicate** -- which says what it DOES observe, where the row's own wording said only what it fails to observe. The conclusion survives (a wedged-but-open pty raises no OSError and returns no EOF, so the hang-blindness stands) but the row must be restated in deepseek's terms before it is acted on.  *** THE ROUND'S SHAPE IS NOW ADJUDICATED: the cheap fix is a SESSION-TAIL SENTINEL, not a per-command change, and the reason is the corrected premise. *** Because `alive` is a NON-EXIT predicate, the cheapest way to add a liveness reading is ONE EXTRA NON-EXIT CHECK AT THE END -- not re-timing the reads that already work. Appending it leaves every scored row's window untouched, so it re-measures nothing; codex's positive-completion detection is the eventual full conversion and carries a fresh matrix. **D1 explains the SHAPE of D4, and D2/D3 keep the expensive forms out of this round.**  *** THE CENSUS NUMERATOR WAS RIGHT AND MY DERIVATION OF IT WAS WRONG IN BOTH HALVES -- corrected 2026-08-25 after a measuring seat re-derived it and I re-checked with `ast`. *** I wrote that the count is `9 of 57` because `three sites sit inside loops, and one site at :263 has a COMPUTED label`. Measured: **EXACTLY TWO** `row()` sites are inside a loop, and **THOSE SAME TWO** carry the computed labels; `:263` is a `put h` format string inside `run()` and emits NO ROW AT ALL. The arithmetic that actually reaches the total is `52 call sites - 2 in-loop + 2 x 4 iterations = 58`. *** AND THE DENOMINATOR WAS STALE: it is 9 of **58**, not 57 -- the shipped probe emits 58 (`GDROM_DEF_RESULT=58/58`). *** So the row said `9 of 57` with a derivation that reaches neither 57 nor 58. **This is the right-conclusion-wrong-reason class the carrier warns gets REUSED**, and it is the second instance in this round: `safe 4`'s warrant claimed `& 1` kills +0x04 when under `& 1` nothing dies. *A reader re-deriving from my sentence does not reach 58, which is the whole test of whether a stated reason is load-bearing or decorative.* **AND THE MORE USEFUL CENSUS IS A DIFFERENT ONE: 13 of 58**, not 9 -- `want == 'survived'` is the wrong predicate for hang-blindness. The right one is *which rows PASS on an EMPTY transcript with alive True*, which adds four zero-count rows (`accept8`, `dma accept`, `nodata notx`, `accept quiet`) whose PASS value is a count of 0, and a wedged emulator produces 0.  *** `hang-blind` IS THE WRONG WORD FOR THE DETECTOR AND THE RIGHT ONE FOR THE ROWS -- corrected by the adjudicating seat against its own earlier phrasing. *** 45 of 58 rows carry a positive oracle a silent session breaks (`printed()` returns `absent` -> RED), so a hang IS caught loudly by the detector -- it is **MISATTRIBUTED**, reading as value failures rather than as a hung session. Strictly better than blind, and the cost is DIAGNOSIS, not detection: a hang regression reddens the battery but the signature points at values. *** AND THE TWO SUPPOSEDLY-BLIND ROWS ARE IN DIFFERENT STATES, which the verify-across-all-tables caveat caught before the record could call both `genuinely blind`: *** `accept8` has NO recorded kill anywhere -- genuinely unproven coverage. `dma accept` **HAS a measured kill on record** -- `m_deldma18`, 31/32, reddening that row alone -- but it is a **#461** mutant, so it is absent from all three #462 tables (verified: 0 red across 40 mutant runs) **and its per-row log is in the `killartifacts` gap**, making it verifiable from PROSE ONLY. *That is this session's own archive defect blocking the verification of a claim, in the round that filed it and wrote the durability rule against it.* FILED as coverage debt, not owed: a row with no kill is UNPROVEN coverage, not a demonstrated false pass, so the stopping rule files it.  *** PARTIAL SHIP at `abf665c`, and the row STAYS OPEN: that commit corrected the probe's own CONTRACT (a wrong record about what `alive` observes) but did NOT fix the predicate. *** The distinction matters for anyone reading the row later: what shipped is the honest description -- `alive` means no EOF and no OSError were seen inside the sampled windows, it is True for a hung, a frozen AND an already-killed process, and a red `alive == died` cannot tell a clean exit from a pty error. What is still OWED is the fix: the session-tail sentinel giving three states (terminated / responsive / unresponsive), which re-measures nothing because it is APPENDED. *A row that ships a corrected record and keeps its defect open is the normal case, not an anomaly -- and naming the commit is what stops the correction from later being mistaken for the fix.*", "note": "  *** CITATIONS RE-DERIVED 2026-08-31 AND BOTH LOOK STALE. ***  This row cites `gate_hygiene.sh:97-98`; today those are comment lines about harness code being left in scope, not about prompt detection.  The subject -- promptless pty probes -- is discussed at the comment *\"TEN pty-forking promptless probes sat\"*, and note the COUNT there is TEN while this row's title says SIX.  **Two possibilities and I did not resolve them: either the row is scoped to a subset, or one of the two counts has drifted.**  Recorded as an open question rather than guessed, because picking one would be exactly the kind of confident reconciliation the wrong-tree and stale-citation rules both warn against.  **DURABLE ANCHOR: `grep -n 'promptless probes' regress/gate_hygiene.sh`.**  *ANCHOR CAVEAT, stated rather than glossed: `'promptless probes'` matches TWICE in `gate_hygiene.sh`, and the citation-form rule asks for VERIFIED-UNIQUE anchors.  Two hits still locate the discussion and both are in the same comment block, so it is usable -- but it is not unique, and saying so is the difference between an anchor that was checked and one that was asserted.  The other two anchors minted today match exactly once.*  *** THE HYGIENE GATE WILL GO RED ON THE NEXT WEEKLY BATTERY, AND IT WILL BE RIGHT. got=15 want=11. ***  Four pty-forking probes have no prompt detection and are not in `GRANDFATHERED_PROMPTLESS`:  `gdrom_cond_probe.py`, **`sgi_re_width_probe.py`**, `wdc_addtoinbuf_probe.py`, `wdc_altoffset_probe.py`.  *** ONE OF THEM IS #497's DETECTOR, SHIPPED BY THIS SESSION TODAY, AND THAT IS RECORDED HERE RATHER THAN LEFT FOR THE BATTERY TO REPORT AS A SURPRISE. ***  **They are genuinely promptless, not misclassified -- checked, because the gate's own comment warns that `grep -L 'endswith('` cannot see REGEX readiness and would produce false positives.**  All four carry `re.search`/`re.match`, so the warning applies; but in `sgi_re_width_probe.py` the regex PARSES a register value (`print\\s+%s\\s*\\n\\s*(0x...)`) while the wait is `while time.time() < end` around `select.select` -- a TIME-BASED wait with no prompt predicate.  That is this row's defect exactly.  **THE COUNT IN THIS ROW'S TITLE (SIX) IS STALE, AND SO IS THE TEN IN THE GATE'S COMMENT -- AND THEY WERE NEVER THE SAME POPULATION.**  The gate comment's TEN counts promptless probes *under `artifacts/`*, which the denominator deliberately excludes; today that population is **20**.  The live promptless count is 15.  So the two numbers differ because they always counted different sets, and both have since drifted.  *The gate's own comment anticipates precisely this: \"Re-derive rather than quote; if the two ever differ, that difference is the finding.\"*  *** MY FIRST DISPOSITION HERE SAID \"DO NOT GRANDFATHER THE FOUR\" AND THE GATE REFUTES IT IN CAPITALS: \"ADDING THEM TO THE GRANDFATHERED SET IS NOT A BYPASS, AND THE DISTINCTION IS THE WHOLE POINT OF THE ROW.  This list is not `probes we forgive`; it is `promptless probes we KNOW about`, and the check's job is to NAME any that appear unnoticed.\"  I read \"the reason written down rather than the count quietly raised\" as forbidding the raise; it forbids the QUIET raise. ***  A red row here is the mechanism working: four probes really do lack readiness, and one arrived today.  The repair is to give them a prompt predicate, which is a round of its own and not a rider on this one.  Letting the battery red honestly costs one red row; suppressing it costs the only check that notices promptless probes at all.  *** AND THERE WAS A SECOND RED ROW I HAD NOT SEEN: `EXPECT_PTY_PROBES=37` against a measured denominator of 41.  Both pins short by exactly four -- the same four -- which is the arithmetic that makes this ONE event rather than two. ***  **A failed gate is a failed battery, and the next one was ~17 hours out.**  Both pins raised WITH PER-MEMBER REASONS, and the four do not share one: `wdc_addtoinbuf_probe.py` and `wdc_altoffset_probe.py` are breakpoint-driven -- promptless for exactly the reason the existing members are, and the first says so in its own comment -- while `gdrom_cond_probe.py` and `sgi_re_width_probe.py` are TIME-BASED DRAINS with no prompt predicate at all, which is a WEAKER reason and is recorded as such rather than folded in.  *The second group is this row's live defect; listing them does not forgive them, it makes them tracked instead of invisible.*  **CONTROLLED BOTH WAYS before commit:** with the four added both rows are GREEN (41/41, 15/15); dropping any ONE reds the promptless row at got=15 want=14, so the check still fires rather than having been switched off.  *And `sgi_re_width_probe.py` was added by #497 -- this session -- so the pin was raised by the round that broke it.  Disclosed in the gate comment rather than absorbed.*  *TITLE CORRECTED 2026-08-31.  It read \"six pty probes have NO prompt detection at all\"; re-derived with the gate's OWN computation the live count is **15** -- 11 grandfathered plus 4 that had arrived since the pin was set.  The old figure named neither its unit nor its method and could not be checked, which is exactly the class the counts rule exists for.  The gate comment's competing \"TEN\" was never the same population: it counts promptless probes under `artifacts/`, which the denominator deliberately excludes, and that population is 20 today.*"}
LATEST ENTRY [{"date": "2026-08-25", "seat": "opus5", "phase": "assess", "note": "PANEL panel_20260825_000248 -- IT MEASURED, and the result overturns a ruling I had recorded as settled. *** THE PREDICATE IS WORSE THAN HANG-BLIND: `alive == True` FOR A PROCESS THAT HAS ALREADY BEEN SIGKILLED. *** `rd()`'s loop is `while time.time() < end:`, so a death landing AFTER the last budget check is never read. `alive` does not mean `hung but not exited` -- **it does not even mean `not exited`.** Four measured branches on a rig built from the SHIPPED session()/rd() (slice sha256 recorded), with controls in both directions: untouched -> alive True, row ok; SIGKILL at t+2 -> alive False, row FAIL (so the rig CAN go red -- not a stuck-green artefact); guest `bra .` with NO SIGNALS AT ALL -> alive True, row GREEN; SIGSTOP -> alive True, row GREEN. *** AND THE #462 CARVE-OUT'S WARRANT IS MEASURABLY FALSE, WITH A SHIPPED INSTANCE. *** The ruling was that three rows are correctly covered because their predicate `computes nothing but liveness, making a non-lethal kill impossible`. It computes `no EOF/OSError was observed inside a budget window`. The instance is THIS ROUND'S OWN M11: table2 records `nodata stale died`, table3 records `survived`, SAME session, SAME command list. It re-synced a scratch tree byte-identical to SEC, rebuilt, reproduced the baseline green, applied the one-line edit and ran that session FIVE TIMES: **5/5 deterministic SIGSEGV**, preceded by `Transfering 408 bytes to 0x00000000 (guest asked for 0)`. **So the shipped table3 M11 entry records `survived` for a session that did not reach `print r2`** -- and the probe's own contract lists TWO candidate causes for that `absent` firing, omitting the one a clean rebuild reproduces: A HOST DEATH THE PREDICATE MISSED. *Honest limit it stated itself: table2's tree is not recorded in the artifacts, so it cannot assert both shipped runs used identical binaries.* **BOUNDING IT, because the gap is narrower than `the detector is hang-blind`: 45 of 58 rows carry a positive oracle a silent session breaks (42 redden, 3 RAISE). The detector is hang-MISATTRIBUTING, plus two genuinely blind rows.**"}]
{"id": "gdromwarnbits", "state": "in work", "commits": "84f6682 84765ec", "hold": "", "witness": "detector:regress/artifacts/gdromwarnbits/bound_assert_controls.sh -- the COMPILE-TIME bound assert gdrom_w_fits_its_carriers, with a five-way control run preserved beside it. *** THIS FIELD READ `harness:` AND WAS MIS-FILED; corrected 2026-08-28 on the assess panel, where the fable5 seat named it. *** `harness:` is admitted only when the round's SUBJECT is the test apparatus, and this round's subject is PRODUCT code. The controls are two-sided on purpose: CLEAN on the shipped source and at nine bits over uint32_t, ERROR at nine over uint8_t, at 33 over uint32_t, and at 40 over uint64_t -- so the guard is demonstrably not a check that cannot fail. The superseded offline mask_equivalence_check.c remains as PROVENANCE only: two seats measured that it duplicates the definitions rather than including them, so it is an AUDIT of one moment and cannot see product drift. RUNG-3 ATTEMPT, per the 2026-08-27 lifecycle ruling: not applicable by construction -- the defect is a COMPILE-TIME representation bound, latent at six bits and unreachable by any guest access on HEAD.", "title": "gdromwarnbits  six of eight warn bits are used and they are hand-assigned hex masks; the ninth overflows a uint8_t SILENTLY and floods", "state_note": "  *** RECLASSIFIED 2026-08-24 (fable5 queue ranking): NOT A ROUND -- an IN-ROUND PREREQUISITE of `gdromcondlies`, and folded into that round's cost. *** The trigger is arithmetic and already known: 6 warn bits are used and `gdromcondlies` adds 3, so 9 latches exceed a `uint8_t` and the enum conversion must happen inside that round rather than being scheduled against it. *Scheduling it separately would put a prerequisite behind the work that needs it.*", "hold_question": ""}
LATEST ENTRY [{"seat": "fable5", "phase": "assess", "d": "OK", "date": "2026-08-29", "grade": "CONFIRMED", "ev": "Agent seat (fable5), adjudication 2026-08-29", "outcome": "UPHELD", "outcome_note": "Every element I could check was correct: assess IS 9/9, 84765ec DID ship 122 lines of product code under this row while `commits` named only 84f6682, and my HELD marker was added to an already-complete stage.", "note": "VERDICT: **THE FULL PANEL IS REQUIRED, AND THE QUESTION WAS ALREADY ANSWERED BY\nMEASUREMENT** -- the nine-seat stage RAN on this very discharge (`84765ec`, 2026-08-28)\nand PAID for itself.\n\n*** MY TEST -- 'a pre-set trigger discharge needs a panel only if it makes a choice the\ntrigger did not already fix' -- IS REJECTED AS A LICENCE, and rejected for the reason I\nmyself flagged, now measured rather than rhetorical: AN AUTHOR CANNOT ENUMERATE THE\nCHOICES THEY DID NOT NOTICE MAKING. ***  I counted ZERO choices.  The shipped file\nrecords at least FOUR, three of them panel-corrected.\n\nTHE ONE THAT SETTLES IT: one seat of nine MEASURED that `GDROM_W_NBITS` had ZERO\nreferences -- 'it sized nothing and bounded nothing, and DELETING it compiled clean'.\n**So the discharge's central 'correct BY CONSTRUCTION' claim rested on a decorative\nsymbol**, and was false until the panel's own finding made NBITS load-bearing.\n\nAND THE PRECEDENT I TRANSFERRED WAS TRANSFERRED ON SHAPE, NOT ON PROPERTY:\n`SH4_VAL_NCLASS` sizes an array and bounds a runtime check, so its by-construction claim\nis true unaided; `GDROM_W_NBITS` was not.  Same conversion, and the property it was cited\nfor was present in one case and absent in the other.  *The trigger's author could not\nhave known they were making that choice -- which is the whole argument.*\n\nTHE RULE, in applicable form:\n  A pre-set trigger buys exactly ONE thing: the DESIGN debate.  When it fires, the round\n  executes the settled form without re-opening pass-1 design, and the brief cites the\n  trigger VERBATIM as a settled premise.  **It buys nothing at REVIEW**: the executed diff\n  is panelled like any diff -- riding in the firing round's pass 2, or on its own full\n  stage whose brief inlines the whole diff -- and the row closes only on full stages.\n  The discharge brief NAMES the trigger verbatim and LISTS every delta between the\n  trigger's text and the executed diff, so seats review the choices actually made instead\n  of re-litigating the settled form.\n\nTHE BOUND, so the panel requirement cannot un-make triggers: a discharge-panel dissent\nagainst the settled FORM itself (enum-ness, count suffix, mask shape) is a LATE DESIGN\nDISSENT -- it files under the late-dissent rule's disposition 3 and does NOT block the\ndischarge.  The panel's jurisdiction is the execution and its unfixed choices.\n\n**THE GATE'S FOUR REFUSALS WERE CORRECT EVERY TIME**, which is worth recording since I\nqueued this question partly because they felt excessive.\n\n`dev_sh4.c` READS BACK CONFIRMED, NO REPAIR: its conversion rode inside `#451`'s panelled\ndiff (`b3ad305`), its shipped comment records a review seat's counter-proposal heard and\nrejected on the merits, and its NCLASS is load-bearing unaided.  The carrier's\n'DISCHARGED -- DO NOT REDO IT' records the INSTRUCTION being complete, never review being\nwaived.\n\nRECORDS REPAIRED ON THIS RULING: `commits` now names `84765ec` as well as `84f6682` (it\nshipped 122 lines of product code under this row); and MY OWN held-marker (HELD-AWAITING-SEAT fable5 -- DEFANGED here on purpose) on the assess stage is REMOVED -- **the stage was already 9/9 when I added\nit, so the marker claimed the round was waiting for a seat that had already answered.**\nThat is the held-marker-counted-as-an-answer shape, inverted.\n\n*** AND THIS ENTRY ITSELF TRIPPED THE DEFECT THE CARRIER WARNS ABOUT: I QUOTED THE MARKER VERBATIM, WHICH RE-ARMS `gen_codex_wall.py` AND WOULD HAVE COUNTED THIS COMPLETED 9/9 STAGE AS HELD. ***  Defanged in place.  The rule is already written down -- 'before quoting any string a tool matches on, ask what that tool will do with your copy of it' -- and I broke it inside an entry recording an adjudication about records discipline."}]
{"id": "asiceventaddr", "title": "asiceventaddr  SYSASIC_EVENT_TO_ADDR is correct only for e in [0,95]: high events decode to DROPPED fourth-word offsets OR to LEGAL mask-register cases that clobber guest state, and no r-guard can see the second kind", "state": "open", "commits": "9ca9cca", "closure": {"by": "", "badge": "", "note": ""}, "hold": "", "witness": "none -- LATENT, and provably so: the highest defined event is 67 < 96", "state_note": "*** #466's PASS 2 OVERTURNED THE ADJUDICATION THAT SHIPPED IT. TWO SEATS INDEPENDENTLY REFUTED THE 'NO BUILD-TIME CHECK IS POSSIBLE' RULING, AND ONE OF THEM BUILT ALL FOUR VARIANTS. #468 ships the guard. ***\n\nThe adjudicator rejected a compile-time check on SHAPE: *an assert can only reference NAMED terms, so a future `#define SYSASIC_EVENT_FOO 96` adds a name it does not mention and compiles clean.* **That is true of a STANDALONE assert and false of a check INSIDE THE MACRO**, which fires at the RAISE and therefore cannot be bypassed by adding a name.\n\nMEASURED by the opus5 seat, four variants under the real build compiler:\n\n    V0 shipped macro           feared edit compiles CLEAN   <- the defect\n    V1 standalone assert       CLEAN   <- adjudicator's objection CONFIRMED\n    V2 check inside the macro  ERROR: negative array size\n    V3 bitfield width          ERROR: negative width in bit-field\n\n**V3 is preferred over V2 and the reason is measured:** with a runtime-variable `e`, V2 silently degrades to a VLA and compiles; V3 hard-errors because a bitfield width must be an integer constant. **Both need only C89** -- clean under `-std=c89 -pedantic-errors -Wall -Wextra` -- so *the C11 leg of the rationale ruled out one SPELLING, not compile-time checking.* And the kimi substitute reached the same conclusion independently, by reasoning rather than building: *'a name-insensitive construction that catches the feared mutant exists, is build-time, and touches neither product code nor the vendored header.'*\n\n**SO THE OPTION SET HAD THREE MEMBERS AND THE ADJUDICATION NAMED TWO** -- comment / standalone assert / **assert inside the macro**. Exactly the three-membered-option-set rule, biting the ruling that invoked it.\n\n*** THE ADDITION LICENCE NOW CLEARS ON A MEASURED DELTA, which it did not before: *** V0 compiles the feared edit, V3 refuses it, and **all 21 real events keep BYTE-IDENTICAL addresses**. That is the A-vs-B measurement the licence asks for, and it is why this ships rather than staying a comment.\n\nINDEPENDENTLY RE-VERIFIED HERE before landing: the feared edit is refused **and fails for OUR reason** (a bitfield diagnostic, not an incidental one); the boundary is exact (`e=95` compiles, `e=96` does not); the header still preprocesses `rc=0` and still holds its 21 numeric event defines, unchanged from before `#466`.\n\n**THE COMMENT'S BAND TABLE WAS ALSO WRONG AND IS CORRECTED.** It omitted the ENTIRE `mask_9` group and understated the clobber domain **5.5x** -- the true domain is `e in [128,479]`, 352 values across NINE mask words, of which the comment named 64. It also mispaired `96..127 -> +0x0c, +0x1c`, which is the same band-to-address error the device file had already corrected an earlier draft for. Restated STRUCTURALLY: the macro treats the event's high bits as a word index into the whole 64-word register file, when only words 0-2 are the `pending_irq` group.\n\n*And a clobber is worse than the comment implied: the mask cases carry no hack-bit discrimination and the store is a wholesale replacement with the tick called immediately, so the raise both FAILS and DESTROYS the guest's programmed IRQ mask.*\n\n*** THREE APPARATUS FAILURES OF MINE ON THE WAY, ALL DOCUMENTED CLASSES, RECORDED BECAUSE THE THIRD IS NEW: ***\n  1. my verification harness redefined `SYSASIC_BASE`, which the header owns -- every leg died of `redefined` and the feared-edit leg's 'pass' was a **FALSE PASS**, failing for the wrong reason. Rule 4 caught it: *did it fail for the reason under test?*\n  2. I wrote the guard with a Python string instead of `chr(92)` and put a **literal `\n` into product code** where a line continuation belongs -- the documented Bash->heredoc->Python trap, propagated to all four trees before I caught it.\n  3. **NEW, AND WORTH THE SPACE: my own comment's worked example -- `#define SYSASIC_EVENT_FOO 96` -- POISONED THE CENSUS that greps the header for event defines.** The harness read the DOCUMENTATION as SOURCE and built a program out of the very construct the comment forbids. **A comment containing an example of a forbidden construct breaks any census that greps for that construct.** Same shape as the grep that matched a comment and was read as code earlier the same day.\n\n*** ADJUDICATED 2026-08-26. MY DISSOLUTION READING HELD -- AND MY FOLD WAS HALF WRONG, WHICH IS THE PART THAT MATTERS. ***\n\n**THE DISSOLUTION CONFIRMED, MEASURED:** every event raise is a `memory_rw` write through the device decode. `SYSASIC_TRIGGER_EVENT` computes the address and issues the write; `SYSASIC_EVENT_TO_ADDR` has exactly TWO occurrences in `src/` -- its definition and that one use -- and every in-tree raiser goes through the macro. The arrays are declared file-locally in the `.c`, so no other unit can index them. **There is no bypass, so the vendored-header LICENCE QUESTION IS MOOT** -- the guard need never live in the header.\n\n*** BUT THE HEADER DEFECT IS BROADER THAN THE r==3 INVARIANT, AND THE EXCESS IS EXACTLY WHAT AN r-GUARD CANNOT SEE. Verified here by arithmetic and by reading the case: ***\n\n    e=96..127   -> +0x0c   r=3   default: -- DROPPED (an r-guard covers this)\n    e=128..159  -> +0x10   r=0   *** case 0x10: `d->mask_13[r] = idata` -- LEGAL WRITE ***\n    e=256..287  -> +0x20   r=0   *** case 0x20: another legal mask case ***\n    e>=2048     -> +0x100+       OUTSIDE the device window entirely\n\n**A raise in [128,159] is decoded as a perfectly legal mask write and CLOBBERS a mask register the guest programmed.** Memory-safe, guest-visible, and invisible to any guard on `r` -- because r is 0, not 3. So the row FOLDS ONLY HALFWAY: the fourth-word half is covered by `asicindexcase`'s already-adjudicated #463b decline at zero added cost; **this row survives as DOCUMENT-ONLY for the mask-clobber bands and the window escape.**\n\n**THE TRUE STATEMENT OF THE DEFECT: `SYSASIC_EVENT_TO_ADDR` is correct only for e in [0,95].** The old title said it 'routes any event >= 96 to relative 0x0c' -- FALSE, true only of [96,127] -- and it contradicted this row's own tail correction AND the shipped comment in the device file, which already says *'an earlier draft said any event >= 96, which is false'*. **The queue's most-read field carried a falsehood its own body had corrected.** Title rewritten.\n\n*** AND I REPEATED THAT SAME FALSE UNIVERSAL TO THE ADJUDICATOR while asking the question -- 'Every event >= 96 gives (e)>>5 == 3' -- when the correction already existed in TWO of my own shipped records. *** A brief built on my own uncorrected summary rather than on my own corrected record: the dropped-qualifier class, aimed at the seat I was asking for help.\n\n**THE COMPILE-TIME ASSERT IS REJECTED ON SHAPE, NOT ON LICENCE, and the reason is this project's own named class.** An assert can only reference NAMED terms, so it pins today's max (`67>>5 == 2`, already true) while a future `#define SYSASIC_EVENT_FOO 96` -- **the exact feared edit** -- adds a name the assert does not mention and compiles clean. **A check that cannot fail on its own target mutant.** The `SH4_VAL` enum idiom does not transfer either: sparse hardware numbers need explicit `= n` per member and a terminal count sees only members above it. **The house precedent points the other way and is in this very device family**: `dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c` chose *'Stated rather than asserted: `_Static_assert` is C11 and this tree is C99'*. Follow it -- STATE the domain restriction.\n\nCLOSING ARTIFACT: a 2-5 line comment in the header beside the macro -- the one place a future event-adder's eyes are guaranteed to be -- pointing at the device file's existing #463 invariant comment, which already points back. A comment adds no product code, so the addition licence is not even triggered. Tag it `/* #NNN: */` per house ethos. Must land byte-identical in `est/`.\n\n*Premises the adjudicator discharged: the two-commit thirdparty figure CONFIRMED -- and sharper than I had it, since `8dd86b5` touched four thirdparty headers but NOT this one, so **this fork has never edited it**; the `[3]` arrays CONFIRMED; and 'a device-file comment cannot govern a header #define' is TRUE of the comment and HALF-TRUE of the guard, which is exactly why the row folds halfway.*\n\n*** THE THREE ASIC ROWS REST ON ONE INVARIANT, AND TWO OF THEM MAY BE ONE FIX. Scoped 2026-08-26 by reading, no run. ***\n\n**THE SHARED INVARIANT: `r == 3` is unreachable by any implemented case.** VERIFIED by reading the switch labels -- they are three CONSECUTIVE words at group bases `0x00/0x10/0x20/0x30`, so `r = (relative_addr / 4) & 3` yields 0, 1, 2 and never 3. All three rows are that one invariant, differing only in **which edit would break it**:\n\n    asiceventaddr    a #define in the vendored HEADER (event >= 96 -> offset 0x0c)\n    asicindexcase    an indexing `case` added at an r==3 offset in the DEVICE FILE\n    asicoffsetkill   an adversarial MUTANT at an r==3 offset no detector row touches\n\n**THE THIRD IS DIFFERENT IN KIND and should not be folded with the other two:** it is a DETECTOR-COVERAGE question, not a product invariant. `kill far` (+0xfc) was added to defeat a GROUP-NARROWING mutant and cannot defeat a SINGLE-OFFSET one; the seat that raised it said plainly it did not build it.\n\n*** THE FINDING THAT MAY FOLD THE OTHER TWO, and it contradicts `asiceventaddr`'s own stated reason for being a separate row: *** that row argues a device-file comment \"cannot govern a `#define` added to a header\". **True of a COMMENT. Not true of a GUARD.** An event `>= 96` does not stay in the header -- it becomes an ADDRESS the driver accesses, arriving at the device as `relative_addr == 0x0c` and decoding to `r == 3`. **So a guard on `r` in the device file would catch the header entry point too**, because the header case MANIFESTS as a device-file access.\n\nIf that holds, one guard covers both rows and the vendored-header edit -- the expensive part, on a file this fork has touched once -- may be unnecessary. **Stated as a question rather than a conclusion because I have not built it**, and the adjudicator was asked the licence question on the header assumption; this finding is being sent to it as a correction rather than acted on unilaterally.\n\nSTOP CONDITION: if the guard-covers-both reading holds, fold `asiceventaddr` into `asicindexcase` as one fix with two entry points and close it as a duplicate; if it does not, the rows stay separate and the header licence question stands.\n\n*** SCOPED 2026-08-26 WITHOUT A RUN -- the row is read-only by construction, and the scoping found a CONSTRAINT that changes the fix's cost. ***\n\n**THE FIX SITE IS A VENDORED THIRDPARTY HEADER.** The macro is at `src/include/thirdparty/dreamcast_sysasicvar.h`, which carries BOTH a GXemul `$Id$` and an upstream **`$NetBSD: sysasicvar.h,v 1.5`** line. **MEASURED: `git log --all -- 'src/include/thirdparty/*'` returns TWO commits -- the 0.7.0 import, and the single ~119-correction hardening commit.** So this fork has edited thirdparty exactly ONCE.\n\n*The precedent is not absent, and it cuts both ways: the header's own banner says it was \"Extended with useful macros and also some more event definitions described in KOS comments\", so GXemul UPSTREAM already extends this vendored file. Editing it is precedented -- it is just rare, and rarity is the cost.*\n\n**WHY THAT MATTERS RATHER THAN BEING TRIVIA:** the row's whole reason for existing separately is that *the edit which reaches this defect adds a `#define` to a header, with no device-file change at all* -- so a structural-invariant comment in `dev_dreamcast_asic.c` cannot govern it. **The only place a guard can sit is the same rarely-touched header**, which means the fix's licence question is sharper than for an in-file addition.\n\n**THE CANDIDATE FIX, stated so it can be adjudicated rather than argued:** a compile-time assertion beside the macro, so that adding an event `>= 96` fails the BUILD rather than silently routing to relative `0x0c` and indexing three-element arrays at 3. It is self-verifying by construction -- a negative control is one test `#define` -- and it costs nothing at runtime.\n\n**BUT IT IS AN ADDITION TO PRODUCT CODE, so the ADDITION LICENCE governs: a MEASURED DELTA or a TRANSFERRED WARRANT, never 'it cannot hurt'.** The delta cannot be measured without a build, and the machine is reserved (owner directive 2026-08-26: hold off on anything needing an idle machine). **`asicbadaddr` is the natural warrant donor** -- same device, same `r = (relative_addr / 4) & 3` decode, same three-element arrays -- and whether that transfers to a THIRDPARTY header edit is exactly the judgement an adjudicator owes rather than me.\n\n*** WHAT IS NOT IN DOUBT AND NEEDED NO RUN: the arithmetic. *** `(e)>>5 == 3` for every `e >= 96`; the highest defined event is 67; therefore latent today, and reachable by a one-line header addition by anyone adding a high event number. Two seats confirmed both numbers from source on 2026-08-24 and I re-derived the macro form here.\n\nNEXT ACTION: adjudicate the licence and the thirdparty-edit question. NOT shipped unilaterally -- a rarely-touched vendored header is precisely where 'it cannot hurt' does the most damage.\n\nRAISED BY THE opus5 SEAT on the asicbadaddr pass-1 panel (panel_20260824_231824), ADJUDICATED 2026-08-24. `SYSASIC_EVENT_TO_ADDR(e)` is `SYSASIC_BASE + 4*((e)>>5)`, so ANY event number >= 96 gives `(e)>>5 == 3` and a relative offset of 0x0c -- which `dev_dreamcast_asic.c`'s `r = (relative_addr / 4) & 3` maps to index 3 of three-element arrays. **LATENT AND PROVABLY SO: the highest defined event is 67 (MATOUTOFMEM), and both the 67 and the 96 threshold were confirmed from source by two seats.** *** WHY IT IS A SEPARATE ROW RATHER THAN PART OF asicbadaddr: THE EDIT THAT REACHES IT ADDS A #define TO A HEADER, WITH NO CHANGE TO THE DEVICE FILE AT ALL -- so the structural-invariant comment asicbadaddr plans to leave in `dev_dreamcast_asic.c` CANNOT GOVERN IT. *** Post-fix the failure mode also changes rather than disappearing: an event >= 96 would write to 0x0c, fall to `default:`, and the pending bit would be SILENTLY LOST where pre-fix it was `exit(1)`. Neither is correct; neither is reachable today. *** DISPOSITION: NO RUNTIME GUARD *** -- a guard that cannot fire is the insurance-that-isn't this tree already rejects. The carrier is a one-line comment at the event-define block IN THE HEADER. **FILED RATHER THAN SHIPPED THIS ROUND, and the reason is the file class:** the header lives under `src/include/thirdparty/`, which this fork HAS edited (the 8dd86b5 hardening commit) but where NO file carries the `/* #NNN: */ ` correction tag the ethos otherwise requires -- so the convention for editing it is unsettled, and settling it is not this round's job. *** THE MANDATORY HALF IS THE RECORD, NOT THE COMMENT: `asicbadaddr` must SCOPE its invariant claim to `dev_dreamcast_asic.c` and name this header path as a separate latent item, rather than claiming the invariant is fully protected. ***  *** CORRECTED 2026-08-25 BY THE #463 RECORDS AUDIT (`_scratchpad/panel_20260825_024101_audit`, grok seat), and the correction makes this row STRONGER. *** Every earlier statement of this row said the macro reaches `0x0c` for \"any event >= 96\". **That is false**: `SYSASIC_EVENT_TO_ADDR(e) = SYSASIC_BASE + 4*((e)>>5)` (`dreamcast_sysasicvar.h:74`) sends `e` in `[128,159]` to `+0x10`, which is r = 0 and perfectly legal. The true predicate is `(e>>5) & 3 == 3` -- `[96,127]` -> `+0x0c`, `[224,255]` -> `+0x1c`, `[352,383]` -> `+0x2c`, `[480,511]` -> `+0x3c`. So the violator is not a single range above a threshold but a REPEATING FAMILY that lands on the fourth word of every 16-byte group -- the same structural shape as `asicbadaddr` itself, one level up in the header. Verified by enumerating `4*(e>>5)` over `e>>5` in `[0,15]`. The LATENCY argument is unchanged and still holds: max over all 21 `SYSASIC_EVENT_*` defines is 67 (`:72`), and `67>>5 == 2` lands at `+0x08`.\n\n*** CLOSURE PROPOSED, NOT MADE.  Verified by me against the source; held for the 2026-09-09\nadjudication because a row closed on one seat's reading plus my own agreement is exactly the\nself-review the conflict-of-interest badge exists to disclose. ***\n**codex (rating pass, 2026-09-07): `low -- the named domain hazard is ALREADY GUARDED`.**  *\"the\nheader now embeds `((e) >= 0 && (e) < 96) ? 1 : -1` in `SYSASIC_EVENT_RANGE_OK(e)`, which\n`SYSASIC_EVENT_TO_ADDR(e)` invokes.\"*\n\n**VERIFIED BY READING:** `dreamcast_sysasicvar.h:135` defines `SYSASIC_EVENT_RANGE_OK` and `:140`\nis `(SYSASIC_BASE + 4*((e)>>5) + SYSASIC_EVENT_RANGE_OK(e))` -- the guard is inside the macro, so an\nout-of-domain event cannot produce an address at all.  Added by `#467`/`#468` (`29b450c`), whose\ntitle says *\"the ASIC domain is now ENFORCED\"*.\n\n**The row's own witness text is now STALE**: it reads *\"LATENT, and provably so: the highest defined\nevent is 67 < 96\"*, which was the pre-fix warrant for it being latent.  The current warrant is\nstronger and different -- the macro rejects the domain structurally -- and that is what the closure\nshould record."}
