{"id": "wdcaddtoinbufbound", "title": "wdcaddtoinbufbound  the ATAPI data-port WRITE arm has no phase guard, so a guest can inject bytes into a live DATA-IN transfer and desynchronise the next 32768-byte chunk", "state": "in work", "commits": "ae68e29", "closure": {"badge": "", "by": "", "note": ""}, "hold": "regress", "witness": "probe:regress/artifacts/wdcaddtoinbufbound/rung3_20260830/ -- RUNG 3, BUILT AND GREEN on one ask.  Unmodified `evbmips -e malta`, wdc via the committed bus_pci/piix4_ide path, free-running, 1.52 s, 20 of 20 predictions across both legs.  Sentinel ADDTOINBUF_RUNG3_DONE.  Controls: RAM liveness; a DEVICE SIGNATURE (two reads of one address giving push order, which a cell cannot); encodings 46/46 words AND mnemonics against the emulator's own disassembler; disk content at known offsets.  On the committed build the guest is handed ITS OWN INJECTED BYTES back as disk data.", "state_note": "*** RIDER FROM THE FABLE SEAT 2026-08-27: THE WRITE-ARM BOUND COVERS MORE THAN DATA\nCORRUPTION. ***\n\nThe CDB dispatch keys on ring OCCUPANCY `== 12` while the write arm is unguarded during\nDATAIN.  So a mid-DATAIN write sequence can drive occupancy to exactly 12 and trigger a\n**SPURIOUS COMMAND DISPATCH, which frees and replaces `atapi_st`** -- while a transfer is\nlive and holding pointers into it.\n\n*That is a lifetime hazard, not a data hazard, and it raises this row above 'the guest\ncorrupts its own transfer'.  Filed from READING; not measured.*\n\n*** FILED BY `#473`, AND ITS RATIONALE WAS CORRECTED THE SAME DAY BY TWO SEATS. ***\n\n`#473` bounded the two DATAIN arms.  It did NOT bound the primitive: `wdc_addtoinbuf()` has\nno capacity check and `head == tail` still means BOTH empty and full.\n\n**THE FILING SAID \"correct and complete for every caller in the tree today\".  THAT IS\nIMPRECISE, and both the Fable and grok seats named the same live counter-example\nindependently:** the data-port WRITE arm pushes guest bytes with no phase guard and no\ncapacity check, and its unexpected-write latch is SUPPRESSED precisely because\n`atapi_cmd_in_progress` is set.  A guest writing while `write_in_progress == 0 &&\natapi_cmd_in_progress == 1` accumulates unboundedly.  **That is the live unbounded route\nand it is independently reachable.**\n\n**AND THE REFILL `#473` ADDED IS ITSELF A CAPACITY-UNAWARE CALLER**, as the codex seat put\nit: *\"at most 32768 calls is not a capacity bound unless at least 32768 free slots are\nestablished.\"*  On every in-tree driver the ring is empty there; on an adversarial\ninterleaved write it is not.\n\n*** PRIORITY ARGUMENT, and it runs the OPPOSITE way to the usual one: `#473` made the\nBENIGN route bounded and silent, so the hole now self-announces LESS. ***  Before that\nround a legitimate oversized READ(10) would trip the `warned_inbuf_overrun` announce.  The\nFable seat's reading is that this argues for RAISING the item's priority rather than\ndeferring it -- what was lost is a POST-HOC diagnostic that fired after corruption began,\nso danger is unchanged and DISCOVERY LIKELIHOOD fell.", "hold_question": "Does a fix belong at the data-port WRITE arm (a phase guard) or at the DATAIN RE-ARM (refuse or flush when the ring is non-empty)?  Note #477 MEASURED that guarding these pushes was worse than no fix at the time -- the suppressed push left occupancy at 12 and CREATED a free -- and that its own PHASE_CMDOUT conjunct may since have defused that objection.  Re-measure rather than transfer the warrant.  Second question: any fix will turn the shipped row `re-arm: refills a NON-EMPTY ring too` RED, so the oracle-correction rule applies -- re-pin first, show the committed build red, then change the code.", "sev": "med"}
