{"id": "wdcfloodstale", "title": "wdcfloodstale  `overrun x2` in wdc_flood_probe.py cannot reach its target since #479 reset the ring on every command", "state": "in work", "commits": "3cdb58a", "closure": {"badge": "", "by": "", "note": ""}, "hold": "", "witness": "harness:mutation -- the re-aimed row reds under Mb2 (the producer's latch DELETED), 1,145,0 against want 1,1,0, measured on the shipped bytes.  The OLD row read 0 on that same mutant, so it was non-discriminating rather than merely red.", "state_note": "*** THE ROW IS OPEN AND NAMES A COMMIT: 3cdb58a carried the MEASUREMENT, not the fix. ***  The gate is right to want the link -- work shipped for this row, and a row that does not name it cannot be traced back from the log.  The FIX (re-aiming the stimulus at the guest data-port write arm) has NOT shipped; that is what keeps the state open.\n\n*** MEASURED, NOT INFERRED -- three legs, by the main loop, on cf300c0. ***\n\n    leg 1  1026 IDENTIFY commands through wdc_command()   ->  0 overrun messages\n    leg 2  CONTROL: the same payload with NO reset        ->  1\n    leg 3  REMEDY: the same overrun via the guest DATA PORT, no tenant  ->  2\n\nLeg 2 is what makes leg 1 a finding rather than a broken harness: the same harness DOES see\nthe message when the ring is allowed to lap.  `overrun x2` in `regress/wdc_flood_probe.py`\ndrives exactly leg 1's stimulus and wants the message ONCE.  It gets none.\n\nMECHANISM: `wdc_command()` calls `wdc_new_command_reset()` unconditionally, zeroing both\npointers; IDENTIFY then stages 512 bytes.  Occupancy after any command is at most 512, so\n1026 commands cannot lap a 262656-byte ring.\n\n*** HONESTY NOTE ON MY OWN ORACLE: `stub_fatals` counts EVERY fatal(), not overruns. ***\nSo leg 3's 2 is ONE overrun plus ONE #464 unexpected-write latch -- the data-port route\narms no tenant, so that latch fires too.  The number is not two overruns, and it is written\ndown that way rather than left to read cleaner than it is.  Legs 1 and 2 are unaffected:\nleg 1 has no fatal() of any kind, and leg 2 pushes through the primitive directly, which\ntrips no unexpected-write latch.\n\n*** IT IS A SCORED GATE ROW AND IT HAS NOT RUN SINCE IT DIED. ***  `overrun x2` is in\n`gate_sh_rounding.sh`'s row list.  The weekly battery runs SUNDAYS; #479 landed on the 27th.\nSo the row is not silently red -- it will go red on the NEXT battery, and whoever triggers\nthat battery will attribute the red to their own change.\n\nTIMELINE, verified in-repo:\n    artifacts/wdcflood/table2_22rows/killwdc.results  base 22/22  2026-08-26T02:35:09Z\n    #479 (2343036)                                                2026-08-27T12:18:26\nThe green baseline predates the change by about 34 hours and nothing re-ran it.\n\n**FLOOR CLAUSE (b) BREACH FOR `#464`:** the probe's own per-site mapping makes this row the\nsole carrier for the ring producer's verbatim-reinstatement mutant.  A dead row leaves that\nsite uncovered today.\n\n**AND THE PROBE HEADER CARRIES A WRONG CAUSE (L4).**  It flags the row for re-pin but blames\na FUTURE fix -- 'marked for RE-PIN when that row lands'.  The row is already dead and the\nkiller was `#479`.\n\nREMEDY, and leg 3 measures it working: re-aim the stimulus at the guest data-port WRITE arm,\nwhich takes no `wdc_command()` and therefore no reset.  `#473` performed exactly this\nre-aiming once on this file's sibling row, recording *a detector aimed at a primitive cannot\nverify a fix made in its caller: right defect, wrong instrument.*  Here it is the reverse --\nright instrument, a stimulus that no longer arrives.\n\nDISPOSITION: FILE and rank URGENT, not fix-in-round -- the adjudicating seat's ruling on\n`#484`, adopted.  It is a different round's floor, excluded by both the round-scope rule\n(different detector, different semantic class) and the stopping rule (not a false pass or\nwrong record of the round that found it).  It wants its own short round BEFORE the next\nSunday battery.", "hold_question": ""}
LATEST ENTRY [{"seat": "opus5", "phase": "review", "d": "OK", "date": "2026-08-27", "ev": "regress/artifacts/wdcfloodstale/reaim_20260827/", "grade": "CONFIRMED", "note": "SHIPPED THE RE-AIM AND MEASURED IT ON MY OWN BYTES rather than on the seat's report. Probe 25/26 -> 26/26; the row reads 1,1,0 against want 1,1,0.  Clause (b) closed: Mb2 (the producer's latch deleted) reads 1,145,0 -> FAIL, where the OLD row read 0 on the same mutant.  145 was predicted by my brief, measured by the timing seat, and measured again here -- three independent agreements on the unit.  The count is 131400 and NOT the arithmetically exact 131328, because at the fill point the unlatched mutant is GREEN; the margin is load-bearing and the comment says so."}]
{"id": "diskimagesign", "title": "#472's clamp introduced two -Wsign-compare warnings that cost a GATE", "state": "in work", "witness": "detector:regress/artifacts/diskimagesign/cascade_20260829", "opened": "2026-08-29", "note": "*** NOT A COSMETIC WARNING -- IT WAS COSTING AN ENTIRE GATE'S COVERAGE, and that was found only by re-running a battery that had been RED AND UNREAD FOR FIVE DAYS. ***  `#472` replaced `len > OVERLAY_BLOCK_SIZE? OVERLAY_BLOCK_SIZE : len` with `len > remain? remain : len`, where `len` is `size_t` (parameter) and `remain` is `off_t`.  gcc warns twice at diskimage.c:1382 under the tree's own CWARNINGS.  **THE CASCADE:** `gate_build` wants ZERO warnings, so it FAILED; it withdraws `$RIG/gxsec-gxemul` at start and republishes only on success; `gate_mips` then `need_exec`s that binary and reported *'SKIP -- missing or non-executable binary'*.  **A whole gate lost, scored as SKIP rather than FAIL** -- byte-identical in shape to what one stale allowlist line cost on 2026-08-23, which is the defect `gatebuildallow` records.  **IT IS NOT A LIVE BUG AND THE ROUND SAYS SO:** `inblock = curofs & (OVERLAY_BLOCK_SIZE-1)` gives [0,511], so `remain` is in [1,512] and positive by construction; the conversion is value-preserving at every reachable value.  Both casts are therefore provable, not plausible.  MEASURED, control first: the reconstructed pre-fix line compiles rc=0/44072 b with **2** sign-compare warnings; the shipped source compiles rc=0/44112 b with **0**.  *** MY FIRST VERIFICATION WAS VACUOUS AND ITS OWN CONTROL CAUGHT IT: *** it reported 0 warnings for the PRE-FIX form, which must be 2.  The compile was failing on a missing include and the script read its empty output as a result -- the phantom-absence class, in a script written to prove a fix.  Take 2 reports compile rc and object size BEFORE the warning count and refuses to read the count without them.  EXPOSURE, stated honestly: warnings need a BUILD, builds are too slow for precommit, so warnings are checked only by the weekly battery -- a warning-introducing commit is invisible for up to seven days, and only if someone reads the result.  *** THE ROW NAMED NO COMMIT WHILE THE FIX HAD SHIPPED.  Found 2026-08-30 by reading `src/disk/diskimage.c`: the clamp now reads `lentoread = len > (size_t) remain? remain : (off_t) len;` with a `#491` comment proving both casts safe -- `inblock` is `curofs` masked to [0, OVERLAY_BLOCK_SIZE-1], so `remain` is in [1, 512] and can never be negative. ***  `git log -S` puts it in **18893de**, now recorded.  **This is the same shape a gate caught on `holdmarkermulti` in the same session -- work shipped, row names no commit -- and it did NOT trip here only because the state is `in work` rather than `open`.**  `check_row_states` scopes its shipped-work test to open rows, so an `in work` row can carry shipped code indefinitely without any gate objecting.  *Recorded as an observation about the checker's scope, not filed as a defect: widening it to `in work` would fire on every round that is genuinely mid-flight, which is what `in work` means.  The honest fix is the habit of filling `commits` at ship time, not a wider gate.*  The row's own EXPOSURE note stands unchanged and is the important part: warnings need a BUILD, builds are too slow for precommit, so a warning-introducing commit is invisible for up to seven days -- and only then if someone reads the battery result.", "commits": "18893de"}
