{"id": "wiringcliff", "title": "EIGHT probe-wiring exemptions expire on 2026-09-20 together and the checker hard-fails -- four are cheap, only four need a design round", "state": "open", "sev": "med", "opened": "2026-08-30", "witness": "harness:MEASURED by running check_probe_wiring.py at --today 2026-09-19 (PASS) and --today 2026-09-21 (FAIL, 8 problems)", "note": "*** EIGHT DATED EXEMPTIONS EXPIRE ON ONE DAY, AND THE CHECKER HARD-FAILS.  MEASURED, NOT REASONED. ***      python tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py --today 2026-09-19   ->  PROBEWIRING_PASS  (46/54 gated, 8 dated-exempt)\n    python tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py --today 2026-09-21   ->  PROBEWIRING_FAIL  8 problem(s)\nSo **every commit after 2026-09-20 is blocked** until all eight are wired or the dates are moved.  The checker is right to do this -- a dated exemption that never comes due is not an exemption -- but eight coming due together is a scheduling hazard rather than a deadline.  *** AND THE POPULATION IS TWICE WHAT THE REGRESS REVIEW NAMED. ***  That review found *'FOUR wdc probes share the single-leg-mode debt and the same 2026-09-20 expiry'* and recommended ONE design round covering four instruments.  Correct about the wdc cluster; the expiry itself is shared by EIGHT probes with FOUR DISTINCT remedies:  **(1) single-leg mode, 4 probes** -- `wdc_altoffset_probe.py`, `_sig_`, `_4bit_`, `wdc_addtoinbuf_probe.py`.  Two-leg differentials; a gate has only one binary.  Two of them additionally need a CD-ROM rig (`ciR:`) and one needs a POISONED image the battery does not build.  This is the one-design-round cluster.  **(2) `hpcmips_ctor_probe.py`** -- rung 2, ~8 s, no rig image; wants a CONSTRUCTION gate.  Different mechanism entirely.  **(3) `sh4_bsc_width_probe.py` -> gate 10; `pit8253_latch_probe.py` -> gate 14.**  Each names the gate it belongs in; these are wiring, not design.  **(4) `fbpending_drain_probe.py`** -- REPORT-ONLY until `EXPECT_CAP` is set, so wiring it without setting that ships a row that cannot fail.  *** THE POINT IS THE SHAPE, NOT THE COUNT: four of the eight are CHEAP (two are plain gate wiring, one is a one-variable decision), and only the wdc four need a design round. ***  Doing the cheap four first shrinks the cliff from eight to four before it arrives, and each is independently shippable.  *Advisory, from the same run: the checker also notes two SELFLABEL discrepancies (`pit8253_latch_probe.py` and `wdc_addtoinbuf_probe.py` -- manifest says detector, the probe's own summary line says witness).  It flags these as advisory precisely because self-labels are measurably unreliable in both directions.*  *** ONE OF THE EIGHT WAS ABOUT TO BE GATE-WIRED WITH NO WAY TO DETECT A TRUNCATION.  FIXED PRE-EMPTIVELY 2026-08-30. ***  `pit8253_latch_probe.py`'s last output was a bare `PASS`/`FAIL` -- a word that occurs in dozens of contexts and is not uniquely greppable.  Its only SCREAMING_TOKEN was `print(\"MEASURED\")`, a **mid-run section header** that a truncated log still contains.  So a gate row written against it could not have distinguished a completed run from a killed one, which is exactly the `killdrivercrash` class arriving in new work rather than old.  Now emits `PIT8253_LATCH_DONE`, on both the pass and fail paths -- a completed FAILURE is not a truncation.  *Doing this BEFORE the wiring means the gate row can be written correctly the first time.  `sh4_bsc_width_probe.py`, the other plain-wiring member, already carries a proper `SH4_BSC_WIDTH_PASS`/`_FAIL` pair and needs nothing.*  *** AND A METHODOLOGICAL CORRECTION WORTH MORE THAN THE FIX, because it nearly became a filed defect count. ***  A sweep for probes lacking a `_DONE`/`_PASS` token returned **34 of 46**.  That number is MEANINGLESS: gate 10 verifies `sh_rounding_probe.py` by grepping `SH_ROUND_RESULT=`, which is a perfectly good completion marker in a different shape.  The convention is per-probe and per-gate, so **there is no 34-probe defect and the question is not sweepable** -- it is 'does this probe's CONSUMER verify completion, and by what?', one probe at a time.  An earlier version of the same sweep put `sh4_bsc_width_probe.py` in the missing list, which is how the pattern was caught: it flagged a probe I had confirmed compliant ten minutes earlier.  **A pattern that selects the wrong set produces a clean, confident, wrong population**, and this is the third instance today.  *** THE CHEAP FOUR, INVESTIGATED READ-ONLY 2026-08-30 SO A ROUND CAN START WITHOUT RE-DERIVING ANY OF IT.  Ranked by what each actually needs. ***  **(a) `pit8253_latch_probe.py` -> gate 14.**  ALREADY UNBLOCKED: it lacked any completion marker and now emits `PIT8253_LATCH_DONE`.  Machine `-E cats`, 10 rows.  Gate 14 is `gate_arm.sh`, which already runs `-E cats`.  Remaining work is the exec line plus row checks.  **(b) `sh4_bsc_width_probe.py` -> gate 10.**  Machine `-E landisk`; already emits `SH4_BSC_WIDTH_PASS`/`_FAIL`.  **Gate 10 (`gate_sh_rounding.sh`) already execs three probes in exactly the needed idiom** -- `python3 <probe>.py \"$PMAX\" \"$KERNEL\" > \"$LOG\" 2>&1 || true`, then `check` rows against the log.  Copying that idiom is the whole change.  **(c) `hpcmips_ctor_probe.py` -> a construction gate.**  Rung 2, ~8 s, NO RIG IMAGE, takes no positional argv, accepts `--binary PATH` (default `$GXROOT/build/gxemul`), emits `HPCMIPS_CTOR_PASS`/`_FAIL`, and already runs its subprocess with a timeout.  `gate_asan_sweep.sh` is the construction gate and sweeps 41 machine types.  ONE MISMATCH TO RESOLVE, named rather than discovered later: that gate drives an ASAN binary while this probe defaults to `build/gxemul` -- `--binary` makes it parameterisable, so the decision is which binary the row should assert against.  **(d) `fbpending_drain_probe.py` -- ONE VARIABLE, BUT NOT A FREE ONE.**  It is REPORT-ONLY until `--expect-cap N` is set (`EXPECT_CAP = -1` means report only), and **wiring it without choosing N ships a row that cannot fail** -- the check-that-cannot-fail shape.  Choosing N needs a measurement, and the probe carries a **31.51 s wall-clock floor by construction**, so it is timing-sensitive and must be measured on a quiet machine.  Cheapest in code, most expensive in care.  *Ordering follows from that: (a) and (b) are mechanical, (c) needs one decision, (d) needs a measurement.  Three of the four can ship before the wdc design round even starts.*  *** `8404520` NAMED: that commit shipped this row's FIRST piece of code -- the `PIT8253_LATCH_DONE` sentinel -- and the attribution check refused the NEXT commit until the row said so.  Third time today this rule has fired (after `census` and `wdcatapistswap`), and every one was a real gap rather than a nuisance. ***  The row stays OPEN: one of eight probes is unblocked, seven remain, and the cliff is unmoved until they are wired.", "commits": "8404520"}
