{"id": "pcc2widthkills", "state": "open", "sev": "high", "opened": "2026-08-31", "commits": "b78bb0f", "title": "dev_pcc2.c: TEN live width kills (of twelve guards -- #119 already fixed two in this same file, and its shape is the answer)", "witness": "probe:regress/pcc2_width_witness.py -- rung 3, 4/10 on the committed build (three aligned accesses each exit1, four controls green); artifacts/pcc2widthkills/witness_20260903_PREFIX.out", "note": "*** THE SAME CLASS #337 FIXED IN dev_wdc.c AND #497 FIXED IN dev_sgi_re.c, AT TWICE THE SITE COUNT, IN A FILE NOBODY HAS EXAMINED. ***  A guest choosing an access width is not an emulator error; here every register in the block insists on a byte and ends the host process otherwise.  **REACHABILITY IS STRONGER THAN `sgidewidth`'s, and this time I checked the domain before saying so.**  `sgidewidth`'s exotic widths turned out unreachable because the MIPS core raises ADEL/ADES first.  **No such filter helps here: the killing widths are 2, 4 and 8 -- all ORDINARY, all naturally alignable.**  A plain aligned 4-byte read of `PCCTWO_GENCTL` needs no misalignment, no truncation and no unusual instruction.  *** HOW IT WAS FOUND, because the method transfers and the finding is a symptom of it: *** all four HIGH rows tonight were in `dev_sgi_re.c`, which invited the question of whether that file is uniquely BAD or merely uniquely LOOKED AT.  Comparing live terminating-call density against ledger attention answers it:  `dev_sgi_re.c` 15 exits / 11 rows / 4 HIGH -- heavily examined.  **`dev_pcc2.c` 13 exits / 2 rows / 0 HIGH.  `dev_lca.c` 11 exits / 2 rows / 0 HIGH.**  The kill density is comparable and the attention is not.  **`dev_lca.c` IS THE OTHER ONE AND IT IS NOT EXAMINED HERE** -- filed as a companion question rather than swept in, because a second file's worth of claims made in the same breath is how a census gets over-claimed.  MACHINE: `machine_mvme88k.c` is the only consumer.  **There is no mvme88k rig in this tree**, which is why the file has escaped attention -- and is also why a witness will be harder here than for the sgi rows: gate 9 constructs machines, but a rung-3 access witness needs a guest that reaches these registers.  *That is a reason to rank the WITNESS work, not a reason to rank the DEFECT lower: reachability by construction is established, and no rig means no coverage rather than no bug.*  *** THE STATED BLOCKER -- \"there is no rig\" -- IS NOT A REASON A RUNG-3 WITNESS IS IMPOSSIBLE, AND THIS PROJECT'S OWN MOST RECENT WITNESS REFUTES IT.  Measured 2026-08-31. ***  `#498`'s rung-3 witness for `sgidewidth` execs `gxemul -V -x -E sgi -e ip32 -M 64 0x80001000:<DUMMY>` and **`DUMMY` falls back to `/dev/null`** when `regress/dummy_kernel.bin` is absent (anchor: `grep -n 'DUMMY = ' regress/sgi_de_width_probe.py`).  **No disk image, no kernel, no boot -- a cold-debugger probe needs a machine that CONSTRUCTS and an empty load file, and that is all.**  Verified by running all four candidates under WSL with `<addr>:/dev/null`: `-E sgi -e ip32`, `-E alpha -e alphabook1`, `-E mvme88k -e mvme187` and `-e mvme188` **all reach `machine:` and print a cpu line**.  *A rig is what a rung-4 BOOT needs.  Conflating the two is what left both of these rows ranked as witness-blocked when neither had been tried.*  **WHAT IS STILL NOT ESTABLISHED, and the attempt is exactly for this:** whether the debugger reaches these registers through the machine's real decode, and whether the observable differs pre- and post-fix.  Constructing is necessary, not sufficient.  *My first run of this test reported all three machines failing -- because I ran the WSL-built ELF from git-bash and got `cannot execute binary file`, while my own grep for `GXemul|machine:` matched the word `gxemul` INSIDE that error and scored it a healthy banner.  A broken apparatus plus a check that matched the error text nearly filed a confident \"these machines do not construct\".*  For this row the target is **`-E mvme88k -e mvme187`** specifically: `case MACHINE_MVME88K_187:` is the arm that adds `pcc2 addr=0x%x` at `PCC2_BASE`, and `mvme188`/`mvme197` do not.  *** PASS-1 PACKET-FED PANEL, 2026-08-31 (`_scratchpad/panel_20260831_054552`).  THE ROW OVERCOUNTS BY TWO, AND THE DISPOSITION WAS ALREADY SETTLED BY THIS PROJECT'S OWN `#119` IN THIS SAME FILE. ***  **THE COUNT.  Twelve `if (len != 1)` guards; TEN still `exit(1)`; TWO already carry a warn-once decline.**  Deriver rather than a literal:  `grep -c 'if (len != 1)' src/devices/dev_pcc2.c` = 12, and an `awk` window over each guard separates the arms ending in `exit(1);` from those carrying `static int w`.  The two already-fixed arms are `PCCTWO_IPL` and `PCCTWO_MASK`.  *** AND `#119`'s SHAPE IS THE ANSWER TO THE DISPOSITION QUESTION, WRITTEN IN THE FILE WITH ITS OWN ROUND NUMBER ON IT: ***  `static int w = 0;  /* #119: was exit(1) -> warn-once + continue */` followed by `if (!w) { fatal(\"[ pcc2: non-byte access to PCCTWO_IPL; ignored ]\"); w = 1; }`.  So the member is **decline + latched announce**, the message says *ignored*, and a prior round already chose it here.  **This row's remaining work is to finish a job `#119` started and left two-twelfths done** -- which is a materially different framing from *twelve host kills nobody has looked at*.  *** BUT THE EXISTING PRECEDENT USES THE ANTI-PATTERN LATCH, AND THE NEW WORK MUST NOT COPY IT. ***  `static int w = 0` inside the arm is FUNCTION-STATIC -- process-global, shared by every device instance, so one guest's access silences the diagnostic for every other `pcc2` in the machine.  That is the `n_trunc_msgs` shape the `sgideprimop` seat censused hours earlier as the tree's one bad model.  The house form is a per-instance `d->warned` bit with `(once)` in the text.  **Copy `#119`'s DISPOSITION and TONE; do not copy its LATCH SCOPE** -- and the round should say whether it converts the two existing sites too, or leaves a file with two latch idioms in it.  **glm's premise audit was right about method and the conclusions split.**  *(a)* It refused my unshown claim that `fatal()` returns.  **Checked: it returns** -- `va_start / va_debug / va_end`, no exit -- so the in-file precedent really is announce-and-continue.  My premise was right and I should have inlined the evidence.  *(b)* It challenged `grep -c '^\\s*exit(1);'` as blind to terminating calls spelled otherwise.  The count is unchanged at 13 -- **but the four extra `exit(` hits are `#119`'s own comments, which is how the fourfold precedent was found at all.**  A methodological objection that found a fact even though the number held.  *(c)* **It refuted my Q3 premise outright.**  I wrote that the read path for these arms assigns `odata`.  Measured: `odata` appears twice in the whole file and both are in a DIFFERENT access function; the twelve guarded arms handle `MEM_WRITE` and `break`.  So a declined *read* of these registers returns whatever the caller pre-set -- and `#95`'s pre-zero in `memory_rw.c` supplies 0.  **The ACCEPT option's premise -- that the model computes a value and discards it -- is false here.**  *** Q1 IS ANSWERED AND THE ROW IS A DEFECT, NOT DOCUMENTATION -- confirmed from the tree's own header, which documents the legality of exactly what these guards kill. ***  `mvme_pcctworeg.h:9-10` states the PCCchip2 CSRs *\"can be accessed as bytes (8 bits), two-bytes (16 bits), or four-bytes (32 bits)\"*, and the m88k load/store path is compiled for `LS_SIZE` 1/2/4/8 and passes that width to `memory_rw`.  **So the ten live `len != 1` kills fire on accesses this fork's own vendor header calls legal**, and the *byte-wide* framing describes the MODEL rather than the chip.  My premise that a wide access might be undeliverable is refuted, and the row's severity goes UP rather than down.  A rung-3 probe has a concrete target: debugger `put h`/`put w` at `0xfff42000 + off` under `-E mvme88k -e mvme187`.  *** THIS ROW NOW HAS A MEASURED RUNG-3 WITNESS, NOT AN ENUMERATED ONE.  The kimi seat stopped reading and built it; I reproduced it rather than recording its measurement as fact. ***  **A. REAL GUEST DECODE, reproduced:** four hand-assembled m88k words placed with `put w`, `pc=0x1000`, `step 4`.  The trace shows `or r2,r2,0x201c ; 0xfff4201c` then `st r1,r2,0x0 ; [0xfff4201c] = 0x00000042`, then `TODO: pcc2: non-byte reads and writes of PCCTWO_SCCERR`.  **A real store, through real address decode, on an unmodified in-tree machine description** -- which is the ladder's rung 3, not a debugger poke.  *** B. A PROBE-AUTHORSHIP TRAP THAT WOULD PRODUCE A CONFIDENTLY WRONG RESULT, and it is worth more than this row: `put b` NEVER REACHES THE DEVICE. ***  Measured: a byte `put` to a guarded register produces **0** device messages, while `put w` to the same register produces **1**.  The debugger's byte path passes `NO_EXCEPTIONS`, and `memory_rw` skips the device call entirely for `DM_DEFAULT` devices.  **So a byte-wide `put` probe prints success, exits 0, and would let its author conclude *no kill here*.**  That is the check-that-cannot-fail shape living in the very tooling this project uses to BUILD witnesses.  Use `put h`/`put w` or real guest instructions.  **C. ALIGNMENT SPLITS THE POPULATION, and two seats reached 5-of-10 independently.**  Only **5 of the 10 live kills** are reachable from a guest instruction stream, because `dev_pcc2_access` switches on the transaction's STARTING offset and the odd-based registers cannot be an aligned start.  The rest are debugger-only.  *A detector must not claim guest reachability for rows it can only reach with `put`.*  **D. AND MY BRIEF UNDERSTATED ONE CASE.**  I asked whether a read spanning two adjacent byte registers is 'a different proposition'.  Measured: a word `ld` at an UNGUARDED base (`0xfff42000`) returns `0x2000000f` -- spanning CHIPID, CHIPREV, GENCTL and VECBASE -- **straight through VECBASE's guard**.  It is not a different proposition; it is already accepted today.  *** L4, AND IT WAS MINE: THE WITNESS I COMMITTED CARRIED A DEATH ORACLE THAT COULD NOT FAIL.  Caught by the flagship seat reviewing an UNRELATED round's diff, in an artifact that happened to ship inside the same commit. ***  `witness_20260831.sh` reported `exit status: ${PIPESTATUS[0]}` on the pipeline `printf ... | timeout gxemul ... | grep ... | head`.  **Index 0 is `printf`.**  It printed `0` whether the host died or not, so the committed `.out` asserted *exit status: 0* at a site this row records as a live kill -- the check-that-cannot-fail shape, in a witness whose entire purpose is to demonstrate a DEATH.  **Corrected to `${PIPESTATUS[1]}` and re-run: the oracle now reports `emulator exit status: 1`.**  The value moved from meaningless to the actual kill, which is the whole point of the channel.  **The REACHABILITY half was never in doubt and is unaffected**: the transcript still shows `st r1,r2,0x0 ; [0xfff4201c] = 0x00000042` followed by the SCCERR message, and both controls (`put b` -> 0 device messages, `put w` -> 1) are independent of the exit status.  *So the witness stays a real rung-3 reproduction; what was broken was the death OBSERVABLE, not the reachability.*  *** IT BREAKS A RULE I WROTE INTO THE CHECKPOINT THE SAME MORNING -- *never pipe a gate through a filter when you need its exit status* -- which is this project's own finding that knowing a rule and having it fire on the right object are different problems.  I wrote the rule about GATES and then piped a WITNESS. ***", "hold_question": "ADJUDICATED 2026-09-02 by fable5: DO NOW, FIRST IN QUEUE.  Direction split YES (reads ACCEPT, writes decline + per-instance latch); addition licence SATISFIED citing #497's sgi_re per-instance bitfield, NOT #119's statics.  *** THE ROW'S OWN FRAMING WAS CORRECTED: FIVE of the twelve guarded registers are guest-reachable, not ten -- refuted at OB:widthkillclass (2026-08-30), which predates the brief that got it wrong. ***  No question remains held; what remains is the ROUND: apply one edit shape to all ten arms, state the 5/7 split in the contract, attempt the rung-3 mvme187 witness, and answer whether real drivers issue the wide writes the decline refuses.", "state_note": "*** PASS 2 FOUND SIX CLEAN SWEEPS of the shipped 60-row detector (b78bb0f), the smallest ONE CHARACTER; the REPAIR is on disk, uncommitted: 82 rows (+8 IPL/MASK, +7 per-arm channels, +2 latch cardinality, +1 operand-swap, +4 odd-arm fall-through). Measured: part 1 nine mutants all killed at 78 (part1/driver.log); the builder's pass 3 killed all six sweeps at 78 and its 3b showed four fall-through TOTAL SWEEPS at 78 that its 3c kills 81/82 each at 82 (opus_pass3_20260903/); part 2 (M_IDATA_GENCTL vs 82) pending at the time of this note. PRETABLE.out written before table 2 (Section 0 records that table 1 had none). OWED before commit: part 2's sentinel, propagate dev_pcc2.c to build/ build-asan/ gxsec-build + two-part clearance, gate 14, CHANGELOG block from the logs, then PASS 3 (the eight other seats; the builder's pass 3 is one seat of it).  *** Superseded text, kept: FIXED (uncommitted at the time of writing; commit follows). Twelve width-guard blocks -> direction split: reads ACCEPT (the memcpy above the switch already served them; the tree's own header says wide access is LEGAL), writes DECLINE + per-instance 12-bit latch. DELETE is measurably wrong (big-endian compose: the low byte is the neighbour's lane) -- M_DELETE killed. The seven odd arms are OPERATOR-reachable (debugger put h, MEASURED) -- all twelve converted, M_OPKILL killed through that path. Detector 60 rows, gate 14 626/0, PREFIX 14/60, BASE 60/60, seven mutants zero sweeps. Design pass on 6 of 9 + one measurement (Opus x2 529, grok x2 CLI). Filed: pcc2c040kill (HIGH), pcc2counter8 (MED), pcc2widecompose. Owes pass 2.  2026-09-04 FIX REVIEW (owner directive): GPT-6 Astra (max) APPROVES the fix, DOES NOT CLOSE; Fable 5.1 GO-WITH-CHANGES.  Seven Astra findings confirmed from the tree; the main loop graded two as open floor cells -- the five HUH?-clear arms were then MEASURED HELD by announce-count rows (part 13 M_HUHV 249/251; the rbq rows strengthen the channel, L4 + coverage, not L2), the INT-set/count-0 cell awaits M_ZINT -> PASS 13 owed: 9 rows (242 -> 251), 7 mutants, gate, panel.  Astra dissents on the floor bound (Opus strength-t); the adjudicator adopts Fable's floor + the generating set.  PASS 13 PANEL COMPLETE 9/9 (2026-09-05 02:17-03:46).  RULING: PASS 14 OWED -- the class closure (three file-reading seats derived the same two gaps: the generating set one-sided at the two new INT cells; the INT axis not crossed with the other count), graded L1 (uncommitted detector material).  The pass-13 state is COMMITTED with the obligation OPEN (thirteen uncommitted passes were the larger exposure); pass 14 is drafted (fix_pcc2_p15.py: +18 rows -> 269, 8 mutants, PRETABLE_pass14, plus a pre-repair breach witness part14pre) and runs after the pcc2defaultkill fix ships (owner priority: critical first).", "reopened": "b78bb0f -- that commit's subject called the width-kill closure closed; the row was REOPENED on 2026-09-03 by its own pass 2 and stayed open through thirteen detector passes (the product fix unchanged, approved block by block on the 2026-09-04 fix review); pass 14 owed at the 2026-09-05 commit."}
