{"id": "lunaabsentcpu", "title": "lunaabsentcpu  dev_luna88k.c reassert_interrupts() calls the NULL interrupt callback of an UNCONFIGURED cpu slot: on the default one-cpu luna-88k two ordinary guest word stores (INT_ST_MASK1 then SOFT_INT1) kill the emulator process with SIGSEGV -- MEASURED, rung 3", "sev": "high", "state": "closed", "opened": "2026-09-05", "commits": "79cd12e", "witness": "probe:regress/luna_absentcpu_probe.py -- a guest store of 0xfc000000 to INT_ST_MASK1 followed by a store to SOFT_INT1 killed the emulator with SIGSEGV on the default one-cpu machine; measured pre-fix, green post-fix", "hold_question": null, "note": "DEFECT, HIGH -- MEASURED (rung 3).  dev_luna88k.c: reassert_interrupts() (anchor `static void reassert_interrupts') walks `for (cpu = 0; cpu < MAX_CPUS; cpu++)' and calls INTERRUPT_ASSERT(d->cpu_irq[cpu]) -- interrupt.h expands that to a direct call through `.interrupt_assert', no NULL check -- while DEVINIT connects cpu_irq[1..3] only under `if (devinit->machine->ncpus >= 2/3/4)' and memset()s the device to zero.  The INT_ST_MASK0-3 store arm (anchor `case INT_ST_MASK0:') does `d->interrupt_enable[cpunr] = idata; reassert_interrupts(d);' with NO ncpus check, and the SOFT_INT0-3 arm sets a per-slot pending bit and calls reassert synchronously.  On the default ONE-cpu luna-88k two ordinary guest word stores -- 0xfc000000 -> 0x65000004 (INT_ST_MASK1), then 1 -> 0x69000004 (SOFT_INT1) -- call a NULL function pointer: the emulator process dies with SIGSEGV, no diagnostic.  WITNESS: _scratchpad/lunaabs/luna_absentcpu_probe.py on the HEAD binary /tmp/gxopus501p4 (dev_luna88k.c cmp-equal to git HEAD 83df95c), committed unmodified `-e luna-88k', cold debugger: C1 RAM liveness OK, C2 device signature OK (MASK0 >>8), A1 the SAME two stores on the CONNECTED slot 0 SURVIVE (attribution), A2 the MASK1 store alone SURVIVES (readback 0x00fc0000: stored as-is), W1 SOFT_INT1 store: HOST DIED signal 11.  LUNA_ABSENTCPU_WITNESS=PRE_FIX_SYMPTOM, 1.4 s; log regress/artifacts/lunaabsentcpu/witness_head_20260905_2130.out, probe as run beside it.  Pre-existing upstream (the loop and the connect guards are 0.7.0 code); #438 removed the exit(1) on low bits in the SAME arm; its probe's D4 row stores 0x84000100 to MASK1 (bits 31, 26, 8 -- bit 26 SET, slot 1 ARMED for the rest of that session) and could not see this only because a cold debugger has nothing pending and the probe never stores SOFT_INT1 (an earlier wording here said 'bit 26 CLEAR'; corrected by the batch-7 Fable adjudication, which also notes that ANY device interrupt after the arming store kills the host on a running guest).  OPTION SET -- batch-7 Fable adjudication RULING (2026-09-05 ~22:40): (a) ALONE -- bound the reassert loop to `d->ncpus' captured at DEVINIT (house idiom dev_mp.c:134/:191 and this file's own RESET_CPU_ALL arm `i < cpu->machine->ncpus'); (b) declining stores to absent slots is REJECTED for this round (an unsourced board-model claim, changes guest-visible readback, reddens two shipped es438 rows D4/E2) and FILED as the question row lunaabsentmaskdecl; (c) a no-op connection fabricates plumbing with no in-tree idiom; (e) a NULL check guards the symptom.  Detector: W1 kill row + the connected-slot control + a `-n 2' ACCEPT row whose channel is cpu 1 ASSERTING (irq_asserted / vector entry), not survival; PRETABLE mutants `cpu <= d->ncpus', ncpus never assigned (slot 0 never asserted: the boot gate reds), `ncpus = 1' hardcoded.  Wire luna_absentcpu_probe.py into gate_m88k_rounding.sh.  Also owed in that round: es438cells (MASK2/MASK3 readback rows).  Ranked HIGH: guest-reachable process death by an ordinary instruction on the machine the project boots (precedent: pcc2defaultkill, m88kexcdeliver ranked on reachability, not on a known driver doing it).  Provenance: retrospective batch 7 -- GPT-6 Astra (codex, max effort, read-only) predicted it from the source ('interrupt delivery can still reach an unconnected CPU callback'); the main loop built and ran the witness the same evening; Fable 5.1 adjudication of batch 7 in flight.  The fix round runs the full nine-seat panel.", "state_note": "\n\n=== MAIN-LOOP ROUND RECORDS, 2026-09-06 -- moved out of the per-seat `entries' column ===\nThese are the round's OWN records -- table results, repair descriptions, stage summaries -- not any seat's\nanswer.  They were written into `entries' with seat=`main', which is not one of the nine declared seats; the\ndashboard generator refused the file and a census confirmed the label is this session's invention rather than\nan established convention (every other entry in the ledger uses a declared seat).  The seat column is the\nper-seat record that check_boilerplate, check_seats_read and the scorecard all read, so a round note living\nthere is a round note wearing a seat's clothes -- the stamped-summary shape one level up.  Moved here verbatim;\nthe same material is in the CHANGELOG round block.\n\n  [2026-09-06 / review] PASS-2 REPAIR (one diff): fix capture moved below the `ncpus > 4` exit; probe v3 adds sessions B2 (sio5 deliver), C2 (both deliver) and E (-n 4: four cpus / cpu3 deliver / others idle); docstring corrections; mutants M_MASK30, M_SWONLY, M_IRQACTIVE0, M_AND1, M_MIN2 added to the eleven; PRETABLE2 written before table 2 (corrections to go to a separate file).  Table 1 (killtable/, v2, 22 rows): BASE 22/22 PASS; M_REVERT/M_OFFBY1/M_LATCH 15/17, M_NULLA/M_GUARDASSERT 16/17, M_NOASSIGN 17/19, M_HARD1/M_SKIP1 19/20, M_NULLAB 22/22 (equivalent), M_NOREASSERT 22/22 (debt), M_CALLGUARD 16/18 -- the pass-1 F4 alternative measured NOT a fix (sio5, siofirst HALT: the SIO line reaches the walk through the device-line callers).  HEAD 15/17 PRE_FIX_SYMPTOM (soft1, siofirst; the denominator drops from 22 when the host dies mid-session).\n\n  [2026-09-05 / assess] WITNESS built before any edit (rung 3, unmodified -e luna-88k): two supervisor stores (INT_ST_MASK1 <- 0xfc000000, SOFT_INT1 <- 1) on the default one-cpu machine end the emulator with SIGSEGV -- witness_head_20260905_2130.out.  Filed from retrospective batch 7 (Fable adjudication of the m8820x rounds) after the #438 probe missed it: that probe stored 0x84000100 (bit 26 SET) but never stored SOFT_INT1, so nothing was pending when the walk ran.\n\n  [2026-09-06 / review] PASS-3 REPAIR (probe v4 -> v5, 34 rows, frozen v5 md5 3fd0198f; fix v2b unchanged): v4 added sessions B3 (redeliver, reenter, noreenter) and C3 (noreenter1) for the deassert side (deepseek, minimax) and pended slots 2 AND 3 in E (grok, kimi: M_SKIP2); v5 (kimi, landed after v4 was cut) pends ALL FOUR slots in E (cpu0/cpu1 deliver4, others-idle removed) and adds C4 (sio5 deliver1: a device line to slot 1).  TABLE 3 (killtable3/) and TABLE 4 (killtable4/, PRETABLE4 written 11:00 before the 11:31 start, untouched): 22 mutants, BASE 34/34 before and after, clone cmp-clean; every mutant killed except M_NULLAB 34/34 -- PREDICTED EQUIVALENT (a both-sided NULL guard is the same predicate as the bound on every reachable configuration; DEVINIT connects exactly slots 0..ncpus-1 and the ncpus > 4 exit bounds ncpus), recorded as an alternative fix with its warrant, not a breach.  M_IRQACTIVEP1 32/34 (cpu2/cpu3 deliver: the parity alias), M_NOREASSERT 31/33 (killed by the noreenter rows; the v2/v3 disclosure withdrawn), M_CALLGUARD 28/30 (pass 2 had predicted it equivalent; it is not).  HEAD 83df95c with v5: 27/29 PRE_FIX_SYMPTOM (soft1, siofirst).  PRETABLE4's one mis-prediction was in the safe direction (it said M_NOREASSERT would stay green on the NEW rows; it stays killed by the v4 rows).  Pass 4 (closing) fired to the scriptable seats at ~12:28 (brief_lunaabs_p4.md); Opus 5 / Fable 5 owed when the wall lifts.\n\n  [2026-09-06 / review] PASS-4 REPAIR, graded L2 (a clause-(d) floor breach: an operand swap drawn from a field the guarded body READS, on the predicate dominating the walk's body) -- repaired IN-ROUND, not filed.  Because passes 3 and 4 both found breaches of the {slot} x {per-cpu array} alias class, the repair closes the CLASS in one diff rather than the cell: probe v6 (md5 3be01e9d) RESTORES v4's session E beside v5's all-four E as session E2 (slots 2 and 3 pending, 0 and 1 idle; rows cpu3 deliver23, cpu2 deliver23, others idle23 -- 37 rows), in which every per-cpu array the walk touches has a disagreeing alias target.  Mutants added: M_ENP1, M_SWP1 (the two predicted sweeps, each ALSO scored on the frozen v5 so the escape is measured), M_EN0, M_SW0, and -- instead of disclosing the other alias forms as debt -- M_ENX1 (`cpu ^ 1`, derived to be killed by sio5 deliver1) and M_ENSH1 (`cpu >> 1`, killed by E2).  A first draft of PRETABLE5 had disclosed `cpu ^ 1` as an escape; that was wrong about C4 and was corrected BEFORE the table ran.  Table 5 (24 mutants) launched 12:5x.\n\n  [2026-09-06 / review] **PASS 5 -- TWO OF MY OWN RECORDS ARE WRONG, both found by codex AND grok independently, and I VERIFIED BOTH BY OPENING THE FILES.**  (1) **THE NARROWED M_NULLAB EXPIRY IS FALSE, and it came from the opus5 pass-4 answer, which I propagated without checking.**  That clause said an EMPTY interrupt name installs non-NULL `no_interrupt_assert' / `no_interrupt_deassert' stubs, so `has a callback' and `slot < ncpus' differ in MECHANISM.  Read `src/core/interrupt.c' (anchor `if (name[0] ==' the empty-string test): the arm assigns the stubs and **DOES NOT RETURN** -- the handler scan runs immediately after it, and an unregistered name falls through to `printf(... failed)' and `abort()`.  So the stub path is DEAD unless the empty string is itself a registered handler name, and `INTERRUPT_CONNECT(\"\")` appears nowhere.  The two predicates coincide **because the stub path is dead**, not because slots 1..3 happen to use non-empty names.  grok adds that pass 1 already had this.  The M_NULLAB equivalence is UNAFFECTED and the fix is not reopened; only the expiry sentence is corrected.  (2) **THE PASS-5 PACKET NAMES THE WRONG RED ROWS FOR M_IRQACTIVEP1.**  Its table says `cpu2 deliver23, cpu3 deliver23' (session E2).  killtable5/driver.log at the `=== M_IRQACTIVEP1' anchor says **`cpu3 deliver' and `cpu2 deliver' -- session E**, and E2 passes.  Verified.  The mutant aliases a WRITE, so the first arrival of each pair consumes the latch whichever session pends them; E2 exists for the READ aliases and correctly does not red here.  A transcription error in a packet that had the log open beside it.  **WHAT THE TWO SEATS RULED ON THE ROUND'S ONE QUESTION: the closure bound HOLDS.**  grok: `function of cpu alone' is the right bound for THIS class, with the derivation -- the one-cpu session forces f(0)=0, session C forces f(1)=1, session E2 forces f({2,3}) inside {2,3}, so every single-token map except the identity dies; cells outside f(cpu) are body-field indices (`enable[irqActive[cpu]]' is [0] on the first walk and dies on C); a joint same-f on enable and software_interrupt_status still dies on E2 (opus5 measured M_ENP1 and M_ENP1E both 35/37); and expanding past single-token f(cpu) is the unbounded chase.  No clean-sweep sits in the remainder.  **ONE REAL PROBE DEFECT, ship-time rather than a new pass (grok): session E2 never sets cpu 0's VBR/PSR, though session E does.**  So `others idle23' can see a steal onto cpu 1 (M_AND1 reads BADREGS with pcs[1] at the vector) and CANNOT see one onto cpu 0 -- a CHANNEL filter on an existing row, not a new cell.  Fix: copy E's two sends.  Both seats also note the product-comment L4 is still present in killtable5/baseline_dev_luna88k.c -- correct, and it stays: that file is the frozen record of what the table ran.  The SHIPPING script (fix_lunaabs.py) was corrected before either answer landed.\n\n=== TABLE 6, 2026-09-06 ===\nTABLE 6 (regress/artifacts/lunaabsentcpu/killtable6_codexpred/), run against codex's two predictions on the shipped 37-row instrument.  BASE 37/37 opening and closing; scratch clone clean against SEC; no sentinel left; frozen probe md5 7170ae7a.  **M_LOWBIT `cpu < (d->ncpus & -d->ncpus)`: 37/37, TOTAL ESCAPE.  M_SOFTAND1 `software_interrupt_status[cpu]&1`: 37/37, TOTAL ESCAPE.**  *** BOTH ARE MEMBERS OF FAMILIES THIS PROBE HAD ALREADY DISCLOSED, WHICH IS CLAUSE (e) WORKING RATHER THAN A FLOOR BREACH. ***  M_SOFTAND1 is the `== 1` cell opus5 disclosed on pass 4 (the body tests nonzero-ness and never partitions the nonzero class; every SOFT value any session writes is 1, so any predicate true of 1 preserves every observation) -- two spellings, one cell.  M_LOWBIT is the ncpus-dependent fold kimi NAMED on pass 5 before a member existed, writing that it folds only at `-n 3' which no session boots.  Contract upgraded from NAMED to MEASURED in probe v8 (docstring-only; proved by comparing executable structure with docstrings stripped against the frozen v7 the table ran).  Closure filed as lunancpus3.\n\n\n*** CLOSED 2026-09-07 AS A RECORDS CORRECTION.  THE FIX SHIPPED IN `79cd12e` ON 2026-09-06 AND THE\nROW WAS NEVER UPDATED. ***\n\n`dev_luna88k.c` carries the repair BY NAME: *\"#503 (lunaabsentcpu): bound `reassert_interrupts()` to\nthe slots connected above.  It walked all MAX_CPUS and called the NULL interrupt_assert of an\nunpopulated slot ... measured, `regress/luna_absentcpu_probe.py`.\"*  The loop bound changed from\n`cpu < MAX_CPUS` to `cpu < d->ncpus`, the `ncpus` snapshot field was added with its own comment, and\nthe 48 kB probe was committed in the same commit.  Kill tables ran; two total escapes were measured\nand both disposed under clause (e) as members of families the probe had already disclosed; the\nresidual is filed as `lunancpus3`.\n\n**So the work was complete and only the row was wrong** -- state `open`, `commits` empty, severity\nHIGH.  It inflated the open-HIGH count and would have misled anyone picking the next round: I\nreported ten open HIGH rows earlier today and the true figure was nine.\n\n*** THE SWEEP, because one instance is not a population. ***  Two derivers were run and the first was\nwrong in the way this project keeps re-learning:\n  * **keyed on a CORRELATE** -- open rows citing a recent commit SHA anywhere in their note -- gave\n    EIGHT, and seven were false: they cite a commit as CONTEXT (a blocker, the round they audit, a\n    records commit), not as their own fix;\n  * **keyed on what DEFINES the class** -- row ids named inside a `#NNN (rowid)` fix tag in `src/` --\n    gave THREE, of which `pcc2defaultkill` is correctly closed and `wdcflood` is `in work` WITH\n    commits, a legitimate intermediate state.\n\n**Population: ONE.**  No checker catches this shape -- `check_row_states` verifies that no open row\nNAMES a committed detector, and this row named nothing; `check_bugfile_sync` and `check_witness` both\npass on it.  *A row can be complete in the tree and open in the ledger with every gate green.*"}
LATEST ENTRY [{"date": "2026-09-06", "phase": "review", "seat": "codex", "note": "#503 PASS 2 (the repair-chain pass the revised fix owed).  VERDICT: ONE MORE REPAIR -- and it was RIGHT about the one thing the revision rested on.  *** IT REFUTED THE REVISION'S OWN RATIONALE: relocating the ncpus capture BELOW the `> 4' exit does NOT remove the fix's dependence on that exit. ***  Above or below, the assignment supplies the same bound whenever execution reaches the walk, because the walk is downstream of the exit either way; both placements rely on initialization rejecting excessive counts.  A CLAMP to MAX_CPUS is what would buy independence, and it found no failure in any accepted configuration -- so under the addition licence there is no measured delta for one.  Disposition taken: KEEP the relocation, CORRECT the claim (L4, in-round).  It also caught that the walk bound and the connect predicate carry the same VALUE rather than being one shared field -- the predicates still read `machine->ncpus` while the walk reads the snapshot -- and confirmed the snapshot is stable in this fork (CPU counts are assigned during configuration, CPUs created before devices, no later mutation or CPU-add path), which is the premise I had checked privately and left as a question in the brief.  On the registers it declined to demand a DECLINE: absent-bank semantics are not checkable from the packet, so neither FAULT nor discarded writes has a warrant, and changing them in-round would be a behavioural change without evidence.  **AND IT PREDICTED TWO SURVIVORS, both since MEASURED at 37/37** -- see the table-6 entry.  It labelled them 'source-derived predictions; I did not rerun the simulator', which is the honest form and is why they were measured rather than acted on.  the codex pass-2 run, preserved at regress/artifacts/lunaabsentcpu/pass2_codex/"}]

