witness: probe:regress/artifacts/wdcaddtoinbufbound/rung3_20260830/ -- RUNG 3, BUILT AND GREEN on one ask.  Unmodified `evbmips -e malta`, wdc via the committed bus_pci/piix4_ide path, free-running, 1.52 s, 20 of 20 predictions across both legs.  Sentinel ADDTOINBUF_RUNG3_DONE.  Controls: RAM liveness; a DEVICE SIGNATURE (two reads of one address giving push order, which a cell cannot); encodings 46/46 words AND mnemonics against the emulator's own disassembler; disk content at known offsets.  On the committed build the guest is handed ITS OWN INJECTED BYTES back as disk data.
state_note: *** RIDER FROM THE FABLE SEAT 2026-08-27: THE WRITE-ARM BOUND COVERS MORE THAN DATA
CORRUPTION. ***

The CDB dispatch keys on ring OCCUPANCY `== 12` while the write arm is unguarded during
DATAIN.  So a mid-DATAIN write sequence can drive occupancy to exactly 12 and trigger a
**SPURIOUS COMMAND DISPATCH, which frees and replaces `atapi_st`** -- while a transfer is
live and holding pointers into it.

*That is a lifetime hazard, not a data hazard, and it raises this row above 'the guest
corrupts its own transfer'.  Filed from READING; not measured.*

*** FILED BY `#473`, AND ITS RATIONALE WAS CORRECTED THE SAME DAY BY TWO SEATS. ***

`#473` bounded the two DATAIN arms.  It did NOT bound the primitive: `wdc_addtoinbuf()` has
no capacity check and `head == tail` still means BOTH empty and full ...[middle omitted]... lished."*  On every in-tree driver the ring is empty there; on an adversarial
interleaved write it is not.

*** PRIORITY ARGUMENT, and it runs the OPPOSITE way to the usual one: `#473` made the
BENIGN route bounded and silent, so the hole now self-announces LESS. ***  Before that
round a legitimate oversized READ(10) would trip the `warned_inbuf_overrun` announce.  The
Fable seat's reading is that this argues for RAISING the item's priority rather than
deferring it -- what was lost is a POST-HOC diagnostic that fired after corruption began,
so danger is unchanged and DISCOVERY LIKELIHOOD fell.
hold_question: Does a fix belong at the data-port WRITE arm (a phase guard) or at the DATAIN RE-ARM (refuse or flush when the ring is non-empty)?  Note #477 MEASURED that guarding these pushes was worse than no fix at the time -- the suppressed push left occupancy at 12 and CREATED a free -- and that its own PHASE_CMDOUT conjunct may since have defused that objection.  Re-measure rather than transfer the warrant.  Second question: any fix will turn the shipped row `re-arm: refills a NON-EMPTY ring too` RED, so the oracle-correction rule applies -- re-pin first, show the committed build red, then change the code.
sev: med

