633: #  at most 512 and 1026 commands cannot lap a 262,656-byte ring.  MEASURED, by a
634: #  single-variable control that reverts ONLY that reset and keeps #484's ring:
635: #
636: #      shipped build            OVERRUN = 0
637: #      #479's reset reverted    OVERRUN = 1
638: #
639: #  *** AND IT WAS NON-DISCRIMINATING, WHICH IS WORSE THAN RED. ***  Measured on the
640: #  committed binary: the old stimulus reads OVERRUN = 0 on the shipped latch AND 0 on a
641: #  build with the latch DELETED.  Clause (b) was open at the ring producer with nothing
642: #  saying so.
643: #
644: #  RE-AIMED at the guest DATA PORT, which takes no `wdc_command()` and therefore no
645: #  reset -- the only surviving route that laps the ring.
646: #
647: #  *** THE COUNT IS 131400 AND IT SITS 72 STORES ABOVE A CLIFF.  DO NOT "TIDY" IT. ***
648: #  Post-#484 the producer refuses at head == 262,655, so push 262,656 is the first
649: #  refusal -- the SECOND push of store 131,328.  Measured, want == 1:
650: #
651: #      n            131328      131329      131400
652: #      shipped         1 ok        1 ok        1 ok
653: #      latch DELETED   1 VACUOUS   3 red     145 red
654: #
655: #  At the exact fill point the unlatched mutant is GREEN.  Shrinking 131400 to 131328
656: #  reads like tightening a hand-waved number to what the arithmetic says, makes the
657: #  comment MORE precise, and silently re-opens the L2 breach.  The margin is a
658: #  load-bearing product of this row.
659: #
660: #  ORACLE IS THREE VALUES, and the third is the anti-fake control.  The smallest way to
661: #  fake this row is to re-anchor P_OVERRUN onto the `:704` DATA text -- then every build
662: #  reads 1 and `disjoint` cannot catch it, because its own stimulus fires neither
663: #  pattern.  So this row counts BOTH sites and asserts that NO SINGLE LINE is matched by
664: #  both.  Asserting "1,1" alone does not close it; a P_OVERRUN aimed at the DATA line
665: #  still gives 1,1.
