83df95c Retrospective re-check batch 3a (dev_wdc.c #337..#464): every fix holds; two guest-visible ATAPI defects filed MEDIUM, seven LOW rows, two merges, twenty records corrected in place
92e917e #486 wdcflood: a scored gate row that was not merely red, but NON-DISCRIMINATING
commit 67120fd60970f9ef2d504bc12bd9f92f7f709a71
Author: indigoidk <indigoidk@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Sun Aug 30 12:31:35 2026 -0500

    #495 wdc: a host store during an ATAPI DATA-IN transfer was pushed into the ring, and the guest read its own bytes back as disk data
    
    Real ATAPI hardware never accepts host data-register stores while the direction is
    device-to-host.  This model accepted them.  The injected bytes grew inbuf_head WITHOUT
    growing atapi_len -- which counts only what the DATA-IN arms pushed -- so they sat ahead
    of the next chunk, the re-arm refilled on top of them, and the guest's whole next chunk
    arrived shifted.
    
    AND IT WAS SILENT.  The one diagnostic that could have announced a stray write requires
    !atapi_cmd_in_progress, and that flag is 1 for the whole of DATA-IN.  The single guard
    that might have caught this was disabled exactly when it was needed.
    
    THE WITNESS: rung 3, unmodified evbmips -e malta via the committed piix4_ide path,
    free-running, 22 of 22 across both legs.  On the committed build the guest is handed ITS
    OWN INJECTED BYTES as disk data (a5/5a); fixed, it gets the poisoned image's content
    (c2/c3) -- an ABSOLUTE oracle, tied to disk through a channel outside the ring.
    
    THE FLOOR DETERMINED THE DELIVERABLE'S SHAPE.  The shipped 128-row offline detector
    scores ZERO failures with this defect live -- it cannot see it -- so clause (a) was
    unsatisfiable from any existing row.  That is why the witness ships AS a detector rather
    than as evidence.  The two are complementary and neither subsumes the other, measured.
    
    === SEVEN MUTANTS, ALL KILLED.  TWO WERE ESCAPES, REPAIRED IN-ROUND ===
    
      M1 drop flag conjunct   20/22    M5 == PHASE_DATAIN   21/22  <- ESCAPED 20/20
      M2 null the repair      18/22    M6 && len == 2       21/22  <- ESCAPED 22/22
      M3 && len == 1          18/22    M7 return 0          mutant leg never parks
      M4 verbatim reinstate   18/22
    
    M5 was predicted by a packet-fed seat that said plainly it COULD NOT CHECK ITS OWN CLAIM
    from the packet.  It was checkable here and it was right: every row injected during
    DATA-IN, where the narrowed guard still refuses.  A DETECTOR BUILT ENTIRELY FROM ONE
    SCENARIO IS BLIND ALONG THE AXIS THAT SCENARIO HOLDS FIXED.
    
    M6 is the MIRROR of M3, and M3 was this round's own mutant.  Both injections were 2-byte
    stores, so `&& len == 2` refused both and swept clean -- the table had measured the inert
    member of the family and concluded the family was covered.  Closed by ONE INSTRUCTION:
    the second injection is now a single byte, so neither width qualifier can refuse both.
    
    === AND THE DEEPEST FINDING WAS ABOUT THE INSTRUMENTS ===
    
    FOUR COMMITTED DETECTORS REPORTED `parked: yes` FOR A GUEST THAT NEVER PARKED.  Each
    tested `if "BREAKPOINT" in <tail>.upper():` -- and the transcript contains the probe's OWN
    echoed `breakpoint add` command.  The check matched its own input and COULD NEVER RETURN
    FALSE.  Right by accident in every run where the guest parked; silently wrong in the first
    run where it did not.  The emulator prints `BREAKPOINT: pc =` on a real hit.
    
    AND THE SCORER WOULD HAVE SCORED SUCH A RUN AS A KILL.  A measuring seat triggered that
    three times in one table: runs with `words 0 of 46`, pc already at HALT and 0.00 s, ON
    BOTH LEGS INCLUDING THE UNMUTATED BASE, scored KILLED (20 red).  Re-run quietly, two
    ESCAPED.  EVERY ONE CARRIED THE COMPLETION SENTINEL -- which proves the probe finished
    WRITING and says nothing about whether it MEASURED.  A false kill is worse than a false
    escape: it certifies coverage never taken.
    
    kill495.sh now refuses to score a run whose BASE leg's controls failed.  The
    discriminator is the base leg, NOT a threshold -- a threshold would reject M7, whose
    mutant leg legitimately kills the guest.  Cause was this round's own concurrency, and the
    #489 precedent covers TREE SAFETY, not MEASUREMENT VALIDITY.
    
    === RECORDS REPAIRS THIS ROUND OWED ===
    
    shipped.diff was rewritten IN PLACE after the table consumed it -- the immutable-artifact
    rule's exact shape, committed by me.  The as-run bytes survived only as a staged blob and
    were recovered from it; both now sit in the killtable dir with PROVENANCE.txt, and the
    comment-only claim was CHECKED (three code lines each, identical), not asserted.
    
    A SUBAGENT CREATED A LITERAL `C:` DIRECTORY INSIDE THE REPO holding a full copy of it --
    1831 files -- and `git add -A` staged them.  Caught before the commit, unstaged, and
    removed with the owner's approval.  It had made fifteen older rounds' citations ambiguous;
    those are now qualified by row directory.
    
    Two files here were written by a SEAT after its report said it wrote nothing.  The content
    is a clean independent replication of the M5 kill and is kept; the discrepancy is recorded.
    An agent's account of what it touched is a CLAIM, and git status is the check.
    
    FILED, not fixed: wdcatapistswap (an operand swap escaping all 22 rows, measured to refuse
    every ordinary data-port write for the life of the device -- not a clause-(d) breach by
    the DEPENDS-ON letter, but the accept side is unpinned) and wdcprobeendian (every rung-3
    probe here has only a little-endian leg, so the BE half of every guard in that switch is
    unpinned by construction).
    
    src/devices/dev_wdc.c is byte-identical in est/ and propagated into all THREE no-VPATH
    build trees, each rebuilt with the two-part clearance run.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

