FILE HEAD:tools/pipeline/seat_verdicts.py
1: #!/usr/bin/env python3
2: """Report each panel seat's VERDICT line, and whether the seat actually ANSWERED.
3: 
4: *** THIS EXISTS BECAUSE THE OBVIOUS GREP IS WRONG, AND IT WAS WRONG FOUR TIMES IN ONE
5: DAY. ***  Briefs instruct seats with a line like
6: 
7:     `VERDICT: SHIP` / `VERDICT: SHIP-WITH-CHANGES` / `VERDICT: DO-NOT-SHIP` -- one line
8: 
9: and every seat ECHOES the brief before answering (codex always does).  So
10: `grep -o 'VERDICT: [A-Z-]*' | tail -1` reads the brief's own menu and reports the LAST
11: option on it as the seat's verdict.  On 2026-08-22 that scored a seat DO-NOT-SHIP when it
12: had not answered at all, and the round nearly re-planned around it.
13: 
14: The discriminator is mechanical and cheap: an INSTRUCTION line lists several tokens, an
15: ANSWER line carries exactly one.  Anchoring to start-of-line is NOT enough on its own --
16: the instruction line often starts with a backtick and the token.
17: 
18: Second failure this guards: judging a seat by FILE SIZE.  A walled codex returns the
19: echoed brief plus its quota notice and can be the LARGEST file in the panel; the packet-fed
20: Ollama seats do not echo at all, so comparing their size to the brief marks them absent.
21: Novel-line ratio against the brief separates the two without reference to length.
22: 
23: usage:  seat_verdicts.py <panel_dir>
24: """
25: import io
26: import json
27: import os
28: import re
29: import sys
30: 
31: #  #461: TOLERATE MARKDOWN EMPHASIS AROUND THE VERDICT WORD.
32: #
33: #  MEASURED on the #460 pass-2 panel: minimax answered `VERDICT: **SHIP**` and grok
34: #  answered `VERDICT: SHIP WITH CHANGES**`.  The old pattern required a LETTER
35: #  immediately after the colon, so the `*` defeated it and BOTH correct answers
36: #  landed under "answered, NO VERDICT LINE (format miss)".
37: #
38: #  *** THAT IS THE MIRROR IMAGE OF THE FAILURE THIS FILE EXISTS TO PREVENT. ***  It
39: #  was built so a DEAD seat is never counted as agreement; here a LIVE seat with a
40: #  correct verdict was being downgraded to a formatting failure, which is the same
41: #  error pointing the other way and just as silent.
42: #
43: #  The leading class is OPTIONAL and bounded, so the findall-count discriminator
44: #  below still works: the brief's own menu line still yields three matches and is
45: #  still skipped.  Only the emphasis characters are new.
46: #  AND IT KEEPS THE WHOLE PHRASE, WHICH THE OLD PATTERN DID NOT.  Capturing one
47: #  word made `SHIP` and `SHIP WITH CHANGES` IDENTICAL in the report -- two
48: #  materially different answers rendered the same -- and collapsed every
49: #  OPTION A / OPTION B / OPTION C verdict to a bare "OPTION", so a pass-1 panel
50: #  that SPLIT read as unanimous.  That defect predates the emphasis one and is
51: #  worse: emphasis lost a seat, this lost the ANSWER while still reporting it.
52: #
53: #  Uppercase-only, up to four tokens: it takes "SHIP WITH CHANGES", "DO NOT
54: #  SHIP", "OPTION A" and "SOMETHING ELSE", and stops dead at lowercase prose or
55: #  punctuation, so "VERDICT: SHIP -- the code is fine" still yields just SHIP.
56: #  *** FOUR FORMS A LIVE SEAT WRITES THAT THE FIRST TWO VERSIONS MISSED, ALL
57: #  NAMED BY PANEL SEATS AND THEN MEASURED. ***
58: #
59: #    **VERDICT**: SHIP          emphasis on the LABEL, not the value
60: #    verdict: ship              lowercase
61: #    VERDICT � SHIP             an em dash instead of a colon
62: #    VERDICT: "SHIP"            a quoted value
63: #
64: #  And one that was worse than a miss: `VERDICT: Ship with changes` returned
65: #  the single letter "S".  Not None -- an actual token, which would have been
66: #  REPORTED as the seat's verdict, because [A-Z][A-Z-]* matched the S and
67: #  stopped dead at the lowercase.  A silent wrong answer beats a silent
68: #  missing answer for damage, and this file exists to prevent exactly that.
69: #
70: #  So: the label may carry emphasis, the separator may be a colon or a dash,
71: #  the value may be quoted, and the value is matched CASE-INSENSITIVELY and
72: #  upper-cased on the way out.  A one-character token is refused outright
73: #  (there is no real one-letter verdict, and it is the signature of a
74: #  truncated match).
75: #  #470: A LINE THAT BEGINS `path:NNN:` IS TOOL OUTPUT THE SEAT STREAMED, NEVER ITS OWN
76: #  PROSE.  The file-reading seats (codex, agy, grok) write their grep and read results
77: #  into the same file as their answer, so a verdict quoted from ANOTHER PANEL'S RECORD
78: #  arrives looking exactly like this seat's conclusion.
79: #
80: #  *** MEASURED, and this is not hypothetical. ***  On panel_20260826_222557 the
81: #  extractor reported `codex ... SHIP-WITH ... answered` while codex had issued NO
82: #  verdict and was STILL RUNNING -- its file grew 909,882 -> 965,763 bytes during the
83: #  check.  The match was at codex.txt:924, inside a line codex had grepped out of
84: #  regress/pass2_ledger.tsv: a receipt from 2026-08-18 recording a DIFFERENT panel.
85: #  A FABRICATED RATIFICATION would have entered the round record.
86: #
87: #  SCOPE, measured across every panel dir on disk (1,310 seat files):
88: #      5,341 lines contain "VERDICT"; 91 of those are grep-output lines.
89: #      1,062 lines the extractor ACCEPTS as a verdict; this rule kills exactly TWO,
90: #      both in codex.txt, both the same grepped ledger line, from which it would have
91: #      taken the verdict "the DECLINE is the".  Zero packet-fed seats affected -- an
92: #      Ollama seat cannot grep, so its output contains only its own words.
93: #
94: #  2 of 1,062 is the whole footprint: it removes the false-positive class and touches
95: #  nothing else.  It does NOT address the other half of `verdictprose` -- extracting
96: #  from a seat that has not finished -- which needs a COMPLETION RECORD and is filed
97: #  with its nine designed controls.  A stable file size is NOT that record: a thinking
98: #  model stalls its output while still running.
99: GREP_OUTPUT_LINE = re.compile(r"^[A-Za-z0-9_./\\-]+\.[A-Za-z0-9]{1,5}[-:][0-9]+[-:]")
100: 
101: VERDICT_RE = re.compile(
102:     #  *** THE DASH MUST BE SURROUNDED BY SPACE, AND THE FIRST VERSION OF
103:     #  THIS LINE DID NOT SAY SO. ***  A bare [-] in the class matched the
104:     #  hyphen inside a word, so "a verdict-driven review process" returned
105:     #  "DRIVEN REVIEW PROCESS" -- a false positive introduced by the fix for
106:     #  a false negative.  A colon needs no spacing; a dash does.
107:     #  *** THE LABEL IS ANCHORED TO LINE START, AND WITHOUT THAT A SENTENCE OF
108:     #  PROSE SWALLOWS A REAL VERDICT. ***  Measured by a review seat:
109:     #
110:     #      "My verdict: the code is fine and the records are honest."
111:     #          ->  "THE CODE IS FINE"
112:     #
113:     #  Case-insensitive matching made every mid-sentence "verdict:" a
114:     #  candidate, and first-qualifying-line-wins means one such sentence of
115:     #  preamble outranks the seat's actual answer further down.  That is the
116:     #  silent-wrong-answer class this file exists to prevent, arriving through
117:     #  the fix for a silent-missing-answer class -- the second time in this
118:     #  round that the loosening did that.
119:     #
120:     #  Only whitespace, emphasis, list and quote markers may precede the
121:     #  label.  Checked against every row: none has prose before it.
122:     #  *** AND THE ANCHOR MUST ALSO ADMIT A SENTENCE BOUNDARY, OR IT BLANKS
123:     #  AN ENTIRE SEAT. ***  Measured: line-start-only rejected 52 of grok's
124:     #  58 answers, because grok streams its reasoning with no newlines and
125:     #  ends the giant first line with `...name the mutant.**VERDICT: SHIP
126:     #  WITH CHANGES**`.  A check that silently blanks one seat in seven is
127:     #  worse than the false positives it prevents -- that is a seat failure
128:     #  manufactured by the tool.
129:     #
130:     #  So the label is admitted at LINE START or after SENTENCE-ENDING
131:     #  punctuation, in both cases through optional emphasis and list
132:     #  markers.  Mid-sentence prose ("My verdict: the code is fine") still
133:     #  matches neither, which is the whole point.
134:     #  *** A QUALIFYING WORD MAY SIT BETWEEN THE MARKUP AND THE LABEL, AND
135:     #  OMITTING IT REPORTED A LIVE SEAT AS WALLED. ***  Measured 2026-08-27:
136:     #  agy ended with "### **FINAL VERDICT: GO-WITH-CHANGES**".  The class here
137:     #  allowed MARKUP before VERDICT but not a WORD, so the verdict was missed;
138:     #  and because the wall test is "keyword in the tail AND no verdict", that
139:     #  miss let the word "non-quota" -- which the seat used while DISCUSSING
140:     #  panel.sh's own quota handling -- tip a 10.6 KB answer to *** WALLED ***.
141:     #
142:     #  A live seat scored dead is the seat-ledger failure inverted, and it is
143:     #  the worse direction: a walled seat gets re-fired; a seat recorded walled
144:     #  that actually ANSWERED becomes an unread review.
145:     #
146:     #  Added to the LINE-ANCHORED branch only.  The mid-sentence branch is left
147:     #  alone deliberately -- that is where "My verdict: the code is fine"
148:     #  produced a false positive, and widening it would reopen that.
149:     r"(?:^[ \t]*(?:[*_`#>|+-]{1,6}[ \t]*)*(?:(?:FINAL|OVERALL|SEAT|PANEL)[ \t]*(?:[*_`]{1,3}[ \t]*)?)?|[.!?][ \t]*[*_`]{0,3}[ \t]*)VERDICT[ \t]*[*_`]{0,3}[ \t]*(?::|[ \t]+[��-][ \t]+)"
150:     r"\s*[*_`\x22\x27]{0,3}\s*([A-Za-z][A-Za-z-]{1,}(?:\s+[A-Za-z][A-Za-z-]*){0,3})",
151:     re.I)
152: #  *** TWO PATTERNS, BECAUSE ANCHORING THE EXTRACTOR BROKE THE OLDEST GUARD
153: #  IN THIS FILE AND THE SELFTEST CAUGHT IT. ***
154: #
155: #  The count-per-line rule -- an INSTRUCTION line lists several tokens, an
156: #  ANSWER line carries exactly one -- is the discriminator this whole file was
157: #  built around.  Anchoring VERDICT_RE to line start made the SECOND token on
158: #  "VERDICT: SHIP / VERDICT: DO NOT SHIP" unmatchable, so findall returned 1,
159: #  the menu passed the guard, and three rows went red at once.
160: #
257f56f #504 sgirandompixel: a failed pixel read handed the caller random() and the write stored it; plus the pass-2 receipt 79cd12e owed, and a measured fix for the codex content-filter refusals
79cd12e #502 pass 9-12 and #503: two fixes shipped, a filing overturned to a floor breach and repaired, and a detector's own contract predicting both of its escapes
cf300c0 #484 wdc: a full ring reported itself empty, and the next push destroyed a byte
1cec2da #476 wdc: #475's clamp WOKE a NULL dereference -- two guest accesses killed the host
d64a1fc #475 wdc ATAPI: clamp the byte count to what REMAINS; plus #474's pass-2 second wave
180afaa #470 verdictprose: a verdict QUOTED from another panel is no longer read as this seat's
733a4b6 #464 pass 2: one wrong record, one filed escape, and two seats the harness was losing
faffad7 panel.sh fires the kimi substitute, and teaching the checkers about it found three unread reviews
ba30e64 #461 both `default:` arms of the GD-ROM device ended the host process
8eeeea2 verdict extractor: a measured regression in my own fix, and the guard that fixes it
e9f8c0c harness pass 2: two measured false passes fixed, verdict detector shipped
3ad72e0 harness: score-claims gate, verdict-extractor fix, floor clause (d), 2 filings
45d7488 A wall is a moment, not a state -- and panel.sh could not see this one
