           "note": "MEASURED, both from the pass-2 panel: `VERDICT: SHIP I think this is ready.` -> `SHIP I THINK THIS`, and `VERDICT: DO NOT SHIP is what I would say` -> `DO NOT SHIP IS`. Visible in production: codex on panel_20260823_173510 reports `SHIP WITH CHANGES | SHIP I THINK THIS | SHIP | DO NOT SHIP`. FILED rather than fixed, and the reason is not triage fatigue: matching a fixed verdict vocabulary would REJECT the real answers this project has recorded (KEEP THE SPLIT, MEASUREMENT SOUND, PARTIALLY CORRECT, DEPENDS ON FINDINGS, OPTION A/B/C), which are seats answering the question the brief asked. And the damage is bounded by the split reporting shipped alongside it -- a swept value now appears as one entry in a `SPLIT, read the file` list rather than as THE answer, so it is loud rather than silent, which is the property that matters."
         }
       ],
-      "state_note": "*** THIS ROW HAD AN EMPTY state_note UNTIL NOW -- I flagged it this morning as unworkable BECAUSE its evidence would have to be re-derived from scratch. It got its instance the same day, and the instance is WORSE THAN THE TITLE SAYS. ***\n\nThe title says the extractor 'sweeps trailing prose into the captured value'. **It also attributes ANOTHER SEAT'S VERDICT, FROM ANOTHER PANEL, TO A SEAT THAT HAS NOT FINISHED THINKING.**\n\nMEASURED on `panel_20260826_222557` (the `#466` pass 2): `seat_verdicts.py` printed\n`codex  909882  95%  SHIP-WITH  answered`. Codex had issued no verdict line at all -- `grep -n '^VERDICT' codex.txt` returns NOTHING -- and it was **still running**, its file growing from 909,882 to 965,763 bytes during the check with its tail mid-grep.\n\nThe matched string is at `codex.txt:924`, and it is not codex's text. It is a line codex GREPPED OUT OF `regress/pass2_ledger.tsv`: a receipt from 2026-08-18 recording a DIFFERENT panel's `SHIP-` verdict. **The extractor read a file-search result as the seat's own conclusion.**\n\n*** WHY THIS IS THE SEAT-FAILURE-COUNTED-AS-AGREEMENT SHAPE IN ITS PUREST FORM, arriving through the tool built to prevent it: *** the round record would have carried 'codex: SHIP-WITH' -- a FABRICATED RATIFICATION from a seat that had not answered. The panel would have been reported complete with its most substantial seat still thinking, and nobody reading the record could tell.\n\n**AND IT IS SPECIFIC TO THE FILE-READING SEATS, which is what makes it dangerous.** A packet-fed seat cannot grep, so its output contains only its own words. Codex, agy and grok stream their SEARCH RESULTS into the same file as their answer -- so the richer the seat's investigation, the more other people's verdicts it quotes, and the more likely a position-blind matcher latches onto one.\n\n**THE DISCRIMINATOR THE EXTRACTOR ALREADY HAS AND DID NOT APPLY HERE: POSITION.** `seat_verdicts.py` uses a position check to catch the kimi wall-in-a-huge-answer class. A verdict is a seat's CONCLUSION, so it belongs at the END of its output -- `:924` of a file that is now past 965 KB is nowhere near it. **A match in the first 1% of a still-growing file is a quotation, not a conclusion.**\n\nSECOND SIGNAL, free and independent: **a file that is still GROWING has not concluded.** The extractor read a snapshot of a live seat. Comparing size across two reads costs nothing and would have refused this outright.\n\nNOT FIXED IN-ROUND: this is a checker change and owes negative controls in both directions -- a real trailing verdict must still be found, and a quoted one must not be. Filed with the instance, which is what the row was missing.\n\n"
+      "state_note": "*** FIX DESIGNED 2026-08-26 BY A FARMED codex JOB, WITH NINE NAMED CONTROLS KEYED TO REAL FILES FROM TODAY'S PANELS -- AND IT REFUTED THE SECOND SIGNAL I PROPOSED. ***\n\n**IT RESOLVED THE CRUX I could not.** The obvious fix is a POSITION check, but this project's briefs demand VERDICT LINE FIRST precisely so truncation cannot destroy the answer -- so 'last match wins' is exactly wrong here. The control `first_real_then_late_quote` pins that: a file with a real verdict FIRST and a quoted bad verdict LATER must return the FIRST, *'proving last-wins was not reintroduced.'*\n\n*** AND MY 'A GROWING FILE HAS NOT CONCLUDED' HEURISTIC IS INSUFFICIENT, which the seat showed with a control rather than an argument: *** `stable_thinker` -- two identical size reads while the process is STILL ALIVE must remain unsettled, *'proving \"stable size means done\" cannot pass.'* **A thinking model can stall its output while running**, so stability proves nothing. The real discriminator is a COMPLETION RECORD / final-message artifact, not file growth. `growth_between_stats` covers the other direction: append between the two stats and extraction must be suppressed even if a plausible verdict was already present.\n\n**THE FULL CONTROL SET -- four positive, five negative, most keyed to files on disk today:**\n  POSITIVE: `completed_codex_with_foreign_middle` (the real `panel_20260826_222557` codex transcript must return CHANGES REQUIRED, **not the middle SHIP-WITH** -- that is the exact measured defect); `agy_verdict_first` (must return GO); `grok_single_line_first` (must retain its opening verdict despite a no-newline streaming form); `legacy_trailing` (a completed artifact with a trailing VERDICT must still be found).\n  NEGATIVE: `active_foreign_only` (in-flight, foreign line present, no completion record -> `RUNNING/UNSETTLED`, verdict `-`); `completed_tool_quote_no_answer` (process exited, no final assistant message -> still no verdict); plus the two above.\n\n**THAT IS A COMPLETE, SHIPPABLE DESIGN THAT NEEDS NO EMULATOR BUILD** -- which is why this row and `probewiringscope` were farmed while the machine is embargoed. The remaining work is implementation plus running the controls.\n\n*Note the seat used TODAY'S PANEL FILES as its fixtures rather than synthetic strings, so the positive controls are regression tests against the exact failure that motivated the row.*\n\n*** THIS ROW HAD AN EMPTY state_note UNTIL NOW -- I flagged it this morning as unworkable BECAUSE its evidence would have to be re-derived from scratch. It got its instance the same day, and the instance is WORSE THAN THE TITLE SAYS. ***\n\nThe title says the extractor 'sweeps trailing prose into the captured value'. **It also attributes ANOTHER SEAT'S VERDICT, FROM ANOTHER PANEL, TO A SEAT THAT HAS NOT FINISHED THINKING.**\n\nMEASURED on `panel_20260826_222557` (the `#466` pass 2): `seat_verdicts.py` printed\n`codex  909882  95%  SHIP-WITH  answered`. Codex had issued no verdict line at all -- `grep -n '^VERDICT' codex.txt` returns NOTHING -- and it was **still running**, its file growing from 909,882 to 965,763 bytes during the check with its tail mid-grep.\n\nThe matched string is at `codex.txt:924`, and it is not codex's text. It is a line codex GREPPED OUT OF `regress/pass2_ledger.tsv`: a receipt from 2026-08-18 recording a DIFFERENT panel's `SHIP-` verdict. **The extractor read a file-search result as the seat's own conclusion.**\n\n*** WHY THIS IS THE SEAT-FAILURE-COUNTED-AS-AGREEMENT SHAPE IN ITS PUREST FORM, arriving through the tool built to prevent it: *** the round record would have carried 'codex: SHIP-WITH' -- a FABRICATED RATIFICATION from a seat that had not answered. The panel would have been reported complete with its most substantial seat still thinking, and nobody reading the record could tell.\n\n**AND IT IS SPECIFIC TO THE FILE-READING SEATS, which is what makes it dangerous.** A packet-fed seat cannot grep, so its output contains only its own words. Codex, agy and grok stream their SEARCH RESULTS into the same file as their answer -- so the richer the seat's investigation, the more other people's verdicts it quotes, and the more likely a position-blind matcher latches onto one.\n\n**THE DISCRIMINATOR THE EXTRACTOR ALREADY HAS AND DID NOT APPLY HERE: POSITION.** `seat_verdicts.py` uses a position check to catch the kimi wall-in-a-huge-answer class. A verdict is a seat's CONCLUSION, so it belongs at the END of its output -- `:924` of a file that is now past 965 KB is nowhere near it. **A match in the first 1% of a still-growing file is a quotation, not a conclusion.**\n\nSECOND SIGNAL, free and independent: **a file that is still GROWING has not concluded.** The extractor read a snapshot of a live seat. Comparing size across two reads costs nothing and would have refused this outright.\n\nNOT FIXED IN-ROUND: this is a checker change and owes negative controls in both directions -- a real trailing verdict must still be found, and a quoted one must not be. Filed with the instance, which is what the row was missing.\n\n"
     },
     {
       "id": "verdictstate",
@@ -19393,7 +19393,7 @@
       },
       "hold": "",
       "witness": "measured -- _scratchpad/panel_20260825_024101_audit/consistency_deepseek-v4-pro_cloud.txt",
-      "state_note": "MEASURED 2026-08-25 on the #463 consistency audit. `deepseek-v4-pro:cloud` returned **`\"response\": \"\"` -- completely empty -- inside a 265,845-byte JSON object whose `thinking` field is 260,678 bytes, with `done_reason: 'length'` and `eval_count: 65536`. It spent its ENTIRE output budget on hidden reasoning and never emitted an answer. *** IT IS DISTINCT FROM ALL FOUR RECORDED CLASSES. *** Not a WALL (no quota text, no 403/429). Not the ECHO-ONLY shape (it echoes nothing). Not a REFUSED CONNECTION (the daemon served it fine, for 267 seconds). Not the shared-transport outage (the other three cloud seats answered normally in the same firing). *** AND IT DEFEATS EVERY SIZE-BASED CHECK IN THE WORST DIRECTION: at 265 KB it is the LARGEST file in its panel and would be scored the best answer. *** The carrier already warns 'verify the CONTENT, never the size; the failure mode is a big file' -- this is that lesson in a new variant, where the bulk is not an echoed brief but the model's own reasoning. **It was diagnosable only by accident**: `panel_ollama.py` does `body = resp.get('response') or json.dumps(resp)`, so an empty response falls back to dumping the raw JSON. Had it written the empty string, the file would have been 0 bytes and indistinguishable from a transport failure. That fallback should be made deliberate, and documented as the thing that preserves the diagnosis. THE FIX, and it is cheap: the seat check must parse any Ollama output that starts with `{`, and report SEAT DOWN when `response` is empty -- naming `done_reason` and `eval_count`, because `length` says 'budget exhausted' while `stop` would say something else entirely. A working checker exists (used to produce this row) and needs wiring into `panel.sh` and `seat_verdicts.py`. CONTRIBUTING CAUSE worth recording separately: the brief was the ENTIRE 18 KB round-block draft. The carrier's rule is to INLINE every fact for packet-fed seats, and that rule is sound -- but inlining an 18 KB artifact and asking for exhaustive cross-checking gives a thinking model enough to reason itself past its own budget. **Inline the facts; do not inline an entire document and ask for an audit of it.**  *** AND A SIXTH CLASS, MEASURED THE SAME SESSION on the matrix-review panel: THE SEAT'S OWN TRANSPORT DIED MID-ANSWER. *** `agy` returned 293 bytes reading `I have launched the topic cluster analysis script ... Waiting for background script completion.` followed by `Error: the connection to the agent was interrupted before the response finished: subscriber fell behind updates, stalled for 6s`. It is none of the five: no quota text (not a wall), it connected and worked for minutes (not a refused connection), it echoed nothing, it emitted a real partial answer rather than an empty response inside vast thinking, and the other seats on the same panel were unaffected (not the shared-Agent-tool outage). **The distinguishing shape: the seat DELEGATED to a background script and its own streaming connection dropped while it waited.** Two consequences. (1) The keyword set every seat check in this project uses -- quota / rate limit / balance / 403 / 429 / refused -- does NOT contain `interrupted`, `stalled`, or `subscriber fell behind`, so this scored as `answered`. Widened. (2) **It is worth RE-FIRING, unlike a wall**: a wall is the provider refusing and you wait it out; this is a dropped connection and the work simply did not survive. The re-fire rule the carrier already states for walled seats applies here with better odds.  *** THE CONTRIBUTING CAUSE THIS ROW FILED WAS MEASURED FALSE THE SAME SESSION. *** It said the trigger was inlining an entire 18 KB document and asking for an exhaustive audit of it, and prescribed 'inline the facts; do not inline a document'. **A SECOND burn then happened on a brief a fraction of that size** -- 11 ledger rows, truncated narratives, ~10 KB -- with the identical signature: `response: \"\"`, `done_reason: 'length'`, `eval_count: 65536`, and 275,778 bytes of `thinking`. Larger thinking than the first burn, on a much smaller brief. **So SIZE IS NOT THE CAUSE**, and the prescription derived from it was wrong. What the two share is the TASK SHAPE, not the input size: both asked for an EXHAUSTIVE PER-ITEM AUDIT with several independent sub-questions per item (four categories over a document; four questions over each of eleven rows). The seat answered a 6.5 KB pass-2 REVIEW brief normally in the same session -- one subject, one set of questions. **The working hypothesis is now COMBINATORIAL: N items x M orthogonal questions, where the seat appears to explore the cross-product before emitting anything.** That is a hypothesis, not a measurement, and it is recorded as such -- the honest state is that the cause is NOT ESTABLISHED and the first stated cause is REFUTED. OPERATIONAL CONSEQUENCE, which does not depend on the cause: **deepseek is unreliable for per-item audit tasks and reliable for single-subject review**, so route it accordingly rather than by brief size. And the detection rule is unchanged and now doubly warranted: parse the JSON, report SEAT DOWN on an empty `response`, and never score by file size -- both burns produced the LARGEST file in their panel.  *** AND THE TEST THAT LOOKED LIKE A CONFIRMATION IS CONFOUNDED -- CORRECTED IMMEDIATELY RATHER THAN LEFT STANDING. *** deepseek ANSWERED a #463 research-stage brief cleanly (1,724 bytes, a real dissent) after burning on two audits, and that was momentarily reported as confirming the task-shape hypothesis over the size hypothesis. **It confirms nothing.** The brief was 3,614 bytes -- SMALLER than both that burned it (19,395 and 10,408) -- so it varies BOTH suspected causes at once and cannot discriminate between them. The honest state of the evidence: burns at 19,395 (per-item audit) and 10,408 (per-item audit); clean answers at 8,583 (single-subject review) and 3,614 (single-subject research). **Size and shape are perfectly correlated across all four data points**, so nothing here separates them. A discriminating test exists and is cheap -- a LARGE single-subject brief, or a SMALL per-item one -- and until someone runs it the cause remains NOT ESTABLISHED. *Recorded because the first stated cause was already refuted once; a second unearned cause would be the same defect with better manners.*"
+      "state_note": "*** TWO MORE INSTANCES 2026-08-26, BACK TO BACK, AND THEY CARRY A CORRELATION THIS ROW DID NOT HAVE: THE BURN TRACKS BRIEF COMPLEXITY, NOT THE SEAT. ***\n\n    panel_20260826_090604    4,032 b   normal answer\n    panel_20260826_190055    2,845 b   normal answer\n    panel_20260826_222557  142,525 b   BURNED  done_reason=length  eval_count=65536\n    panel_20260826_230207  222,981 b   BURNED  done_reason=length  eval_count=65536\n\n**The two burns are on the day's two most COMPLEX briefs** -- one asking it to verify band arithmetic across three separate claims, one asking five questions spanning two rounds. The two clean runs were simpler briefs to the same seat, same transport, same day.\n\n*** THAT REFRAMES THE CLASS. *** The row recorded it as a SEAT property -- something to detect and work around. Today's evidence says it is at least partly a BRIEF property, which is actionable in a way a seat outage is not: **I can shorten the brief.** That matches the carrier's oldest lesson about packet-fed seats -- their behaviour is governed by the brief, and this project once spent DAYS misdiagnosing a brief defect as a seat outage.\n\n**AND THE SECOND BURN SHOWS WHAT IS BEING LOST, which the first instance could not.** Its output ends mid-sentence: *'So verdict likely NOT RATIFY due to #467. But maybe the question is from a panel'* -- **it had REACHED a verdict and was second-guessing itself when the budget ran out.** That is a substantive dissent destroyed by a token limit, not a seat with nothing to say. On that same panel the other dissenting seat found a LIVE DEFECT, so a lost dissent here is not hypothetical cost.\n\n**DETECTION IS ALREADY SOLVED AND SHOULD NOT BE RE-ARGUED:** `seat_verdicts.py` reports these as `SEAT DOWN -- spent its whole budget on hidden reasoning (length, N tokens)`, by reading `done_reason` rather than size. Both of today's burns were caught and named correctly.\n\n**WHAT IS STILL OWED is the MITIGATION, and it is now cheap to state:** keep a packet-fed brief to ONE question with the facts inlined, rather than a multi-part audit. Splitting a complex brief across two firings costs one extra invocation and preserves the answer.\n\nMEASURED 2026-08-25 on the #463 consistency audit. `deepseek-v4-pro:cloud` returned **`\"response\": \"\"` -- completely empty -- inside a 265,845-byte JSON object whose `thinking` field is 260,678 bytes, with `done_reason: 'length'` and `eval_count: 65536`. It spent its ENTIRE output budget on hidden reasoning and never emitted an answer. *** IT IS DISTINCT FROM ALL FOUR RECORDED CLASSES. *** Not a WALL (no quota text, no 403/429). Not the ECHO-ONLY shape (it echoes nothing). Not a REFUSED CONNECTION (the daemon served it fine, for 267 seconds). Not the shared-transport outage (the other three cloud seats answered normally in the same firing). *** AND IT DEFEATS EVERY SIZE-BASED CHECK IN THE WORST DIRECTION: at 265 KB it is the LARGEST file in its panel and would be scored the best answer. *** The carrier already warns 'verify the CONTENT, never the size; the failure mode is a big file' -- this is that lesson in a new variant, where the bulk is not an echoed brief but the model's own reasoning. **It was diagnosable only by accident**: `panel_ollama.py` does `body = resp.get('response') or json.dumps(resp)`, so an empty response falls back to dumping the raw JSON. Had it written the empty string, the file would have been 0 bytes and indistinguishable from a transport failure. That fallback should be made deliberate, and documented as the thing that preserves the diagnosis. THE FIX, and it is cheap: the seat check must parse any Ollama output that starts with `{`, and report SEAT DOWN when `response` is empty -- naming `done_reason` and `eval_count`, because `length` says 'budget exhausted' while `stop` would say something else entirely. A working checker exists (used to produce this row) and needs wiring into `panel.sh` and `seat_verdicts.py`. CONTRIBUTING CAUSE worth recording separately: the brief was the ENTIRE 18 KB round-block draft. The carrier's rule is to INLINE every fact for packet-fed seats, and that rule is sound -- but inlining an 18 KB artifact and asking for exhaustive cross-checking gives a thinking model enough to reason itself past its own budget. **Inline the facts; do not inline an entire document and ask for an audit of it.**  *** AND A SIXTH CLASS, MEASURED THE SAME SESSION on the matrix-review panel: THE SEAT'S OWN TRANSPORT DIED MID-ANSWER. *** `agy` returned 293 bytes reading `I have launched the topic cluster analysis script ... Waiting for background script completion.` followed by `Error: the connection to the agent was interrupted before the response finished: subscriber fell behind updates, stalled for 6s`. It is none of the five: no quota text (not a wall), it connected and worked for minutes (not a refused connection), it echoed nothing, it emitted a real partial answer rather than an empty response inside vast thinking, and the other seats on the same panel were unaffected (not the shared-Agent-tool outage). **The distinguishing shape: the seat DELEGATED to a background script and its own streaming connection dropped while it waited.** Two consequences. (1) The keyword set every seat check in this project uses -- quota / rate limit / balance / 403 / 429 / refused -- does NOT contain `interrupted`, `stalled`, or `subscriber fell behind`, so this scored as `answered`. Widened. (2) **It is worth RE-FIRING, unlike a wall**: a wall is the provider refusing and you wait it out; this is a dropped connection and the work simply did not survive. The re-fire rule the carrier already states for walled seats applies here with better odds.  *** THE CONTRIBUTING CAUSE THIS ROW FILED WAS MEASURED FALSE THE SAME SESSION. *** It said the trigger was inlining an entire 18 KB document and asking for an exhaustive audit of it, and prescribed 'inline the facts; do not inline a document'. **A SECOND burn then