#486 wdcfloodstale -- the re-aimed `overrun x2`, measured on the shipped bytes.

Every number here was produced by the main loop on cf300c0-era HEAD, and every one of
them was ALSO produced independently by a panel seat.  They agree.

  build                                     WDC_FLOOD_RESULT   overrun x2
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  shipped, BEFORE the re-aim                    25/26          0      want 1   RED
  shipped, AFTER  the re-aim                    26/26          1,1,0  want 1,1,0  ok
  Mb2 -- the producer's latch DELETED           25/26          1,145,0            FAIL

*** THE OLD ROW WAS NON-DISCRIMINATING, NOT MERELY RED, AND THAT IS THE FINDING. ***
Measured by a seat on the committed binary: the old stimulus reads OVERRUN = 0 on the
shipped latch AND 0 on a build with the latch deleted.  Clause (b) was OPEN at the ring
producer with nothing anywhere saying so.  The re-aimed row reads 1 against 145 -- it
discriminates by a factor of 145.

*** 145 WAS PREDICTED BEFORE IT WAS MEASURED, THREE TIMES INDEPENDENTLY. ***  My brief
derived it, the timing seat measured it, and I then measured it again here on my own
mutant.  Derivation, so the number carries its deriver: WDC_INBUF_SIZE = 512*513 =
262,656; nothing drains, so tail stays 0; the producer refuses at head == 262,655, so
push 262,656 is the first refusal -- the SECOND push of store 131,328.  At 2 pushes per
mov.w that gives 1 + 2*(131,400 - 131,328) = 145.

*** THE CLIFF, AND IT IS WHY THE COUNT IS NOT 131328. ***  Measured by the timing seat
across four builds:
4945:     "note": "*** MERGE WITH devexit: THEY INTERSECT AT 23 LINES AND NEITHER FILING SEAT SAW THE OTHER'S ROW. ***  Census of DEVICE_ACCESS handlers that test access LENGTH and then exit(1):\n    dev_pcc2.c 11 | dev_sgi_re.c 6 | dev_sh4.c 2 | gdrom/osiop/pvr 1 each\nA handler that cannot service a width (hlen) whose failure mode is process termination (devexit) is ONE DEFECT ON ONE LINE. Genuine merge: one fix design (the #218-#223/#438 shape), one detector shape. What survives separately is only a coverage ledger, not a round.\n\nRANKED FIRST OF THE CLUSTER on severity, not ease: exit(1) in a handler is the only outcome in the packet that destroys ALL guest state -- every process, plus whatever the disk overlay had not flushed. First round is [[sh4bcr]], witnessed at rung 3 today.\n\nThe filed witness is real and independently confirmed: ARM does emit 8-byte accesses (cpu_arm_instr_loadstore.c:101 `const int datalen = 8;` for LDRD/STRD, force-aligned at :229, handed to memory_rw at :233/:367), and DEV_IRQC_IRQ=0x0 / MASK=0x4 / UNMASK=0x8 means an 8-byte access at offset 0 spans two registers with OPPOSITE legal directions.\n\nA NUMBER TO CITE FROM THE INSTRUMENT, NOT THE PROSE: census_exits.py on HEAD gives 397 sites (214 direct + 183 macro), 119 ACCESS, 30 device files -- not the 398/120 in circulation. The delta is #438 (cf4b083). The instrument is current by construction; the prose is not.",
5396:     "note": "*** VERDICT: THE FILE IS THE WRONG UNIT -- MEASURED, NOT ARGUED. Close as documented-only with the census as the standing answer. *** **91% IS THE KILLER:** of 118 `.c` files in src/devices/, **107 carry at least one terminating site** -- so a per-file 'stops the host' flag marks 91% of the population, because CHECK_ALLOCATION at init is near-universal (144 of 183 macro sites are INIT, and INIT is 47% of the whole tally). **A metric that flags 91% of its population ranks nothing and schedules nothing.** Only **29** files carry an ACCESS site at all, so 78 of those listed are permanently zero in the only bucket that matters. Even within ACCESS the distribution defeats per-file completeness: top 5 files are 54%, median 2, and 13 of 29 have exactly one. **THE QUEUE HAS CONFUSED THESE DENOMINATORS MORE THAN THE BRIEF ADMITTED:** it traced 114 -> 170 -> 220 -> 253/271 -> 360 across the record, with devexit's own closure note carrying two different universes in one sentence AND the warning 'RECORD THE UNIVERSE OR THE NUMBER LOOKS WRONG LATER'. The brief said 'confused once'; it is at least three times. THE THREE UNITS, and only two are needed: **FILE is a SCHEDULING unit and never a coverage denominator**; **guest-reachable ACCESS SITE (90) is the ELIGIBILITY denominator** -- with the caveat that even ACCESS contains known non-defects (dev_cons.c:80's intended exit(0), dev_ram.c:132's guest-unreachable mode), so 90 is eligibility and not a defect count; and **DE-FATALISED PREDICATE is the OBLIGATION denominator**, the only one that maps onto existing machinery (accept rows and clause (d) are both per-predicate). *** THE RECORD THE ROW ASKS FOR ALREADY EXISTS, PER-SITE, IN THE SOURCE: 51 sites across 12 files already carry a `/* #NNN: was exit(1) */` tag *** -- dev_pvr.c 25, gdrom 5, osiop 5, 8253 4, pcc2 4, and seven more. That is more precise than the per-file tally requested, written at the site, keyed by round, in the fork's own tagging ethos. Combined with `census_exits.py --by-file`, which ships and the seat ran, both halves are satisfied. BOUNDED RESIDUAL: nothing answers 'of the 90 ACCESS sites, which already carry a tag?' -- a one-flag addition (`--tagged`), discharged once, not a standing policy. CONSTRAINT ON THE PREDICATE UNIT, measured and limiting: **the latch idiom is not uniform** -- at least four spellings coexist (struct `warned_*`, function-local `static int warned`, `static int w_warned/r_warned`, and others) -- so a predicate census needs the idiom normalised first, which is separate work and must not be smuggled in."
17991:    "note": "*** THE DETECTOR WAS INVERTED AND A SEAT CAUGHT IT BEFORE ANY CODE WAS WRITTEN. *** Its rows asserted the OLD fatal text must be ABSENT, but the agreed #447/#459 shape KEEPS the diagnostic and drops only exit(1) -- so DELETING THE WHOLE REGISTER ARM scored 13/13 while the intended fix scored 8/13. Re-pinned in oracle-correction order: expectations first, committed build measured RED, then the code. The discriminating shape is driving the SAME site twice in one session -- pre-fix dies, the fix says it exactly once, a deleted arm says it zero times. A survival-only oracle cannot separate the last two.\nTHREE MORE DETECTOR DEFECTS, each found only by measuring: all four 'same site twice' rows were running ONE access (pc set to the second site, step 1), so they read 1 whether the latch worked or not; a rebuild lost the width rows' LOAD variants, and restoring exit(1) on WRONG-WIDTH READ ONLY then scored a clean 14/14 while one guest MOV.B still ended the host; and the accept row was survival-only, never touching GDROM_DATA, so a mutant stripping the whole success path scored full marks.\n*** THE FOURTH DE-ESCALATED PREDICATE HAD NO ACCEPT ROW AND ITS MUTANT SCORED 22/22 WITH A BYTE-IDENTICAL VECTOR. *** Four host-fatal predicates were removed; three gained rows and `len` gained none. The mutant -- log, then decline instead of continue -- is the round's OWN CENTRAL CLAIM sitting unobservable, and it is the likeliest future edit here, since it is what the other three sites in the same arm do and what dev_wdc.c:155-167 does for its own bad-length case. Fixed in-round under L3 and DISCHARGED BY MEASURED KILL: m_widthdecline scores 22/23, red on `accept w8` alone.\nTHE FIX IS DELIBERATELY NOT UNIFORM. Three sites decline; the width guard LOGS AND CONTINUES, on dev_pvr.c:1577-1581 (#187)'s stated warrant that removing it changes no bound. Two pass-1 seats voted to EMULATE narrow access instead, and a build measured that ACTIVELY HARMFUL -- cmd_count stepping by 1 makes eleven byte writes plus one word write go 11->13, skipping the ==12 equality, reaching a site this round had assessed unreachable, and writing cmd[12] one past the array.\nCORRECTED BEFORE SHIP: 'six byte stores dispatch exactly as six word stores do' is measured TRUE FOR THE TOC PACKET 0x14 ONLY, whose odd bytes are already zero -- idata>>8 forges every odd command byte to zero. A RELOCATION, not a regression, but it means 'changes no bound' is the memory-safety argument and not the whole safety story. Also, the per-site latch decision is this round's own measured warrant and is NOT inherited from dev_wdc.c, which SHARES one flag between its two sites.\n:334 JOINED, AND THE JOIN FAILED A TEST IT COULD HAVE BEEN RECORDED AS PASSING. It and the COND_DATA_AVAIL site are one predicate family, and shipping three of four would leave a device where a declined read is safe in one state and lethal in another. But the reopening rule prices 'no new oracle', and reaching :334 needed a new opcode, a new loop shape and a new state manipulation. Recorded as DELIBERATELY OVERRIDDEN rather than met."
24874:   "note": "*** THE SCOPE QUESTION ON `#497` WAS HIDING A SEVENTH LIVE SITE, AND WITHOUT THIS ROW A ROUND NAMED `widthkillclass` WOULD HAVE FIXED 6 OF 7 AND READ AS COMPLETE. ***  Found by the fable5 seat answering that scope question; verified here by opening the file.  **THE SITE**, `grep -n 'len = %i not implemented' src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c`:  `} else { fatal(\"sgi_de: len = %i not implemented\"); exit(1); }`  -- the else of an accept set of {4, 8}, so **len in {1,2,3,5,6,7} ends the host process**.  A single guest `sb` to the DE window does it.  **IT DOES NOT JOIN `#497`, and the reason is the joining clause working rather than being dodged.**  It is a DIFFERENT REGISTERED DEVICE (`sgi_de`, its own `memory_device_register`), its accept set is {4,8} rather than {8}, and -- the part that actually settles it -- **its registers are READABLE**, so its accept rows read back through `odata` while `sgi_re`'s read back through debug lines.  That is a second fix shape and a second row species, which is exactly what the same-detector-no-new-oracle arm excludes.  *** BUT IT MUST BE NAMED IN `#497`'s PRETABLE AND ROUND RECORD. ***  A round called widthkillclass that fixes six of seven live width kills IN THE SAME FILE and does not say so writes the false-unanimity record the late-dissent rule forbids.  The sibling-parity check exists for precisely this and would have caught it only if someone asked what the NEXT device in the file does.  SHARES the struct: one malloc hands `struct sgi_re_data` to all four handlers, so whatever latch infrastructure `#497` lands is already reachable from here -- the fix is cheap once the witness exists.  *** DESIGN SETTLED 2026-08-30 BY ADJUDICATION, BEFORE EITHER ROUND STARTS, SO NEITHER BURNS ITS PANEL ARGUING SCOPE. ***  **TWO ROUNDS, and both separate from `#497` -- all THREE round-scope conjuncts fail, worked through rather than asserted.**  SAME DEVICE fails: `sgi_de` and `sgi_re` are separate `memory_device_register` registrations at `baseaddr+0x2000` and `+0x1000`.  SAME SEMANTIC CLASS fails: `sgidewidth` is the WIDTH class with a real accept set `{4,8}` a decline must PRESERVE, while `sgireunmapped` is the BADOFFSET class where nothing legal reaches the arm at all.  **SAME DETECTOR / NO NEW ORACLE fails, and this is the one that decides it:** `sgi_de`'s registers are READABLE so its rows read back through `odata` -- a real device-signature channel -- while `sgi_re`'s else arm has NO BACKING STORE and its rows can only observe the diagnostic and the guest-gets-zero channel.  *Those are different oracles in kind, and the tempting collapse -- 'both are a refused access ending the host' -- is exactly what the no-new-oracle clause exists to refuse.*  *** THE CHANNEL FINDING, AND IT IS THE MOST IMPORTANT LINE IN THIS BLOCK. ***  `#497`'s `d_returnzero` escaped 35/35 and the reason generalises to BOTH these rounds: **the `(once)` announce prints IDENTICALLY for a correct decline and for a `return 0`**, because the announce happens before the return statement.  So the DIAGNOSTIC channel does not kill it either -- and neither does host survival, since a guest bus error leaves the host alive.  **The only discriminator is what the GUEST RECEIVED.**  Every round in this family owes a SENTINEL-SEEDED READ ROW: seed the destination register, perform the declined read, and assert it reads back 0 -- a correct decline delivers zero, `return 0` faults and the register keeps its seed.  *That is the row `#497` was missing, and the measuring seat reached the same repair independently while this adjudication was being written.*  *** AND THE OBVIOUS FIX IS WRONG HERE, WHICH IS THE ROUND'S REAL DESIGN CONTENT. ***  `#497`'s decline splices to the common tail, and copying that here would ship a NEW defect.  **`DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_de)`'s tail is not inert:** it reads `if (startFlag) draw_primitive(cpu, d);` and only THEN does the read writeback -- and `startFlag = relative_addr & CRIME_DE_START` is computed for **any** access, malformed ones included.  Verified by opening the file.  Today `exit(1)` fires BEFORE the switch and before `draw_primitive`, so a malformed access never draws.  **A naive fall-through would run `draw_primitive` over STALE `de_reg` state on a bad-width store carrying bit `0x800` -- a framebuffer-corrupting side effect the kill currently prevents.**  The fix would introduce a defect the defect was preventing.  **THE CORRECT FORM IS A DELIBERATE PARTIAL TAIL:** latch-warn once, then `if (writeflag == MEM_READ) memory_writemax64(cpu, data, len, odata); return 1;` -- skipping the switch AND the draw, while still delivering the defined zero the sketch rule requires.  **Say this in the brief**, or a seat proposes the naive splice by analogy with `#497` and it looks right.  **ONE latch bit**, not two: the kill is a single direction-agnostic message (`sgi_de: len = %i not implemented`).  Contrast `sgireunmapped`, which needs two -- the count follows the MESSAGE count, not the direction count.  **The de-escalation accept clause FULLY APPLIES** and its rows are strong here because the registers are readable: `len==4` and `len==8`, read and write, still land and read back through `odata`, each reddening under the least-observable growth form.  **No `regnr` OOB risk from the decline itself** -- the register access lives inside the `len==8`/`len==4` arms, so a bad width already skips it.  *** MY SEVERITY ESCALATION WAS FALSE, AND THE ROW'S ORIGINAL FRAMING WAS RIGHT.  Refuted independently by the fable5 and codex seats on the 2026-08-31 pass-1 panel, and re-derived here before accepting it. ***  I claimed widths 3/5/6/7 are reachable through `memory_rw`'s boundary truncation, so an ordinary 8-byte `ld` near the window end would kill the host.  **It cannot.**  `cpu_mips_instr_loadstore.c` raises ADEL/ADES for any misaligned access BEFORE `memory_rw` is reached (`if (addr & (LS_SIZE - 1))`, under `#ifndef LS_1` so byte accesses are exempt), and the unaligned `lwl/lwr/ldl/ldr` family calls `memory_rw` at `addr & ~(wlen-1)` -- also aligned, also natural width.  So a width-w access arriving at the device is w-ALIGNED.  `sgi_de` is registered at `baseaddr + 0x2000` (page-aligned) for `DEV_SGI_DE_LENGTH` 0x1000 (a page multiple), so a w-aligned offset is a multiple of w and the largest is `0x1000 - w`, giving `paddr + w == 0x1000`, which is NOT `> length`.  **TRUNCATION NEVER FIRES FOR THIS DEVICE -- enumerated: ZERO truncating accesses.**  **CORRECTED ARITHMETIC:** the device sees only len in {1,2,4,8}; {4,8} are accepted; the declined set is **6144** accesses -- 4096 byte (`sb`/`lb`, any offset) and 2048 halfword (`sh`/`lh`, 2-aligned) -- of which **3072** carry startFlag.  My brief said 8201 and 4105.  *** THE ROW'S OWN \"one guest `sb`\" WAS ACCURATE ALL ALONG, AND I 'STRENGTHENED' IT INTO AN OVERCLAIM. ***  *** AND THE TWO PACKET-FED CONFIRMATIONS ARE WORTH NOTHING, EXACTLY AS THE DOCTRINE PREDICTS. ***  glm and minimax each reported re-walking 8201/4105 and reconciling -- but they were re-walking MY PREMISE, not the source, because a packet-fed seat cannot open `cpu_mips_instr_loadstore.c`.  Both file-reading seats that could open it refuted it.  **The split is exactly along the FILE-READING / PACKET-FED line, which is the whole point of classifying seats by transport.**  A brief that states a derivation directs every seat to check the arithmetic instead of the premise.  *** THE CLASS, AND THIS IS ITS THIRD INSTANCE IN ONE WEEK ON THIS FILE: `sgilinearoob` (reduced by an `offsetof` a seat compiled), `sgideoob` (WITHDRAWN -- skipped `relative_addr &= ~CRIME_DE_START`), and now this. ***  **But the refinement matters: in the first two I reasoned where I should have enumerated. Here I DID enumerate -- and enumerated the WRONG DOMAIN, walking every (width, offset) pair as though the CPU delivered them all.  Enumeration protects you only if the domain is right, and the domain is itself a premise that needs auditing.**  SEVERITY UNCHANGED IN KIND: the `else` arm is reachable, a plain `sb` at any offset ends the host, and the row stays HIGH.  Only the reachable-width claim is corrected.  *** AND MY ACCOUNT OF THE PANEL WAS WRONG IN A WAY THAT UNDER-CREDITED A SEAT.  Corrected 2026-08-31 by reading glm's answer instead of its verdict line. ***  I recorded that \"both packet-fed seats that engaged re-walked the figure and reconciled\", and that the split fell PRECISELY along the transport line.  **That is a cleaner story than the evidence supports.**  **glm -- PACKET-FED -- identified the assumption, derived the correct alternative numbers, and correctly refused to settle it:**  *\"the enumeration silently assumes misaligned guest accesses reach [the device]... widths 3/5/6/7 are guest-reachable only if this fork's MIPS core does not raise AdEL/AdES first.  **Under alignment enforcement the counts drop to 6144 declined / 3072 startFlag-set and the truncation chapter closes entirely** -- but the sb/sh doors and the draw-hazard survive untouched, so the defect stands either way.\"*  It also named my framing defect exactly: *\"'an ordinary 8-byte ld three bytes before the end' is an ld at a MISALIGNED address -- not ordinary on MIPS; its reachability is exactly the open premise.\"*  **6144 / 3072 is the number I computed only AFTER fable5 told me, and glm had it from the packet -- with both branches stated and the undecidable one flagged rather than guessed.  That is precisely what `panel_ollama.py`'s preamble asks a packet-fed seat to do, and it is the strongest packet-fed answer on record here.**  **THE HONEST TALLY:** codex, fable5 and grok (all FILE-READING) refuted the claim outright; **glm (PACKET-FED) identified it and bounded it**; minimax and deepseek (packet-fed) reconciled the wrong figure without flagging alignment.  *The doctrine's point survives and is even strengthened -- a packet-fed seat cannot SETTLE a repo fact -- but \"score a packet-fed agreement as zero\" is too blunt: glm did not agree, it CONDITIONED.  A conditioned answer from a seat that cannot check is worth more than an unconditioned one from a seat that can.*  *** THE 25-ROW KILL TABLE, NINE BUILDS, AND THE OBVIOUS FIX IS NOT THE RIGHT ONE. ***  `mR` (latched decline + `goto` to the common exit) **25/25**.  `mB` (early `return 1`) also 25/25 but not recommended -- it duplicates nothing today only because the tail's remaining step is a no-op, and diverges silently the day the tail grows.  **`fix` -- the shape the brief's own logic recommends, decline + `startFlag = 0` + fall through -- scores 24/25 and the red row is the point:** a declined 1-byte write still reaches the switch, which prints `[ sgi_de: TODO: non-zero CRIME_DE_SCISSOR: ... ]` about **a write that never happened**.  Product code announcing a TODO for a discarded `idata` -- the same wrong-record-in-a-diagnostic class the ACCEPT member was discovered through.  **`goto <label>` to a common exit is HOUSE IDIOM, not an invention** -- 10 device files use it, including `dev_sgi_mec.c` in this same SGI family.  The grep is the answer to \"is this too clever\".  **ONE latch bit, and it must be a NEW one.**  Per-message-site gives one bit here (one guard, one text).  But `mC` -- reusing #497's `SGIRE_W_TLBA_LEN` -- scores 23/25: the four handlers share one malloc'd struct, so any odd-width access to `sgi_re`'s TLB_A window silences `sgi_de`'s decline entirely.  `1<<6` is free.  **ACCEPT ROWS AT `+0x7fc` AND `+0x7f8`, NOT THE NOMINAL LAST SLOTS.**  `+0xffc`/`+0xff8` cannot carry an accept row because they trip `sgideprimop` -- the last legal slots WITH startFlag CLEAR are the real anchors.  *That is a fact about the device, not a convenience, and it belongs in the row comments.*  Plus a FIFTH row `tail_acc`: an accepted width at `+0xfd0` must STILL reach `draw_primitive`, and it is the only row that kills a mutant clearing startFlag unconditionally -- without it, disabling all drawing sweeps clean.  **THE SMALLEST BREAKING EDIT IS BLIND TO A LOWER-HALF-ONLY TABLE.**  Delete the `goto` and keep the latch: 19/25 against this table, but **25/25 against any table whose rows all sit below 0x800**, because no such offset can reach `draw_primitive`.  *That is the anchor rule one level up -- rows that never vary the START bit cannot see a repair that only matters when it is set.*  *THE GOTO-IDIOM COUNT, DERIVED RATHER THAN QUOTED, because the seat's figure and my first grep disagreed: `grep -rl '[^a-z_]goto ' src/devices/*.c` gives **10 files**, matching the seat.  My first attempt gave 6 because it pinned three specific label names (`do_return`, `ret`, `out`) and `dev_sgi_mec.c` uses `goto skip`.  **The figure that actually bears on this fix is narrower and better: 8 device files use `goto` INSIDE a `DEVICE_ACCESS` body** -- dc7085, i80321, le, mk48txx, ne2000, pvr, vr41xx, wdc -- walked by scanning each handler's braces rather than the whole file.  Either way the idiom is in-tree and the question \"is a goto too clever here\" is answered by the tree.*  *** #498 SHIPPED.  Fix + 25-row detector, and the detector's FIRST DRAFT SHIPPED A CLAIM IT COULD NOT BACK. ***  I wrote 22 rows and a contract naming `tail_acc` as *the only row that catches a repair hoisted to every access* -- **while `tail_acc` was not in the file.**  Three of the seat's measured rows were missing (`hi_kill`, `tail_acc`, `tail_acc_x`), and without them the 22/25 mutant that silently DISABLES ALL DRAWING would have swept clean.  That is the CLAIM -> ROW pre-table check failing inside a detector's own header, caught by diffing my row set against the measured one rather than by trusting my own contract.  Now 25/25, matching the seat's set exactly; the gate's row list and both denominators repinned 22 -> 25.  *The 22-row artifact was DELETED rather than kept beside the 25-row one: it measured an incomplete instrument, and a preserved log whose instrument was wrong is not evidence, it is a trap for whoever cites it.*  **`tail_acc_x` pins a host DEATH on purpose** -- the accepted-width access reaches `draw_primitive` and dies there, which is `sgideprimop`.  When that row is fixed this one goes RED BY SUCCEEDING, so it inherits the oracle-correction ordering: re-pin first, measure the committed build red, then edit.  *** PASS 2 FOUND A REAL DETECTOR HOLE, TWO SEATS INDEPENDENTLY, AND IT IS NOW CLOSED. ***  `len == 2` was not pinned as a live decline anywhere.  The witness killed on sb, sh, lbu AND lhu; the detector's survival and answer rows used sb and lbu ONLY, and `sh` appeared solely as the SECOND access in `two_widths_*`, whose oracle is *\"len=2 is never announced\"*.  **That row stays GREEN whether len==2 is ACCEPTED, still KILLED with the old text, or correctly DECLINED -- three machines behind one green row -- and the ONE latch bit is why: after `sb`, a later `sh` is SUPPOSED to be silent, so silence proves nothing.**  fable5 predicted the escaping edit as `else if (len == 4 || len == 2)` at a full 25/25 and walked it row by row rather than building it during a live panel; grok reached the same hole from the witness side.  **Four rows added (`lo_kill2`, `lo_decl2`, `lo_alive2`, `lo_sent2`), each in its OWN session so the fresh latch makes the len=2 announcement observable at all.  Detector 25 -> 29, all green, gate repinned.**  *** AND THE PROCESS BREACH, RECORDED RATHER THAN BACK-WRITTEN: #498 IS THE FIRST POST-MANDATE KILL-TABLE ROUND WITH NO `PRETABLE.out`. ***  Five sibling rounds carry one (gdromcondlies, gdromexitsix, wdcaddtoinbufbound, wdcaltoffset, widthkillclass -- this round's own parent).  **Both of its costs are visible in this round**: the CLAIM -> ROW check is exactly what polices *a contract naming `tail_acc` while `tail_acc` is not in the file*, which I caught late and by diffing; and the de-escalation enumeration -- every predicate that gated death, accept side AND refusal side per reachable width -- is where the len==2 hole would have surfaced at WRITING time.  *Back-writing the block now would violate its own mtime-order rule, so the honest record is \"skipped, costs enumerated\".*  **AND ONE DISPOSITION WAS WRONG: deleting the 22-row artifact.**  The house remedy for a misleading record is FREEZE-WITH-BANNER, not destruction -- REVIEW_FINDINGS.md's scope banner and the seat table \"kept for its REASONING only\" are the precedents.  The deletion was pre-commit and is therefore unrecoverable, and it strips the evidence from three durable prose claims that cite it.  A `WRONG-INSTRUMENT` banner would have cost three lines.  *** PASS 2's MEASURING SEAT FOUND TWO MORE 25/25 ESCAPES THAT SURVIVED THE 29-ROW VERSION, BOTH FLOOR BREACHES, BOTH ONE KEYSTROKE.  L2 -- repaired in the follow-up commit. ***  **`bracegoto`: move `goto de_declined;` INSIDE the latch braces.**  Only the FIRST decline then jumps; every later one falls through to the register switch AND `draw_primitive()` -- measured `exit1`, *the exact host death this round exists to remove*.  It escaped because **every row in the detector exercised the FIRST decline of a fresh session**; `lo_latch` came closest with two declines but chose two lower-half offsets, both landing on the switch's silent `default:`.  **`bit5collide`: `1 << 6` -> `1 << 5`, one character**, colliding with `SGIRE_W_LINB_LEN` and silencing the decline outright.  `sharedlatch` tripped only TLB_A, making it a **POINT TEST AGAINST A SIX-MEMBER SET** -- `grep -c 'd->warned |=' ` gives 7 sites, and five of the six partners escaped.  **Both repairs are BOUNDED -- no new rows and no new sessions**, which is why they are a repair rather than the `#392` chain: the upper-half sessions gain one lower-half decline in front, and `sharedlatch` trips all six siblings.  29/29 after.  **AND A ROW OF MINE WAS VACUOUS BY CONSTRUCTION.**  I moved `hi_kill`'s oracle off the measured form onto `len == 4` -- an ACCEPTED width on every build in the story -- so the channel could not vary.  Measured identical on the pre-fix build, on all eight of this round's mutants, post-fix, and on all five of the seat's; even a verbatim reinstatement left it green.  Re-pinned onto the measured len=1 upper-half form.  *** THE PRETABLE SKIP IS NO LONGER AN ABSTRACT PROCESS POINT: `#490`'s OWN PRETABLE BLOCK PRESCRIBED \"a latch-bit collision and a brace slip\", AND THOSE ARE MY TWO ESCAPES BY NAME. ***  Its GUARD-FIELD check asks *who else writes this field, and can they set my bit?* -- seven sites here.  Skipping the block cost exactly the coverage it exists to find, and that is measured rather than argued.  **FLOOR (a) AND (b) DO HOLD**, measured because no artifact showed it: a verbatim reinstatement of the pre-fix arm scores **14/25**, eleven rows red.  *** AND I BLINDED THREE SEATS: THE PASS-2 BRIEF TRUNCATED THE DIFF AT 7000 CHARACTERS AND THE 1545 BYTES CUT WERE THE ENTIRE FIX -- the latch, the message, the goto and the label. ***  The brief was 13.5 KB against a 30 KB cap, so the truncation bought nothing; the cap was arbitrary and I never checked what it removed.  **Two seats refused to answer around it and named the exact cut point**, which is the honest packet-fed response.  All three packet-fed seats were RE-FIRED against the complete diff rather than letting the record show six answers where three were blind.  *** AND THE `mC` 23/25 FIGURE IS CONFOUNDED -- codex checked the generator rather than the score. ***  It combined the shared latch bit with the `startFlag = 0` fallthrough, so its two reds (`dec_switch`, `sharedlatch`) come from TWO edits.  Pure bit-0 reuse against `mR` would lose only the latch row.  **The conclusion is unchanged and the citation is wrong**: the own-bit requirement is established by `bit5collide` -- a genuine one-character edit escaping all 25 rows -- not by 23/25.  *A score cited for a property its mutant did not isolate is the frozen-number class wearing a different hat: the number is real, the thing it is offered as evidence for is not what was measured.*",
28150:   "note": "*** THREE SEPARATE ROUNDS INDEPENDENTLY REACHED FOR THE SAME IDEA AND ALL THREE USED PROCESS-GLOBAL STATE.  Found 2026-08-31 by re-deriving a census a seat had made with a narrower pattern. ***  **THE DERIVER, because two separate counts of this class have already been short:**  `grep -rn 'static int w = 0;|static int first = 1;|static char .*_used\\[' src/devices/*.c`  ->  **10 sites across 3 files** on 2026-08-31:  `dev_jazz.c` 2 (from `#179`), `dev_pcc2.c` 4 (from `#119`), `dev_sgi_re.c` 4 (upstream `rop_used`).  **THE DEFECT.**  A `static` inside a function is process-global, not per-instance.  Every one of these is a *warn-once* latch on a GUEST-DRIVABLE diagnostic, so **one guest access to one device instance permanently silences that diagnostic for every other instance of the same device in the machine** -- and for every later machine in the same process.  The house form is a per-instance bit in the device's own struct (`d->warned` plus `(once)` in the message text), which `#337`, `#460`, `#461`, `#497` and `#498` all use.  **THE INTENT IS RIGHT EVERYWHERE AND ONLY THE SCOPE IS WRONG.**  `#179`'s comment says *\"guest-reachable; ignore the write instead of fatal()-flooding host stdout\"*; `#119`'s says *\"was exit(1) -> warn-once + continue\"*.  Both are correct de-fatalisations that this project would make again.  **This row is about SCOPE, not disposition, and must not be read as reopening either round.**  *** HOW IT WAS FOUND IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING: TWO CENSUSES OF THIS CLASS WERE BOTH SHORT, IN OPPOSITE WAYS. ***  A measuring seat censused it while answering an unrelated question and reported *\"4 lines, ALL in this file, ALL inside draw_primitive(), the tree's only such sites\"* -- true of its pattern (`static int first`/`_used[`) and false of the class, because `#119` and `#179` spell their latch `static int w = 0`.  Then a packet-fed seat on a DIFFERENT row challenged an `exit(` regex of mine as too narrow; the count it challenged did not move, **but chasing the four extra hits surfaced `#119`'s comments and hence the second file**.  *Neither seat was looking for this class.  A pattern-based census is only ever a census of its pattern, and the way out was two independent narrow greps disagreeing.*  **SCOPE NOTE for whoever takes this:** `dev_jazz.c` is one of the six paths in `tools/est_divergence.txt`, so a change there is SEC-only and does not owe an `est/` counterpart -- unlike the other two files.  **CONTROL a fix owes:** a per-instance latch must be shown to announce ONCE PER INSTANCE and not once per process -- i.e. a machine with two instances of the device, expecting two messages where the current code emits one.  That is the measurement that distinguishes the repair from a no-op rename.  *** THIS ROW'S OWN COUNT WAS WRONG WHEN FILED -- 23 SITES ACROSS 7 FILES, NOT 10 ACROSS 3.  THAT IS THE THIRD MISCOUNT OF THIS CLASS, AND THIS ONE IS MINE, USING THE DERIVER I WROTE WHILE TELLING OTHERS NOT TO TRANSCRIBE NUMBERS. ***  **The wider deriver:** `grep -rnE 'static int (w|warned|first) *= *[01];|static char [a-z_]+\\[' src/devices/*.c`  ->  **23 sites / 7 files**: `dev_osiop.c` **8**, `dev_pcc2.c` 5, `dev_sgi_re.c` 4, `dev_jazz.c` 2, `dev_pvr.c` 2, `dev_disk.c` 1, `dev_ps2_gs.c` 1.  **My deriver matched `static int w = 0` and `static int first = 1` and missed `static int warned = 0`, which is the DOMINANT spelling** -- it accounts for 16 of the 23.  `dev_osiop.c` alone has more sites than my entire filed count, and I never looked at that file.  *** THE LESSON IS ABOUT DERIVERS, NOT ABOUT THIS CLASS.  I filed this row saying \"record the deriver rather than the number, because this class has already been miscounted twice\" -- and the deriver I recorded was itself too narrow.  A deriver is only a census of its own pattern, exactly like the number it replaces; what it buys is that the NEXT reader can widen it, which is what happened here. ***  *Found because a `pcc2` seat's answer mentioned a `static int warned` site my census did not contain, and I re-ran the grep instead of assuming the seat meant one I already had.*  **`dev_osiop.c` should be looked at first by whoever takes this row**: 8 sites, several commented `#118/#119: was exit(1)`, so it is the same de-fatalisation campaign and the largest single population."
28159:   "note": "*** FILED AS ONE SITE IN dev_lca.c; CENSUSED THE SAME DAY AND IT IS A CLASS.  Widened low -> med. ***\n\nTHE DERIVERS -- run them, do not trust these numbers, and note they answer DIFFERENT questions:\n\n    # (1) the whole cast population -- mostly HARMLESS and NOT the defect\n    grep -rc '(int)idata\\|(int) idata' src/devices/*.c | grep -v ':0$'\n\n    # (2) the sharp sub-class: a cast inside a diagnostic at a site that then EXITS\n    #     (a python window scan; see the round record for the exact form)\n\nOn 2026-08-31 (1) gave 309 casts across 70 device files and (2) gave 45 host-kill sites, about twenty of which carry an explicit full-width guard on idata.\n\n*** MOST OF THE 309 ARE NOT DEFECTS, AND SAYING SO IS THE POINT -- an 8- or 16-bit register loses nothing through (int). ***  The defect needs BOTH halves:\n  * a guard that tests the FULL 64-bit value -- `idata & ~MASK' or `idata != CONST'; and\n  * a diagnostic that prints the TRUNCATED one.\nThen a guest write whose only distinguishing bits are above bit 31 dies, while the message reports a value that would have been accepted.\n\n*** THE SHARPEST INSTANCE, VERIFIED BY COMPUTATION AND BY READING THE ARM: dev_pvr.c's `if (writeflag == MEM_WRITE && idata != 0x0cff0000)' paired with `(int) idata'. ***  A guest writing 0x1_0cff0000 fails the 64-bit compare and the host dies -- and the message prints 0x0cff0000, **EXACTLY THE VALUE THE CODE SAYS IS ACCEPTABLE**.  So the diagnostic tells a maintainer the guest wrote the correct value and died for it, which sends the investigation in precisely the wrong direction.  dev_lca.c's `idata & ~1' is the same shape, milder: it prints 0x0 for a value whose low half is genuinely zero.\n\nSEVERITY REASONING, stated rather than assumed: none of this changes WHETHER the host dies -- the guards are unchanged and the kills are the kills.  It changes only what the log says about WHY, at the one moment the log is all that survives.  That is why it is med and not high, and why it is a RECORDS-CORRECTNESS defect in product code rather than a control-flow one.\n\nNOT A ONE-LINE SWEEP.  Each site needs its own read: the fix is the format specifier plus a (long long) cast, but which sites are genuinely reachable with a >32-bit datum depends on the register width and the guest paths that reach it, and a blanket edit to 45 diagnostics is exactly the unlicensed addition this project refuses.  The honest first round is the ~20 sites whose guard is demonstrably full-width, ordered by whether a guest can deliver a >32-bit write at all.\n\nFound while checking whether the dev_lca.c site was a one-off, as parallel work during a gate run.",

